[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104b5f45-a5a5-53b8-81f3-1afa7e730667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278738} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1d9c0f-74a7-5840-a8f1-946c355e9f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279310} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1151a9-1581-5ce8-97ab-8d91464f04d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280107} {:answer/id #uuid "de04ac09-2b08-5de9-947d-c33d64af0400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280549} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ed382-97b6-5633-976b-856f4f15d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279310} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f8be0c-a6d9-58ec-b1bd-534c48dbccb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cf0a2c6-6793-5c24-82b1-b8c9fc0536bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163225" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1ef5062d5bc045c1e1f7fa58c513315852f74aa15539c001662b0055583f01b.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=2$ and the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10x.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(32-1)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =31\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17226919-ce93-5049-994f-31d7cd5c10fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278798} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3b17cc-de83-57bb-914f-013137289a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586} {:answer/id #uuid "78cff65f-0d69-5e60-9e1c-47253821dc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279669} {:answer/id #uuid "8d83c82c-bb2f-532f-ab7d-a58f6e2cc4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279853} {:answer/id #uuid "e875a915-69ff-5a94-9c30-f7fd03b5fb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279586} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf0fe541-fc86-58e2-a9d5-645ba6b20816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2726afac-54d3-5734-a6e0-8fa5ce77d367" :question/math-academy-id "q-163228" :question/problem "![](images/04/044395de3b30755d0c83d489709c38420827f6984af740bc56a3dc5bfaa33794.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=2$ and $r=3$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-4)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24eeb6dc-6a62-5d90-a207-0acfc7258433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278919} {:answer/id #uuid "349f0582-d339-5e4d-9545-b31c93cda4da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279045} {:answer/id #uuid "538264a8-2746-5c22-950d-ad36b0c19129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279351} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b8065a-0247-55f8-99df-fad5f0147068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280342} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ebd0da-d30a-58a4-863a-0db44212f7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279351} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2db863-9898-51d6-ba39-8b881e966e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b6b312d9-6efa-541f-ad5d-be93b575260c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163230" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad7c939aded3f5e5e1e2f3b5e5743609e331625479c25f6a55659be730ed1cf5.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt[5]{2}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=15y.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt[5]{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt[5]{2}}\\cdot (-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "535f04ee-70e9-5f01-b34b-53660081decb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279349} {:answer/id #uuid "94c9f393-f7e3-5725-b98c-409c0939e18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279914} {:answer/id #uuid "be2b3b90-dc97-502a-b811-4f9ef9fa7d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280264} {:answer/id #uuid "cb391e7e-7c21-53a9-b284-5d3daf858d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280371} {:answer/id #uuid "ed47a95d-b3d6-55c8-bf2e-23e1f2592039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280371} :answer-field/id #uuid "14fa30c2-c058-5d5d-bcd3-afbb3922e2e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec31a85b-303d-5111-a311-f0cb89d6a30f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163242" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1a19303937f1d515ca820516e10a73974b00f83884b05abb58dee49c81207f4.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the upper half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=8\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{8\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =4M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1423879f-45e2-5f3a-bf42-98ceec2f0d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278771} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac0e119-d0b2-54a1-9a0c-77fb3087de25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279430} {:answer/id #uuid "b756c4f9-63a7-586c-a61f-d37beaab985f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280202} {:answer/id #uuid "dadffaa0-9dbd-5f20-98a2-b1ad72baf426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280525} {:answer/id #uuid "db6569e4-0abd-57d4-8672-9919fc65d2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278771} :answer-field/id #uuid "69710f6b-ffa0-54e7-80a3-cc71b4b02c60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e73324e-535c-5fbc-b61e-13f64e0e8c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163243" :question/problem "![](images/04/044386c438976bf17e2f51241659f167f7eedfb6509d0a60f3f70ba91457e71f.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first, second and third quadrants bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27060319-319a-5c93-87a4-021b464e69b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278938} {:answer/id #uuid "96cddbff-aa96-5c15-b451-85bc3b5899ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279926} {:answer/id #uuid "d6297845-0a0a-51e3-9056-3d94de569441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280482} {:answer/id #uuid "d932caf8-3377-5328-b82d-2c72ffe1d077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280511} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6871d0-cf0d-5786-9a5c-fd24e0463ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280794} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bf3c23-4651-531a-8ea1-05778a87081e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4da84d34-65b6-5264-9689-a8f568b0485b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163278" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f84a8fcd4ed668a6b94d5b084ade49dc8fc027feff4baea862e2feaf68221534.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(-1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{[-\\frac{{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}({cos}^{5}⁡\\pi -{cos}^{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{8}{5\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02228a55-f0cd-5d20-a57e-0a6be96e6db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278600} {:answer/id #uuid "20802c9f-44b7-54c4-98fc-e21f7cad1d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278891} {:answer/id #uuid "2f618d22-048b-597a-97ca-eb05ca9ee64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279004} {:answer/id #uuid "34a613ef-9f35-5a47-9476-0c26e7c87ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279046} {:answer/id #uuid "d964673e-ff68-55cd-ae5e-82c200ba663a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280512} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b55bdf5-772a-5e7e-b2a0-07c63cb6c150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "128ce1a7-9b5d-51fb-9a3d-78ee6a5214c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163279" :question/problem "![](images/71/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ on the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bc3abb-b97c-51e3-bac4-38ebd3f9974a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279205} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9a89bf-1165-5025-aad4-e1717fcca8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49M}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279716} {:answer/id #uuid "835796e1-86ce-58fc-9beb-727a52f0b56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21M}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279767} {:answer/id #uuid "8a2a138f-9f8d-5c08-9c3b-a5703b1f5cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{93M}{70}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279826} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6cc418-6bd6-5e7a-9c5b-a65d6a49bf4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{51M}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279826} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc71749-05e0-5682-9015-b3a92d11c706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77c3fd9b-a588-5227-b546-d84306dcef4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163496" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f9732e0b81ba2e171a0464996e7b0d27aa47a0b32da59fa40cbba0cdbf8402a.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ bounded by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=2,$ as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}kr\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}k{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr=\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }r\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{93M}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fe1d58-696d-5801-846b-4606facb1790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278634} {:answer/id #uuid "25ee5860-9bda-526e-bbdd-a5847511bb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278929} {:answer/id #uuid "47b6317d-7da1-5aab-9ed3-b9b41c973f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279234} {:answer/id #uuid "db28513a-71bd-51e9-8dc5-90eb817afd4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280527} {:answer/id #uuid "fc36ee44-244b-55b3-878c-0cc2acd7ee6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280527} :answer-field/id #uuid "af93d820-8c9e-5b5d-981c-2e952953e275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b5e602d-da1f-5649-9bd5-641c23ed74ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-163594" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{x}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9\\pi }M{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec9ea01-bfeb-5dfa-bb0d-e8dda89e8cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281819} {:answer/id #uuid "2f167cc3-d0d4-5732-aea7-9737587d5446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282107} {:answer/id #uuid "618693f7-594d-59ce-a534-76a8e9e2b379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡3+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282519} {:answer/id #uuid "888a6635-e022-5c1a-a6c7-78955c8928cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡3+5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282867} {:answer/id #uuid "8957ae1d-f55e-532c-822e-e38dcfa2b32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282874} {:answer/id #uuid "a46851a8-a830-5300-ba0a-c94605be1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283122} {:answer/id #uuid "a598ce3d-2796-5960-afd3-75d843fcccbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} {:answer/id #uuid "ab50b4da-933b-580c-90e3-d57fe918ca3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283174} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c751ad-dde6-5f48-96a4-ca006c0a37ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283278} {:answer/id #uuid "e0621d5d-1546-515b-b160-26c36601cee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283647} {:answer/id #uuid "eac1d727-6b15-5ede-a524-9f9b5cca59df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283741} {:answer/id #uuid "f038538b-7314-51bc-a23b-a2dc236cb725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283787} {:answer/id #uuid "f848449c-f299-591e-af24-cf140841f848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "405060cf-b243-58c6-a1be-efface6f737b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282243} {:answer/id #uuid "51a33775-5e8e-5511-a7fa-4cf8b0f87580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282390} {:answer/id #uuid "636cf8dc-4952-5687-b207-e110dd35d904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282545} {:answer/id #uuid "baf7447a-3cc1-5d56-8d01-0e389cc82d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283303} {:answer/id #uuid "e1dea525-7cce-5f64-bf7d-d97fb6b394f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283657} :answer-field/id #uuid "1673793e-a953-562c-871a-ec238b6164bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ed19a02-b52e-57ba-89d7-67df43752d50" :question/math-academy-id "q-163641" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+6x-4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4{y}^{3}-4x+8y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=6x+6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=12{y}^{2}+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6x+6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12{y}^{2}+8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=12\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=80.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ccfc6-c980-5f87-98b3-cb27c26de86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281784} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc546d0-b0b6-5368-af23-5ec0c2c96352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281949} {:answer/id #uuid "48389a8d-c104-542e-8686-3b973c39c4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282301} {:answer/id #uuid "7f03e21b-fba2-5b1e-bbaa-ba00d1112786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282783} {:answer/id #uuid "c372c923-e6e7-5ef4-8346-000c6e272e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282783} :answer-field/id #uuid "533cc9b6-1b67-5a24-ae39-2f81ecaf9c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64116d30-10df-5392-b872-327ec4131913" :question/math-academy-id "q-163642" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d578f04-67db-563d-8983-4db4ec73c898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282226} {:answer/id #uuid "51f7bf8f-4efd-5d7f-96b7-3c77cf3c6f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282392} {:answer/id #uuid "79cd09f4-4199-52dc-8152-20707a405549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282729} {:answer/id #uuid "b246dcba-e8c5-564a-8cfe-9bdc4c3f4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283235} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf33d54-714b-5fe6-ae21-03e2980d4f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282729} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed1e5bb-49ac-5272-a6a7-33e9778ce400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f138cc5-4903-5e5d-bf77-73940588090b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163643" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,\\pi ),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\pi  & \\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,\\pi )=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-\\pi \\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c85455b-d139-5529-bab2-45b2402995e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{52}{41}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282346} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3d630b-464d-5f06-83fd-c3957186b264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{67}{60}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282355} {:answer/id #uuid "d843aee0-301b-5b09-a077-abfdb2a72f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283574} {:answer/id #uuid "e735c20e-83c3-513b-a594-3908a91217d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{15}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283701} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a31c4e-3057-59d9-9eff-b136a1e834fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{93}{70}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283809} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a94be4-e4e1-5140-99a8-e71aa3065376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f598df43-5e0b-5786-812e-47618ac821c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163645" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies region bounded by two circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{14\\pi }M\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{14\\pi }Mr.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{14\\pi }M{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{14\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{31}{5}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{93M}{70}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{93M}{70}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{93}{70}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f78bd33-6ec2-5ea3-bb73-62d6a977bd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281971} {:answer/id #uuid "3994874d-b134-5ecf-a02a-504f3e025f28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282197} {:answer/id #uuid "7d529ed8-ebde-5140-9360-e33503ddd154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282773} {:answer/id #uuid "8bbed6b0-47f7-590c-890d-8f3d0d662468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282898} {:answer/id #uuid "d72ffc3d-0686-54db-8aab-a650691c726e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281971} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcf2344-c192-5bbf-8bcf-ebc4470b0678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "394b0509-9101-5b75-8b23-1291af4adfe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163650" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2$ for $x\\ge 0.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{y}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{5}{sin}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\left(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +1}{8}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot ({\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{8}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{3\\theta }{8}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }{32}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{M\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab6a3c0-c3e1-5faf-a3b5-1f9df8eb0439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281788} {:answer/id #uuid "963c0ac7-fb75-5f26-be6d-095144cc1a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282988} {:answer/id #uuid "96e5370c-ae50-5f3f-ab56-f5c60baf2007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282997} {:answer/id #uuid "de215552-ef7f-52d8-824c-221bc96c5356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283628} {:answer/id #uuid "e874ee3e-9978-5715-8678-f0a76a792f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282988} :answer-field/id #uuid "486a8471-c9dc-5d4f-98c6-ca2593d040fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "48056df1-bf31-5ab9-9987-0816b504b8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163654" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 6.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{9\\pi }{y}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{9\\pi }{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{r}^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot 36\\cdot {[\\frac{\\theta }{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9\\pi }\\cdot 36\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =4M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4M\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b3b03b-db7c-5e14-bf30-0c8e621a1cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282153} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6feef2-1cdf-5a8f-af31-d193a5e7b5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282344} {:answer/id #uuid "9b583607-518c-5a2b-857a-27893d2e3e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283043} {:answer/id #uuid "ee827f89-c824-5a29-84e7-d603d75dd34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283769} {:answer/id #uuid "f2efdc3d-8ffc-5626-8aeb-007c3da3965a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282344} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff9427d-b4f4-5845-9d75-84d60b4d221a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e21ba823-c822-5cad-82d7-ecb3f63bc4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-163657" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region between two circles ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{x}^{2}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11e011-ddea-59e8-8dff-d43e8421c05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282075} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb4490-4039-5683-91a8-0a65f669f1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282173} {:answer/id #uuid "56d26e9d-75f1-50b6-8862-bb604ee95175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282425} {:answer/id #uuid "d64f12cb-2342-5b14-a877-2b90fa45fea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283553} {:answer/id #uuid "f291a168-3756-5501-8c49-8ca24e7425b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282173} :answer-field/id #uuid "131473c8-21f1-5275-8cd8-8aaf77c5c763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bedf3e3-7671-5f19-9c93-56fbbbe7e210" :question/math-academy-id "q-163658" :question/problem "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2}{9\\pi }M({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{2}{9\\pi }M{r}^{2}.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }M{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }M({\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }M\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }M\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{M\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb319d62-cd43-5416-b0dd-4e83919c9d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465501} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb461ff-be99-576b-bd61-91dd215bc98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465501} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ad56ac-7ece-5f66-8726-bc1880a95996" :answer-field/key "${a}_{11}=5$ and $det(A)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466594} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c79f8c24-9355-5076-90c6-a4367316d750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465503} {:answer/id #uuid "0f345692-7fb4-5ba8-be20-f1cb86aeca50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465504} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c07de42-afc1-558c-98b8-9dbe9fa265a9" :answer-field/key "$A$ is positive-definite" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466595} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ad342ee-39e5-5988-818a-6cf740e323a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465505} {:answer/id #uuid "61e4a967-940f-553b-b00f-ad2e376fcd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465506} :answer-field/id #uuid "66e05cae-0986-5eea-b30c-263f052d000a" :answer-field/key "$a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176421a6-fe88-5aee-94c6-bf927e037529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668467} {:answer/id #uuid "daf65277-30a7-55ed-a28f-2c0eac0fe806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668467} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa091a57-22dd-542d-b0d2-15fc21823e97" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59d86f24-1bbb-55d7-8154-a7330272c0b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163779" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=5$ and $det(A)=2$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=5,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is positive-definite” is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=5>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is positive-definite”; “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35364fce-dfa4-57e3-b4b3-7ffde26baef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465507} {:answer/id #uuid "abe81707-7bee-5f3d-9998-a89d0e5199e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465507} :answer-field/id #uuid "a40580c0-664c-5c83-92d6-b7ba6d5f88f8" :answer-field/key "${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4f5088-9b18-50ac-a385-00e43fa6bfd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465509} {:answer/id #uuid "30bdc7e6-fb2d-5f8e-8626-9b6d3ba37448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465510} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4dbc50-2382-5ace-b738-59f70f7581ec" :answer-field/key "$A$ is negative-definite" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35500f5f-9589-57c1-b99a-e40289eb9def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465511} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd6160-5664-504d-af50-fadc01831e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465511} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb722114-368c-5b3a-8c4c-b807849699e9" :answer-field/key "$a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466599} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8e34aba-b782-57dd-865f-8d8dbc2d18bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668469} {:answer/id #uuid "16d96e96-fe19-533c-9f2d-f69f99d58c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668469} :answer-field/id #uuid "be401d20-a82e-5719-988f-f54823a964bf" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03651691-7356-5261-96f0-0d0badb00da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163780" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=4$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is negative-definite” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e792ff8-af62-57d5-a566-a450e4d2c073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465513} {:answer/id #uuid "c06e501a-7fca-57b5-8fd8-00c7ba0828ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465513} :answer-field/id #uuid "7767a756-a8d3-5be5-bfc9-c38d9f8739ab" :answer-field/key "${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d91f22ee-b903-5aa9-a3a0-e9971360e020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465515} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaa9832-b748-5975-984c-dd66a942f7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465516} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa63e50-d620-5761-8485-c5faf4821dd9" :answer-field/key "$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476a495d-1c2e-59e7-a3f1-54bf4179f4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465517} {:answer/id #uuid "6a781903-27e5-5a45-afd1-4d6f7a5fdce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465518} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b937cb6-cf67-521a-8508-427cc88aed42" :answer-field/key "$a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d661981-7ef6-547f-8559-ebc1a2e52f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668471} {:answer/id #uuid "28d0287c-1fe7-58c2-ae53-e5d4f2d2915d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668471} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7966b7-90b2-5399-8401-a776cbc48f54" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c598e2d2-d26a-59ea-a0e5-a7e53e350ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163789" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite” is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a saddle point of $f$” is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite”; “$a$ is a saddle point of $f$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf8f149-6cad-5a5c-9d26-11b86bcaa720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465519} {:answer/id #uuid "6b04f584-4740-59cf-8497-f747191febb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465520} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddfefddd-b168-5878-87f2-f8f0e1876a2e" :answer-field/key "$det(A)=-19$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9329ab-4f40-5e23-86a5-bf4d0b285377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465521} {:answer/id #uuid "5441ae78-27c1-5694-8618-d6f72b5ef496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465522} :answer-field/id #uuid "d143db0c-e1c0-5daa-9470-d0193838ae88" :answer-field/key "$A$ is negative-definite" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12620dec-4540-5bfc-a97c-4be11c2d223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465523} {:answer/id #uuid "13ec6254-73f7-5076-8506-15526b0e95bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465523} :answer-field/id #uuid "c993b7a5-1549-5886-b715-49e5a62df038" :answer-field/key "$a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466605} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175831f0-e7f9-5e35-90a3-24f7cc808fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668473} {:answer/id #uuid "da4efb78-4184-513f-b7bf-fcb1ac9798c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668473} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b967bef-110d-57ea-aea5-ec9445b4f570" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "00b2158a-941e-5a2f-b68c-c230bee6df19" :question/math-academy-id "q-163791" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=-19$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement “$a$ is a local minimum of $f$” is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $det(A)=-19$; “$A$ is negative-definite” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25e85b48-e25d-5d7e-a12f-8159bd832968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282020} {:answer/id #uuid "80ceac4a-d282-5a63-9ed2-59aac7236bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282797} {:answer/id #uuid "94e4061d-6bf2-551d-9d08-13b546a38728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282973} {:answer/id #uuid "c4291e89-c785-5f31-ac5f-5ded7082483e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "217" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283399} {:answer/id #uuid "f19b55ab-60ba-5093-8d3d-8adece2d60a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "279" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282973} :answer-field/id #uuid "72cb7e9d-9783-5828-ab3a-82a4edc2416f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "89ef4ae2-d60c-5f6f-8b7b-82204e9a3ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-164171" :question/problem "![](images/78/78c28583961f2588c3e080d227e2e4f20a557ed77dcb20a6332092fe5915b22c.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (1+2)=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=5\\cdot 7-3\\cdot 9=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,8\\,|\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e16935-1003-52ed-a03b-f4c61dd870d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281751} {:answer/id #uuid "264a88bc-6062-5e3b-9a17-7a92ed55ba6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282024} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf06f2c-9f8f-538b-8f01-0758002d1584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10+10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282087} {:answer/id #uuid "5f45f379-dd47-579f-966c-3906aef12d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282491} {:answer/id #uuid "719a6e30-a3a3-51da-9f47-cbd0d927b100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282491} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b1957c-b96b-5d85-8ee8-bf171f645eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ffd9d977-ab54-5f18-98fb-c284e3e5b28b" :question/math-academy-id "q-16419" :question/problem "If $z=5+5\\text{i}$, calculate $z+\\overset{―}{z}.$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(5) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=5+5\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=5-5\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(5+5\\text{i})+(5-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(5+5)+\\text{i}(5-5) \\\\  & =10+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2df0088f-9801-59cb-8c24-c224eb0a7caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282091} {:answer/id #uuid "799920f9-5030-59a3-9129-135ce2061d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282728} {:answer/id #uuid "99119964-b1bb-5b8d-af31-d8847d8d09fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283017} {:answer/id #uuid "d412ee6a-b879-5b55-ab1f-46612658360f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283538} {:answer/id #uuid "fc02a390-cb73-5c68-8cda-7a82e7f49a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282728} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d65ef1-894e-5d24-b88d-a5db2e59abff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b1dbe1a-4744-5d2b-b075-9b0768e44a32" :question/math-academy-id "q-164529" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\sqrt{3}Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dff99d8-4315-57a5-ab5b-4823227846a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282229} {:answer/id #uuid "412d0866-ca19-5761-a3fb-4a3c5cd477c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282249} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6c948e-6894-5ce4-8baa-2b42f6ba0f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283190} {:answer/id #uuid "c257c833-4928-58af-aba1-b3c6c989d552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283380} {:answer/id #uuid "d2466787-6200-586e-b915-a6efc0b46d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282249} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f65c39-39dd-5a0c-9783-45c0df932054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f2fde67-bcf7-53c3-b2cf-283192588e86" :question/math-academy-id "q-164536" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,2X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107a8255-05a4-55dc-ad4d-961eeb4348c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281839} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae8b64b-a88c-5598-90aa-8ff521dafab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282063} {:answer/id #uuid "625f0cec-33dc-5e19-a828-1cfb4755b743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282531} {:answer/id #uuid "c42293f2-fdb3-5ff2-a206-d8d51f0c90c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283398} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a326e5-a2eb-55a6-9457-f0be853691fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281839} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50970dc-e5bf-5386-96f4-7e3f9bb9ebcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "33af4b17-5f7f-54f3-b197-4ddacd030cca" :question/math-academy-id "q-164538" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,5X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e51b29d-3922-57b7-a6db-262bdf2aa512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281960} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6d6188-c837-556f-856b-7a21e729e1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283312} {:answer/id #uuid "d87cf499-0457-573f-8994-a0b74b5d8c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283576} {:answer/id #uuid "e7708729-b3dc-5172-83d7-5fa45e6f9f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283704} {:answer/id #uuid "f40bf110-64d2-5366-8339-b49c72ee0916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283312} :answer-field/id #uuid "917996d3-386e-52a1-b9cf-e5900e417135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d47c9c2f-b85d-5864-9454-5cf822acfda9" :question/math-academy-id "q-164539" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,3X,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a324868-94e5-584e-83f8-a07e6eaf0feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282320} {:answer/id #uuid "92e1e968-1153-558b-9b20-1f5c2fdd9352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282957} {:answer/id #uuid "98bd0430-93ca-5ae5-bf92-623afa068449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283013} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f160f4-b61f-5cb1-8690-2f3e6a0defbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283435} {:answer/id #uuid "cae8b5d3-e82c-5d1d-8340-805c30ad03cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282320} :answer-field/id #uuid "9102502b-9c5b-5909-8319-71302abf7d3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7653a746-08d6-57ac-b90a-90dc212f031b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164570" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a572e24-0fab-5a7c-b96c-fb5059da7e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282322} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab8c6ed-c3cf-564e-8023-87206f2d51c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282611} {:answer/id #uuid "c50d40f0-2b6d-50bc-9fd2-20340f5efb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283405} {:answer/id #uuid "db164c54-1ade-58f5-b8b6-99fd041e9872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283601} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6868ef-45b2-555e-b88a-9b1a6637f829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283405} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5ed087-e4ec-5d5e-aa92-eace7f622ead" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4b7d13c3-1036-5b34-ad8f-b788a7cdde1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164571" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,y,\\theta )$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee1cedb-cf77-58df-88d0-8a09770af4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281966} {:answer/id #uuid "c350ea3b-7c60-57ed-9b6d-5996931e44c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283390} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa1e793-8653-5dda-b245-4abab68b60d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283643} {:answer/id #uuid "f27ec993-c084-5621-930b-e38ab654e84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283806} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2a1eeb-8df8-5051-8d48-50ac5a2f841e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281966} :answer-field/id #uuid "a15395ba-6933-5238-a791-1735ca46312d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a55182cd-3d5d-5237-a9f1-ae80e95752b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164576" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cbe7350-461e-50da-a656-9c51e2759d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282350} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0312d7-2ee1-5704-ad25-426443059d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282458} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b83361-516f-51a3-aa05-80036b7b45cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283277} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cc6887-730e-5cdc-967f-b54769391913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283511} {:answer/id #uuid "edcceffb-5be6-5100-8def-58f883144f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283277} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c46c94c-4e65-54d2-b81f-cda5b581d6fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f9bb7567-4a33-5f5f-afbf-0fd2306a3699" :question/math-academy-id "q-164580" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=3Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=3Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,3Z,\\sqrt{3}\\,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3Z,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f9a284-47ca-5fab-a99b-2f23c162ae05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281741} {:answer/id #uuid "65e0b51f-22e1-56df-bd50-57dd0191972b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282564} {:answer/id #uuid "65e2eba7-c0b3-5d96-b7cc-03b5edaa759d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282565} {:answer/id #uuid "8373a2eb-156b-5c9d-ac13-c1d180bc4de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282825} {:answer/id #uuid "c0616702-a7d0-533b-922a-e64fc0a780de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282564} :answer-field/id #uuid "72e34ce0-52b9-5821-919a-60ed69db02cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "616360d8-4e4c-5cad-aa7e-d6671a30098b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164581" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-{z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a997d6-f23f-5726-8d5e-dce9ff282de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281841} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1cd54-3056-5e19-8510-9daec11242a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282328} {:answer/id #uuid "84cc567f-e8ba-5c43-b77b-f8734d8a8e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282833} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff49fa6-d3e8-526b-89af-4becd987d0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282936} {:answer/id #uuid "ade82791-0d7b-5826-a005-00a455d3ba99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282833} :answer-field/id #uuid "096ab15f-2311-5b6b-a954-8b99afb22072" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6cc69269-3a00-5c14-8bae-00fffeafbde7" :question/math-academy-id "q-164585" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=6Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{2}Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=6Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{2}Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,6Z,\\sqrt{2}Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6Z,\\,\\sqrt{2}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ca9b3f6-a34b-52fe-8611-8027b61b8d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282223} {:answer/id #uuid "750e2f9a-bf2a-50ec-989f-581b2cf355d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282691} {:answer/id #uuid "7a00790d-ca37-5b81-aa89-b3c4195e6718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282733} {:answer/id #uuid "7d530887-cbac-5423-966c-867f23fd57ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282774} {:answer/id #uuid "85288df3-165f-56de-9fb0-de5eb11bc629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282837} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b32d818-b11e-592c-b2a9-0d4301658974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50acb18d-42b2-51b5-84ca-fb90f2f0a7c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-164586" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Z,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Z,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Z,4Y,2X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Z,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292677a6-e892-524f-95f1-273431940aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282045} {:answer/id #uuid "58eb1845-8939-531a-8500-bbd3129321ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282445} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fd0174-03e4-5b03-8bcd-2bba687a9ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283135} {:answer/id #uuid "c80eda4e-da10-580a-b688-018a1d6a19ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283437} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f551df-9b45-55c6-b296-1e87587a1c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283437} :answer-field/id #uuid "002b9cd7-553f-5381-9f24-6db4d6c581b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "19a5066e-bfd6-5f62-b6bf-e79cc6306a4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-164587" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,4Y,2Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5931fbcb-d5d0-514f-9a40-b9dacd7f5ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282447} {:answer/id #uuid "5b96e589-82a0-56f3-8019-45d38ccf7778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282465} {:answer/id #uuid "9af211ae-d2c1-5419-914b-d99b2a2e61c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283040} {:answer/id #uuid "c7155d84-c3f7-59e4-acae-f39485ce9da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283422} {:answer/id #uuid "e377d96d-2e54-5472-ad65-76a95cf2bbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283673} :answer-field/id #uuid "2670f341-82e8-5d70-983c-019cc825c363" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b54313ba-f7d3-5825-b960-2ab13d62a75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-165282" :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec9ea01-bfeb-5dfa-bb0d-e8dda89e8cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281819} {:answer/id #uuid "2f167cc3-d0d4-5732-aea7-9737587d5446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282107} {:answer/id #uuid "a46851a8-a830-5300-ba0a-c94605be1ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283122} {:answer/id #uuid "a598ce3d-2796-5960-afd3-75d843fcccbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c751ad-dde6-5f48-96a4-ca006c0a37ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f183aa4f-94f5-53a7-8fa6-3240e8f8d306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47feb8ed-9de6-5cab-9530-5d8b3448a054" :question/math-academy-id "q-165413" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab50b4da-933b-580c-90e3-d57fe918ca3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283174} {:answer/id #uuid "e0621d5d-1546-515b-b160-26c36601cee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283647} {:answer/id #uuid "eac1d727-6b15-5ede-a524-9f9b5cca59df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283741} {:answer/id #uuid "f038538b-7314-51bc-a23b-a2dc236cb725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283787} {:answer/id #uuid "f848449c-f299-591e-af24-cf140841f848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283647} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1857575-157f-5eb5-ba46-57df0567255a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e623a86a-8a2d-5ca1-949a-2b47126336d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-165414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{\\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e84fab9-e746-5643-9f88-bbdedd0aebee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282099} {:answer/id #uuid "3714acca-aab3-506a-bb9b-3e48939becd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282180} {:answer/id #uuid "3f6af8c7-6cdf-5f3f-bf89-1f1ebde3bdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282237} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb78399-3d43-5cbd-a3ad-3693c8b26d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282466} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb945ce-c0cd-5bb8-b67b-7713c30f3159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283495} :answer-field/id #uuid "a815d77e-d6b1-5a12-9ccf-a217d10b65c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3d8dc28-6054-520c-8968-9399a2a4230c" :question/math-academy-id "q-166546" :question/problem "Find the equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $f(x)={log}_{5}⁡(2x+4)-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the vertical asymptote of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡x$ is $x=0.$\n\nTo find the equation of the vertical asymptote of $f(x),$ we set the argument of the logarithmic term equal to zero, and solve for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e3c4350-ac40-5ff9-8a09-56250f13df9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282483} {:answer/id #uuid "70df90bc-b41f-53c9-ace7-14daa143036c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282655} {:answer/id #uuid "7268a672-d5da-52a3-9409-8ef22f8f8007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282668} {:answer/id #uuid "aca0a2ac-645b-50e7-b37a-d45fee6649df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283184} {:answer/id #uuid "da63b78c-2331-50f1-a0b0-42446f4d812e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283184} :answer-field/id #uuid "85e23c55-3564-5b82-9ccb-0a33e23e13b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b15a7e63-b8ab-5083-9941-85a35f55da3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-166549" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)+1 & =0 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(4-2x) & =-1 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4-2x)} & ={2}^{-1} \\\\ 4-2x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2x & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{7}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103a6466-1db1-59e7-ac62-150d0d173d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281838} {:answer/id #uuid "1f57dcc0-bc0b-56e2-bb7d-eb149d855ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no minimum value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281969} {:answer/id #uuid "96d863e6-e7df-59ab-b424-a2d1e3ff090d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282996} {:answer/id #uuid "c71bfba1-cdd7-5e51-b222-85339f0ef584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283423} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2148e3-14fa-5cdc-8f8a-fc0556a67d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281969} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0be3b05-41da-51e0-bac8-4e0550c3d9b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92f6c1a4-5622-588c-a7ee-2153dc26416b" :question/math-academy-id "q-166550" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $f(x)=4{log}_{2}⁡(2x-3)+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and the function has no minimum value."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1dd875-1ab5-5d5c-8cee-d4a760e0fad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282077} {:answer/id #uuid "36e21432-61e2-5f75-bf7b-6c17d3bac35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282176} {:answer/id #uuid "878e3c0b-0fa7-530a-8b50-3b0123c90209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282857} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ea2448-8707-56af-883d-3fdfe6fbad5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} :answer-field/id #uuid "99251eef-a601-5418-8667-74393704a93e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15c14f08-def7-5eb6-8ced-3b42bca65403" :question/math-academy-id "q-167498" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "390ae2c8-dec1-5d3f-9c6b-1792d16e78b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282190} {:answer/id #uuid "4e373f20-5a94-571c-b052-8a90de7fdc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282363} {:answer/id #uuid "62273b79-193d-54d1-862d-e78f13b5f7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282526} {:answer/id #uuid "9376d9e5-6005-5501-a544-4fcc88cf2498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282959} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac240dd-95e7-55ec-a84e-d3b0436abcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282526} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f8ccfc5-1cac-5ad6-b112-c28a3ec8eaf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1db2761c-31e6-54a0-9828-fe3bf1a8a7c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-170377" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284631 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8332a7-4bd1-52e2-98d7-9038f8ce3235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281930} {:answer/id #uuid "6c39f138-7816-5f6d-a956-7902c5315703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282617} {:answer/id #uuid "97a55d9e-4e6c-5c38-b529-32f84ac78a16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283001} {:answer/id #uuid "acab738c-9574-5721-b030-a0b0df4c366b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283185} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa10416-ed51-536c-89e2-fee3615750c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283642} :answer-field/id #uuid "96932f9e-93ef-50d0-97d7-7284d7425f0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "496fb18e-ba8b-54b4-a477-d1058ddecd7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-170410" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f787485-bbf8-5fcc-8585-8b059c6d1316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281970} {:answer/id #uuid "30948e13-e0e4-533a-a6f9-5aaa08610944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282120} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb4dd99-b1d6-5677-9be4-afce453696e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283750} {:answer/id #uuid "f22aaf14-e55f-5ffd-ba05-b66de4ba750f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283802} {:answer/id #uuid "fb81bdb4-f9c2-5f58-a975-316cb82f7bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283890} :answer-field/id #uuid "e275f989-8b1b-539a-87a4-fba67d62a361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bda4f08-ef7b-5966-96c3-3621b7deb316" :question/math-academy-id "q-170412" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-4{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+14{x}_{3}^{2}-24{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 & 0 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -19 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc383aa-c6d8-5cb4-9f37-78f78398d296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282112} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcd893c-01c1-52b0-8774-2ca408dfc3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282899} {:answer/id #uuid "97b0eb56-a4d3-596c-a9c5-af16c716e2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283002} {:answer/id #uuid "e5691c8f-e6ed-5728-ace3-4968571878b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283691} {:answer/id #uuid "ef691221-3ed8-5189-8810-d04927763ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283774} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32f94de-a65d-57b8-a1d7-93ea2655c742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "876ee8ae-f199-5a6e-ad24-4a4c31d3be35" :question/math-academy-id "q-170494" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=9-6\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1b/1bc5f38d44d9df32a7493f8d210bbc17d716d8a63316e3d730a1c4258d56fe46.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{6}{9}\\right)=arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)≈0.588\\,00.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.588\\,00≈5.70\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f3ea30-3f9d-5508-ab40-ee2dba4f0888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281708} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5452df-294d-5701-b4af-bbf16d6e0530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282056} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d984c-3905-5e24-bf07-c5dd11a46a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282364} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2908d2-0e8d-5999-a64a-a03cd4b68ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} {:answer/id #uuid "c1eb5798-946a-56c8-9937-6c6a91e2207e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283306} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ab9ad5-298c-5e24-9d44-dc14f057d818" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "80991c77-0c00-52ac-bae2-41a3b83ff730" :question/math-academy-id "q-170515" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-18{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+81{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -27 \\\\ -9 & 81\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}9-\\lambda  & -27 \\\\ -27 & 81-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(81-\\lambda )-729 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-90\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -90) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\,90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=90.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58e43fd8-bedb-54d5-9723-4873762018ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282444} {:answer/id #uuid "6080b967-b1e5-508e-9eb9-b06f08ffc56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282507} {:answer/id #uuid "b44dda1b-d74d-5302-ac25-2322f81791f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283250} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c243a3-3d78-5363-a946-67271923a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283874} {:answer/id #uuid "fdac630b-fc7e-5910-8320-fe5c826d997e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282444} :answer-field/id #uuid "b28de760-832c-5bd3-997a-40bc410c2fcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "315ff370-acb2-54c0-a94c-7932c53e50d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170525" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+12{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-12-\\lambda  & 16 \\\\ 16 & 12-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-12-\\lambda )(12-\\lambda )-256 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-400 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -20)(\\lambda +20) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =20,\\,-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fcdee7-7ac9-56de-88c2-bc6e649d3116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282514} {:answer/id #uuid "8401fb17-15b9-5dc5-a5e7-d89a3bfa1e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282829} {:answer/id #uuid "94cc9450-39d8-5e58-92e4-93954393e7bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282971} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b2ebcc-3812-51ec-b6fd-80b665e2ed43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283429} {:answer/id #uuid "f4181463-8020-5bfc-b239-9574c62c514c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283821} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4313712-6e47-551e-bc79-54931942e74b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "40232e39-995b-5a30-a61d-aaa5fd4339dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-170526" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=8{x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $4{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{4},$ \n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -8 \\\\ -16 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}32 & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}32-\\lambda  & -32 \\\\ -32 & -16-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (32-\\lambda )(-16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda -1\\,536 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +32) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=48.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea60620-2ab9-5040-8270-703c9a1d6b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281818} {:answer/id #uuid "7936ae4e-f008-5af5-820c-1674250b0ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282726} {:answer/id #uuid "7c387048-20f2-5147-a642-ff8e502ef022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282760} {:answer/id #uuid "90f5b318-f4a9-5268-90ba-ba237d66d663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282944} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3d6901-3436-54f0-a0b1-ce4e806a253f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281818} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec15cdba-b1f7-5ecd-9880-c9e9cda591f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9bd05e5a-ceb5-5b6d-a1ee-24c21df80b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-17371" :question/problem "Given that $y=3ln⁡x+2{x}^{2}-5x$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3ln⁡x+2{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3ln⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+4x-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x}+4x-5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{3}{x}+4x-5)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1}+4\\cdot 1-5 \\\\  & =3+4-5 \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbb468a-9928-5c35-89ed-eaf197f78886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281954} {:answer/id #uuid "618693f7-594d-59ce-a534-76a8e9e2b379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡3+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282519} {:answer/id #uuid "888a6635-e022-5c1a-a6c7-78955c8928cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡3+5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282867} {:answer/id #uuid "8957ae1d-f55e-532c-822e-e38dcfa2b32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282874} {:answer/id #uuid "956605d2-ce2e-57d7-b286-ab43b24751bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282519} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7e9ba42-2c84-571b-94b9-4df571509ab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e51c642-d912-5d35-8cf2-7c273fba130d" :question/math-academy-id "q-17388" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}+5{e}^{x}$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x}+5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({3}^{x})+5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}ln⁡3+5{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={({3}^{x}ln⁡3+5{e}^{x})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & ={3}^{0}ln⁡3+5{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\cdot ln⁡3+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =ln⁡3+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21d15fb-2160-5ddb-8d0d-dd3c8859d25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283230} {:answer/id #uuid "bd88e00f-0bab-53c5-ae3a-cd2e0c598b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283335} {:answer/id #uuid "c82943c8-67cf-544d-9c5b-c8f16482d03a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283440} {:answer/id #uuid "ceae27ce-631e-5de9-8e9e-ef20729a9fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283494} {:answer/id #uuid "d62a89b2-11e2-569b-beb8-fd34178406a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283494} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7b61891-05e3-593d-9055-f9ddd0b32bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33e3f449-e82e-5b53-88bd-da8b2571cb27" :question/math-academy-id "q-17654" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-cos⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int cos⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =sin⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (-cos⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  & =-\\int cos⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-sin⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4dddb2-8ce0-518b-941e-482ca5265f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282747} {:answer/id #uuid "bc54686a-049f-5040-b7a9-b8062cc1c750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283321} {:answer/id #uuid "be37be01-215e-5391-bca4-f872aefba533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283341} {:answer/id #uuid "ea403761-0f48-5b27-a322-4bdbfaf43080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283735} {:answer/id #uuid "f3762c53-5b99-5615-8c9e-e78f50dd46f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282747} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d75bdd-6edb-5b58-9b7f-46c8d0ecd14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a05f390f-2f4a-5c91-9fbd-0dbf4e6d9e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-17912" :question/problem "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=-tan⁡x$ when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change  when $x=\\pi $ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-tan⁡x) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\pi ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-{sec}^{2}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $-1.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "1045aaaf-0a76-5469-afdc-876ec049ae29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{11}^{x}-ln⁡11-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284929} {:answer/id #uuid "10c998c3-16a5-5186-a341-ea5db157e875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284935} {:answer/id #uuid "1618e03f-9620-5bd5-85cd-1b7993323dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{6}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284987} {:answer/id #uuid "203dab4a-3058-5e0e-90fa-52dcc35d2407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285078} {:answer/id #uuid "2be26547-4c09-5374-8bce-e8a7c5344cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} {:answer/id #uuid "46da0d85-3682-54d0-b58f-5f7b61e746ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2cos⁡x+4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285442} {:answer/id #uuid "52f316ad-3c3e-52eb-aff0-84068ba4a2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285549} {:answer/id #uuid "55c1f225-5772-53df-9957-91ede62f7763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285577} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb9929b-9ec9-53dc-8eef-e5d5f9b92e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}+\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(3{x}^{2})}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285639} {:answer/id #uuid "66e054b3-95bb-5323-86e6-76b98c6154cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285725} {:answer/id #uuid "92c0eef2-67dc-58fb-baa0-cc77ea56c821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286120} {:answer/id #uuid "a626cf8e-2190-59a0-ac5b-d97bc0916669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286304} {:answer/id #uuid "aa30aba2-91df-575b-9e42-a1c8a2d47cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{11}^{x}ln⁡x-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286338} {:answer/id #uuid "c13aff45-c853-5d8b-ae11-7b4d6befdcb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ln⁡11+\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286556} {:answer/id #uuid "c234a623-5300-5076-898f-6693292506e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{arctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286565} {:answer/id #uuid "eff2509a-f3c8-5644-a073-b8761ffb95e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{11}^{x}ln⁡11-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1991bbb0-ef8f-5a10-b346-2f1814800921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285015} :answer-field/id #uuid "38ff489e-ee02-5c49-bcb3-9ca5df403b23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1c00eaa2-97d9-5d18-b472-fd971e44b02f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181575" :question/problem "If $A={5,15,45,135,…,7\\,971\\,615},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n5,\\,15,\\,45,\\,135,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{15} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=5({3}^{n-1})=\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{N} & =7\\,971\\,615 \\\\ {3}^{N} & =4\\,782\\,969 \\\\ N & ={log}_{3}⁡(4\\,782\\,969) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(4\\,782\\,969)}{{log}_{10}⁡(3)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{5}{3}(3)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2217fe-dcb0-50c0-a6fb-3520c1b699bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285758} :answer-field/id #uuid "4edde710-adcb-56b7-b1ea-8ff3a64cc3b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0f3f92d9-7968-5a40-b27d-a5448f33107a" :question/math-academy-id "q-181576" :question/problem "If $A=\\{2,10,50,250,…,2\\,441\\,406\\,250\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =5 \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{10} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=2({5}^{n-1})=\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{N} & =2\\,441\\,406\\,250 \\\\ {5}^{N} & =6\\,103\\,515\\,625 \\\\ N & ={log}_{5}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(6\\,103\\,515\\,625)}{{log}_{10}⁡(5)} \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{2}{5}(5)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 14\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $14$ elements, we must have $|A|=14.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5127f79-84ec-5a63-9053-8fc55f00b0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286727} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0da287a-f97d-535e-8052-a16a00840c54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b8a1a95-0f23-5970-9fcd-2420a63c21a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-181578" :question/problem "If $A=\\{3,18,108,648,…,181\\,398\\,528\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,108,\\,648,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{108}{18} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=3({6}^{n-1})=\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{N} & =181\\,398\\,528 \\\\ {6}^{N} & =362\\,797\\,056 \\\\ N & ={log}_{6}⁡(362\\,797\\,056) \\\\ N & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(362\\,797\\,056)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{2}(6)^{n}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e838f71-c8fc-5cc9-b5e8-71952927da3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285952} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfb84326-ae02-5e54-b1a2-11c13b41b8b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6db0e5e4-2597-5059-a427-ab2af5a1e3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-181582" :question/problem "If $A={1,6,36,216,…,2\\,176\\,782\\,336},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,6,\\,36,\\,216,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {6}^{n-1}={6}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{N-1} & =2\\,176\\,782\\,336 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{6}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(2\\,176\\,782\\,336)}{{log}_{10}⁡(6)} \\\\ N-1 & =12 \\\\ N & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{6}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 13}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $13$ elements, we must have $|A|=13.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c04387e-2de4-5007-8a28-35e3647172d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285629} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2f9327-45dd-55ee-a150-8f7cf0251230" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dce5ff3b-6287-5298-b5a6-85554a4237ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-181583" :question/problem "If $A=\\{1,11,121,1\\,331,…,214\\,358\\,881\\},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,11,\\,121,\\,1\\,331,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =11 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{11} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {11}^{n-1}={11}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{11}^{N-1} & =214\\,358\\,881 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{11}⁡(214\\,358\\,881) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(214\\,358\\,881)}{{log}_{10}⁡(11)} \\\\ N-1 & =8 \\\\ N & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA=\\{{11}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $9$ elements, we must have $|A|=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4e15c16-0f94-5733-93fa-0e8b2466221c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286591} :answer-field/id #uuid "0431b34f-0f02-5d9b-8b24-8db8b4e87c2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d6b9cd7-fb00-52fc-be6a-1180e9d7096e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181584" :question/problem "If $A={1,9,81,729,…,3\\,486\\,784\\,401},$ then $|A|=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n1,\\,9,\\,81,\\,729,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{9} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}=1\\cdot {9}^{n-1}={9}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{9}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{N-1} & =3\\,486\\,784\\,401 \\\\ N-1 & ={log}_{9}⁡(3\\,486\\,784\\,401) \\\\ N-1 & =\\frac{{log}_{10}⁡(3\\,486\\,784\\,401)}{{log}_{10}⁡(9)} \\\\ N-1 & =10 \\\\ N & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA={{9}^{n-1}:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le 11}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $11$ elements, we must have $|A|=11.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0608f7d5-cb5d-5c12-9904-be67e454cb51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465713} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e2376b-22de-5207-8959-882360f31747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465714} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9244d4-ca29-5b09-ad60-1f882ad66970" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c75b4b6-2e79-52bd-a819-8ed4e83fe956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465715} {:answer/id #uuid "e095a3ae-0bbe-5338-98a3-f4b4bf7e6dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465715} :answer-field/id #uuid "b765a2fa-7d98-5023-a0f1-a961bd05dc4d" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466701} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "666b4d37-5ccf-50da-8f1a-4bedddc9892b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465717} {:answer/id #uuid "576fb5f4-86dc-5c68-9abb-6fb175b7ba2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465718} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec2b8b6-edfa-5cfb-9457-1478e9b90ba5" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "614d2565-08b2-5cdc-9df9-02ccb7032ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-181650" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,…\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nB=\\{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,2{sec}^{3}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,…\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf41930a-e02a-5bf1-999e-558be53fd1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465719} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fb9b89-a596-5bd6-95f2-b8f58bc9a715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465720} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db97015-4657-5b3a-8988-5a1cda4af5a1" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}A=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ab02f8-3072-57d1-91ef-e9494c550f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465721} {:answer/id #uuid "ec147f98-965d-5f68-a749-c182da54c5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465722} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc5c70be-94f5-5cab-b793-286375bde231" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}B=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ef7ac6b-6eac-5c02-937f-1eec66fbc7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465723} {:answer/id #uuid "39a94884-0c74-5050-932d-8ceb38c13403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465723} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e26f0a-758a-5de5-99e7-f577b7dad806" :answer-field/key "$\\begin{gathered}C=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\\\\\,n\\in {N}_{0}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "135e3cdb-fb83-5539-93de-65d28076eb80" :question/math-academy-id "q-181652" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({sec}^{2}⁡x)=2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,{sec}^{2}⁡x,2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)=-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nC={3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=4,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $B$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c9f2a3b-0eee-5493-8f44-dd1ed8681bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285179} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5fde06-579f-50d8-9d4e-5f60a07068f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the sets are infinite." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285490} {:answer/id #uuid "6be2998c-ad5a-5230-9daf-acddd1a47077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285777} {:answer/id #uuid "70bb31a8-1743-568a-bde7-2d6a0db6eb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285825} {:answer/id #uuid "afd57f33-a3e6-563c-86fa-61ed975825fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285777} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6adf5f-85de-5dcd-a425-1ef950f73b4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2136b6c3-2aad-5c78-a7bc-7af98e2a3169" :question/math-academy-id "q-181653" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$\n- $$\n\\displaystyle C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nA={-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2{csc}^{3}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)=-{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,-{csc}^{2}⁡x,2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x,…}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d5ba56-de94-5e4c-b177-c5dc46ffbd51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285000} {:answer/id #uuid "77114028-3901-51ca-9e0e-c825093eb6e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285895} {:answer/id #uuid "7dacdb7f-b468-5607-98ad-e22d7d3b1b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285945} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c9c2b5-e4ef-5a55-b086-105ff3094bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286267} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb37ef9-8f27-5f7e-8602-dfc794e1f1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286978} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17a460e-868e-59c7-9907-c5516e2c691c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "418920c2-d3a5-5269-af1c-6648eaf6ff36" :question/math-academy-id "q-18271" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(A)=-30,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-k & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2k & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k\\cdot 3+0-3\\cdot (-6k) \\\\  & =-3k+18k \\\\  & =15k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-30,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15k & =-30 \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f8a0b14-4d5c-5c97-adda-ad9333ee9989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439563} {:answer/id #uuid "dd74231f-397c-535b-b601-fd3c79196c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439564} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc6cf2f7-d1dc-587c-98ad-df0c79eb39bd" :answer-field/key "$p=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440139} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b99de5ad-e5ec-584b-85db-f3fe0159078d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439565} {:answer/id #uuid "6a78a0f8-e11d-55e4-ae7a-bb450fd88339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439566}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439565} :answer-field/id #uuid "60fa672d-cabd-5202-b542-e29c05c6092c" :answer-field/key "$p=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440140} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0824bc4a-1fb6-5f8e-8f46-06f33e4a0f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439567} {:answer/id #uuid "4bccbe1d-f704-50a7-a7cc-0373aa409b61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439567} :answer-field/id #uuid "b90240d0-5950-5b79-9a0c-e7150762e3ac" :answer-field/key "$p=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440141} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e7c5d4-430e-5f20-88bd-00f87d74ace8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439569} {:answer/id #uuid "82587c1b-bbe1-5f58-a819-0bba3bb63520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439570} :answer-field/id #uuid "1827baf7-1958-50ff-a89b-0e47a7c1cf1e" :answer-field/key "$p=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a6ffa24-d6e8-52ac-ac3d-5fab4f24df3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-18272" :question/problem "Given that $M=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $det(M)=-23,$ find all the possible values of $p.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & p & 0 \\\\ p-3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\begin{vmatrix}p & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ p-3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & p \\\\ p-3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (3p-{p}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3-2{p}^{2}+6p \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+6p-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-23,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{p}^{2}+6p-3 & =-23 \\\\ 2{p}^{2}-6p-20 & =0 \\\\ {p}^{2}-3p-10 & =0 \\\\ p & =\\frac{-(-3)\\pm \\sqrt{(-3)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-10)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pm 7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions are $p=-2$ and $p=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45901bed-283a-5f6b-a0e8-c82fb99c77cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285423} {:answer/id #uuid "590cea1c-0a7c-5f07-8068-694058c3a868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285603} {:answer/id #uuid "6649b745-668c-5e5d-b83b-1cf253b1e095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285716} {:answer/id #uuid "d89e0dd4-5791-5cf7-b3cb-314c7f78a09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286760} {:answer/id #uuid "e7904946-6db5-50df-9602-9962763b4fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285716} :answer-field/id #uuid "70430d88-1fa4-5196-ac2c-0e31afc80076" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dc94d59e-a3d1-5387-8338-9ada65b5d11f" :question/math-academy-id "q-18281" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0+9)-4(0-6)+1(0+2) \\\\  & =18+24+2 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{44}\\cdot 6=\\frac{3}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14576e31-28a0-51c9-98e0-cb0e88b08edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284968} {:answer/id #uuid "87d3e443-ddd4-51ad-bc3b-642891265441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286034} {:answer/id #uuid "a41b5f85-604a-5b3f-adf2-0829d0c2aa47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286292} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e84826-763e-57fb-8b0f-9e77c3cef0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286446} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4a17fa-acb7-5e63-84dc-98fea7d83242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286034} :answer-field/id #uuid "12bcbace-edf5-5ef6-b7b6-ab1e80cb8fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b297987-b62d-5714-afb9-ac77feefadc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18282" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6942a4fb-bae8-501d-ad2a-66103f54ad63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285753} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6dd01f-de8b-5f99-94f9-eab25a4ecb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285936} {:answer/id #uuid "82e68463-04bd-50cb-87ed-af0f56c6cae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285988} {:answer/id #uuid "9b48164a-0903-562b-8d03-395f496b0c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286211} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e18ab7-2363-5919-84cd-f61b850984a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285753} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed4da02-e9fd-53f8-937f-fdc5a00e81e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9f46698-54a7-56c6-b512-47750a5ae9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-18283" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $-4.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =15-(-1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}=\\frac{1}{-4}\\cdot 16=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e56ee5-4fb6-5816-869f-e39c23a78b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285084} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbf79ad-839a-5229-93e9-65fad5cfd1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285385} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb285a6-7afd-5ad8-ab82-98a513400c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cea90f-f0ed-5d57-bca3-334408a81e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287013} {:answer/id #uuid "fd525363-2a62-5343-aac2-348e4bfefb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287105} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac120b1e-d324-518f-9e93-f08acaa655f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "670b2139-910a-54c1-b1cc-9c11eb5fdc1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18284" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $-16.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22}=\\frac{1}{-16}\\cdot 4=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f415776-37dc-54e3-9800-2d01e510bf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284919} {:answer/id #uuid "60a2a07e-a39e-5566-8bba-7375b15d76ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285675} {:answer/id #uuid "7595df60-c82c-590b-b868-221b7368f2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285879} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ab4c0e-9b0e-5237-9348-d4be45093c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286252} {:answer/id #uuid "f133bf14-3c6d-5286-a6d1-bf00852e4721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286252} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa7a81a-d6ae-5365-9fa0-1142a694af59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5cade28-be41-57ee-910f-46e652127d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-18285" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-0+(0-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232327fd-1dfa-5996-8e84-95bb6a9f8cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285103} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f41583-27a2-56ac-9a18-17faf2b31721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286592} {:answer/id #uuid "c776e656-4613-5245-90ac-bb7e8e9dd6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286614} {:answer/id #uuid "d31191b1-ed1c-5824-a66f-2602858ec4ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286707} {:answer/id #uuid "ef342a1d-6164-5836-994c-98cb5484b941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{39}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286982}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286707} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c346e23-1fbd-5f16-925c-878fb4d2e5f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dcb03ff4-bc8a-5928-846f-20e56388fdb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18286" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $39.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}=\\frac{1}{39}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{39}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06123fff-7c78-51f2-81b7-d35d5c15b5e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284836} {:answer/id #uuid "11d094be-9dc7-5405-8cce-9907e9c51654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284945} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9cbbbd-12e1-5cce-8786-a3ceed193971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285356} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1a2f27-2e9e-5b7f-a874-29bebc8a2d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286347} {:answer/id #uuid "b72dc0b0-3001-5783-b493-cbb1bcd44dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284836} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c45b83d-dd3d-58f3-83e0-2b583f24f687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "35c43087-68b2-5ef3-b590-6196109cdd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-18287" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $24.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =24-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}=\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 24=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aba01f9-7f51-5a8f-a7a8-dc4fa8fb483a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285161} {:answer/id #uuid "51897cf6-89ff-547a-a77d-fb4c229f6e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285537} {:answer/id #uuid "83757217-5865-5baa-8ad8-1b2c54dde4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285995} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f44981-7ac4-5b60-aa71-18f4807ae1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286563} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e75515-8616-5fe3-a6a1-8dc2e1fa3a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{71}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286563} :answer-field/id #uuid "909dfb56-2abf-58f1-9f5d-3fcdb81ce8b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b862f7e3-dfeb-537e-b5d6-1de6a752faf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-18288" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $71.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-3-10) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{71}\\cdot 13=\\frac{13}{71}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203dab4a-3058-5e0e-90fa-52dcc35d2407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285078} {:answer/id #uuid "46da0d85-3682-54d0-b58f-5f7b61e746ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2cos⁡x+4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285442} {:answer/id #uuid "55c1f225-5772-53df-9957-91ede62f7763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285577} {:answer/id #uuid "66e054b3-95bb-5323-86e6-76b98c6154cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285725} {:answer/id #uuid "92c0eef2-67dc-58fb-baa0-cc77ea56c821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285577} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a13dae-3ed1-5b21-b23c-46bc3ddb17e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3974f0a3-ea97-56a9-a9e2-a7e59e1e84b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-1847" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x)=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)=cos⁡x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x) & =4(-sin⁡x)+2(cos⁡x) \\\\  & =2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fa2ca0-5410-5738-8228-11c0d9ec4e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284832} {:answer/id #uuid "21163962-dbce-5c4a-9b9a-9ca9a5b9852a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285087} {:answer/id #uuid "3b82e03f-7320-5fd8-aa40-e2facea3ab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285348} {:answer/id #uuid "9eea7b77-2513-5960-bc9f-8572e987d50f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286240} {:answer/id #uuid "e5038023-5b22-563c-be39-21b7f5c99eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286240} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef361f71-5834-5185-8f00-5ef7a49d8455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f830e92-57d1-5d05-97f7-0f6a59992f23" :question/math-academy-id "q-1900" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=2x-5cos⁡x$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is given by the value of the derivative ${y}^{′}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-5cos⁡x) \\\\  & =2-5(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & =2+5sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(30) & =2+5sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2+5\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $\\frac{9}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d15acc-1672-5ec4-bb78-f3474884b897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284830} {:answer/id #uuid "48fabaf5-be8a-589c-8cf0-074c455a9240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285458} {:answer/id #uuid "63f73e2e-4004-5fc8-9825-f8337f6c2b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285697} {:answer/id #uuid "766bb2aa-4af5-5e09-aed3-a777505c1a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285891} {:answer/id #uuid "e40e7c86-1ed2-5d5e-9940-61e65ceaec5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8570317-ecab-5ed9-a868-188d2c2ead8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9daa906-697e-5ad9-9a96-96e98679f403" :question/math-academy-id "q-1904" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ if $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-x)+5(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2x-5csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the derivative and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =-2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-5csc⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)cot⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1618e03f-9620-5bd5-85cd-1b7993323dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{6}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284987} {:answer/id #uuid "2be26547-4c09-5374-8bce-e8a7c5344cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} {:answer/id #uuid "35fc1512-0fee-559d-9989-9672ff8d1d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285285} {:answer/id #uuid "5c628087-cf37-51b1-ac2c-fb127ba0396b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285634} {:answer/id #uuid "c234a623-5300-5076-898f-6693292506e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{arctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} :answer-field/id #uuid "418f8f05-ac62-5559-a3d3-33900d47dceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9a202483-d8b8-51e4-982b-f5e54acb06af" :question/math-academy-id "q-19067" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)arctan({x}^{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, using the formula for the derivative of $arctanx$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arctan({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =arctan({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{3})^{2}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xarctan({x}^{3})}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}{1+{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287922 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c998c3-16a5-5186-a341-ea5db157e875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284935} {:answer/id #uuid "361c6acf-d3c4-566b-81c5-4427943429df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{2})}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285288} {:answer/id #uuid "52f316ad-3c3e-52eb-aff0-84068ba4a2fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285549} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb9929b-9ec9-53dc-8eef-e5d5f9b92e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}+\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(3{x}^{2})}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285639} {:answer/id #uuid "a626cf8e-2190-59a0-ac5b-d97bc0916669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285549} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5b5a09f-002f-5cfd-b2b9-fcc7e7b6ff20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "acce5149-acaa-575b-8d72-2597df12abd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-19071" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, using the formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ and applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3}))-\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-({x}^{3})^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{x})}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{6}}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡({x}^{3})}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12140e52-3b06-5185-be22-2844d3e7ceba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6{e}^{x}}{1+9{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284948} {:answer/id #uuid "27d7bad3-db61-5498-b1b5-1e9ca7cba98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})}{1+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285134} {:answer/id #uuid "67fbe76a-ae93-5e25-b985-9023ce73635e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})}{1+3{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285744} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdbd40d-c1be-562a-bc3f-ac47c16b29dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6{e}^{x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})}{1+9{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286950} {:answer/id #uuid "ed93ae5e-af93-53b5-90ac-6a55b7664419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})}{1+9{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdd3579-1c4f-5103-8e40-492c1c99af75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b87a417-c49c-58f2-aac5-0b730775e872" :question/math-academy-id "q-19080" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={arctan}^{2}⁡(3{e}^{x}).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({arctan}^{2}⁡(3{e}^{x})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{(3{e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+9{e}^{2x}}\\cdot 3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{x}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3{e}^{x})}{1+9{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d0cca3-b930-583a-a67f-b7d2fbb30dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}(1+\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2}))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284811} {:answer/id #uuid "29fc1d7b-795e-5330-9fed-248e9bdfb72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}(1+{\\operatorname{arcsec} }^{2}({x}^{2}))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285156} {:answer/id #uuid "9615675e-d4e7-5987-b50d-4218c3a5988d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}(1+{\\operatorname{arcsec} }^{2}({x}^{2}))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286150} {:answer/id #uuid "c54342e1-8752-5e65-99b0-9ee8dc40302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}(1+{\\operatorname{arcsec} }^{2}({x}^{2}))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286596} {:answer/id #uuid "d33e9472-db4f-5b53-bf24-f8efe37e24bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(1-\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2}))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286150} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb52bc86-fbbc-58f3-a753-d854efd39ec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7aebb99a-4bdf-525d-8f3e-1c1f2acc8b2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-19082" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2})).$" :question/worked-solution "The formulae for the derivatives of $arctan$ and $\\operatorname{arcsec} $ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arctanx) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsec} x) & =\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formulae above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2}))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2}))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2}))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{|{x}^{2}|\\sqrt{({x}^{2})^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(\\operatorname{arcsec} ({x}^{2}))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{|{x}^{2}|\\sqrt{({x}^{2})^{2}-1}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{{x}^{4}-1}(1+{\\operatorname{arcsec} }^{2}({x}^{2}))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d6c7d7-cbc8-50f3-a344-45446b405f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284858} {:answer/id #uuid "2557b0d8-9f3c-5e2b-a795-8eef0be2c846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285117} {:answer/id #uuid "265b440a-0541-5605-93d6-05c026a9c562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285122} {:answer/id #uuid "7663eb58-65d7-5b9c-a07b-0641ecb1169d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285889} {:answer/id #uuid "eb874120-2ccd-5cec-a284-10e8ad025134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284858} :answer-field/id #uuid "2efe99a9-b212-56ab-a943-1bebc7a16391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "884748ff-1cf0-5818-a246-3daa4fae09f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1921" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={2}^{x}+\\operatorname{log}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({2}^{x}+\\operatorname{log}⁡x) \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10} \\\\  & ={2}^{x}\\text{ }\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "329a0351-2458-5dc2-afb3-fb83d076e70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sec}x+{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285251} {:answer/id #uuid "3d41ba8e-fd4a-5c5b-8e2c-3eb30e482c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285366} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2ff268-76d1-583f-9abe-62b917b9c7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{csc}^{2}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285621} {:answer/id #uuid "93783351-25ff-5c87-8ae9-958c98c327e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}x+\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286129} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7d1570-7a65-5731-b765-bf52675c003d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286945} :answer-field/id #uuid "a44b9e2e-ea9e-5094-bc4f-bc781e7df5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a541e9ea-7fd8-5568-9e5c-f9ef03f5eaf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1931" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}tan⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}tan⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (tan⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(tan⁡x)+({e}^{x})({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733a21b6-530d-58ad-a226-6cac63423ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{ln}10}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{log}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285849} {:answer/id #uuid "b59cbc6b-900a-5727-b8b9-ede6d31b43fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{x}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{log}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286442} {:answer/id #uuid "b7de04db-df2e-5b86-833c-791e2ab5c775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{log}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286464} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d4f500-323e-5311-9fac-df967ad59f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{log}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286604} {:answer/id #uuid "df108f3b-d4a9-5d27-b27b-ac44d2256aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{x}-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286604} :answer-field/id #uuid "87489006-c764-5370-a687-c0a258ab9b3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d56c895-83f0-5d1c-bce1-e5e5d8df4d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-1934" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=cos⁡xlog⁡x,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=cos⁡x$ and $v(x)=log⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(cos⁡x)}^{′}log⁡x+cos⁡x{(log⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =(-sin⁡x)(log⁡x)+(cos⁡x)\\left(\\frac{1}{xln⁡10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{cos⁡x}{xln⁡10}-sin⁡xlog⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d4843e-1503-5b14-9ef4-6d9bd866b768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284994} {:answer/id #uuid "5f513476-ecb1-5481-9bb0-8cd729eba6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285660} {:answer/id #uuid "c159be5b-9ba9-50ec-90b5-6beba2ba516f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286558} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb10ca-14c4-5f21-a345-f92c3250e22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a909e4-a442-5f93-bb1c-8af5b5eeb86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5759735-03db-58c8-afc1-42a56314fd9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8eefdd68-c49a-5b6b-9578-f46e438f3aac" :question/math-academy-id "q-2038" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =sec⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  & =-\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-(sec⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =-sec⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0439511f-6bcb-5df1-b107-723ed7eac55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284822} {:answer/id #uuid "11540c92-a30f-5e29-852e-f44110b49f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284939} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd6e6c8-c066-57ed-aca5-d04d9fd31bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285371} {:answer/id #uuid "766a4044-6e95-5e6f-af50-99ef14df0af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285890} {:answer/id #uuid "d33d668e-b0ca-57c8-867a-44e3e4914dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284822} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7cd0e3-04ea-5f3a-b65d-50397f5fe917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dfd9b617-c165-5285-b91a-34485ed418b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20907" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1),\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1))=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "03286992-4e5f-59a4-a2d5-770d425c81b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288052} {:answer/id #uuid "08139daf-29b8-522c-86f5-f4b319f2f128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}sin⁡x-2cos⁡x}{10{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288095} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac8f83a-3129-5a17-9f82-1d0be2333401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288113} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff4a424-0cef-5827-8c65-342739e67db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{12}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288148} {:answer/id #uuid "1008f91c-5312-57ff-b584-94eee41fe74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/valu