7592186439046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebcf390-41da-5232-afcf-76ca3cd35d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438036} {:answer/id #uuid "c855dbc6-62a7-535d-acf4-3e0422823e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438036} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9ad80d7-8062-5402-b3db-57b743c9edc8" :answer-field/key "The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b955e63-485d-5f8d-acaf-14b8077b8a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438038} {:answer/id #uuid "2efbdf03-740a-56f5-983f-e118131d7d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438039} :answer-field/id #uuid "97cebb1e-e8e5-51e9-a969-4786401b7b78" :answer-field/key "$tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4be11add-d875-5219-b83f-33d16bf37abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-28675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+\\pi )=tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi $” is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$ is true. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $$\n\\displaystyle tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=tan⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=tan⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be00b005-d8ae-5556-989d-9072cc71e838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438100} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbcac9c-60a0-50a4-942c-941011f376c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438101}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438100} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c87592-feba-5843-b53f-3dd7cba9448b" :answer-field/key "The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6abcd6a9-7b5e-5d6b-9996-2c86b49036f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438102} {:answer/id #uuid "8b000f1f-8a8f-5516-94cf-8fdecbb9fac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438103} :answer-field/id #uuid "988a8e84-3487-5c91-b92c-117dfb072fd4" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eae80eb8-ecc1-50d6-bf8d-03a87f7208d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438104} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1d5a9d-8037-593f-a297-14c9b5d70607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438105}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438104} :answer-field/id #uuid "22795c00-f02a-5d3a-8c66-e44547341575" :answer-field/key "The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" :question/math-academy-id "q-29608" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$” is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$ is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement “The range of the function is $[-1,1].$” is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155da407-c8ba-5fa2-9127-413d6bff8d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3ln⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295391} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca76ff5-0227-56f5-94be-d80b462a5d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295528} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5899f6-d895-5413-bfee-0587b9cf1149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295802} {:answer/id #uuid "705c2710-b928-5586-a10e-67ac7d7acb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "bd595728-5d66-5fce-842a-829a9bf9e495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-3}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6439f4c-9689-5960-aa2c-ca05e0a5ed1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3f7435c-4593-5eb7-979c-b6475dc0ac4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-29831" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-3$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9f3ccb-57a5-54e7-9651-cd8e38e071bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295330} {:answer/id #uuid "137963fa-e7bf-5b99-b97d-16a6e2b83b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295376} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6cacd1-8561-54df-98e0-e7424109595d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296168} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e2f64-0c17-5dfe-a27c-81e2452e3e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c450e82f-bb3f-51ae-bbf9-78580de51634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eec65b66-7ead-5d6e-b179-5aacd453b9d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29834" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00628492-eb93-5878-979e-30036d304edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{10/3}-\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295270} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5a70b3-ab83-532b-aa08-1862b689d752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{7/3}-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295352} {:answer/id #uuid "37558070-eb2f-5f46-8242-8655de914964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}{x}^{2/3}-ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295579} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f41b7f-152b-52ff-9cf7-213f37faf4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{1/3}-\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296236} {:answer/id #uuid "d19e531c-0ef6-56ff-a397-6ea185e85664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/3}-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295270} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c825a2-113a-5331-8971-e3d904594718" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c693357-ab6d-5b31-ab83-a3f2da57f6b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-29886" :question/problem "Find the integral of $2{x}^{7/3}-\\frac{1}{3x}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{7/3}-\\frac{1}{3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{7/3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7/3+1}}{(\\frac{7}{3}+1)}-\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{10/3}-\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176c0cc3-ba47-5a28-bd33-bd9278429e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295407} {:answer/id #uuid "2f103c39-3c15-50c6-8f4f-b6fcedebd3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{2}+5ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295537} {:answer/id #uuid "58a0d4bf-d3f2-5150-8b06-787b98cd79e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295780} {:answer/id #uuid "73445bd7-7a1a-5461-a8e3-361ce1af59fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+2ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295898} {:answer/id #uuid "970ce0c0-c57d-5784-8a2c-ff97639ff009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295407} :answer-field/id #uuid "abead380-5729-56aa-a799-0bc9297616ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a09c1ef5-54ac-5fb8-be82-1b666b5326c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-29888" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{3}{{x}^{-1}}+3{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{3}{{x}^{-1}}+3{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abba1e8-07a5-5982-8931-920e2b54ed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295324} {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} {:answer/id #uuid "218a6eff-a0a2-5775-878d-fb334d854a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295454} {:answer/id #uuid "5203ee4a-c762-565e-a1e7-1c414c5d786d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295738} {:answer/id #uuid "e96a5e23-083b-5d3f-bddc-50fd9a0aed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} :answer-field/id #uuid "65380cf5-c8c6-595a-9584-e99d721414af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77e80b12-44f5-5ee6-b586-d6d7898a0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-29915" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the exponents and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2-3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862529b9-fa10-5663-a389-1d4abfe49254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295987} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a0028d4-4665-560f-bf42-ddeb7f0a35f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fbc60ab-10b2-5a55-952e-88966a85c0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-301609" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First note that $ln⁡(0.9)<0.$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{4}$ looks like an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $ln⁡(0.9)$ causes $y=ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=-\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{4})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca28647c-48bc-5c48-9036-4d369fe85f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9415b214-7670-5ef9-82ed-74e5c40b1c9e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "34a796fc-8b16-58cc-88b1-3c76c0415be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-302827" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4675341d-47de-58d1-9169-e4ea300f6534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295666} {:answer/id #uuid "49060b70-8ca0-5560-a25f-c33fdf13abe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295684} {:answer/id #uuid "73e82961-671e-5f43-927c-85b68cebb79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295902} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc5e39c-b266-5cff-bbd7-b9a7c961e739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296018} {:answer/id #uuid "902705ff-4eac-5fc0-8d8a-b9ebd4cdfeff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295666} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5828d6-8af8-5472-82db-0c5fb62611d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7ade3ef7-2470-5279-802e-68d9ba79206f" :question/math-academy-id "q-3038" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(1/x)}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40b42a09-c84f-5e27-aa71-22ffa00a131a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295637} {:answer/id #uuid "46d34dd6-7ce6-543f-bb84-942ca79da9e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{35}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295670} {:answer/id #uuid "848d0e2f-034e-5cdc-9b3a-9b15d6fe23a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295977} {:answer/id #uuid "91dee78c-9f40-52e5-9788-520d2cd18f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{15}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296051} {:answer/id #uuid "cc26a454-48c4-53fb-bb19-66ee3b5f4493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296337}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296337} :answer-field/id #uuid "18b12910-219b-5da8-99e4-afd60c6f045c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd3c17c9-23ec-5ace-941a-93cdddad613f" :question/math-academy-id "q-30578" :question/problem "$-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275b6be7-476f-5664-8d4c-fb7a3832606d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295483} {:answer/id #uuid "84433299-4f1f-5551-8f4d-432c1612da82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295975} {:answer/id #uuid "93487fcb-1176-5aaf-b67c-c9275110a880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296055} {:answer/id #uuid "a49b7592-7b1d-5172-a1d6-a14ce8a1fe9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134} {:answer/id #uuid "bbae1e1c-4c20-53de-80d8-7f1ca34b8c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134} :answer-field/id #uuid "021cad1d-dfec-5c2f-a8de-ca1eb6ba39db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "90ca11f6-8893-5beb-98a2-24781da24a2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-30588" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87066e75-093e-5691-a45a-e1a86f0a53fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295992} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc24bef9-c819-59a0-b467-817f630d6de4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a09e33e6-12c2-544c-8fa4-02ef6f3be0de" :question/math-academy-id "q-308849" :question/problem "Given that $y=8ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8ln⁡x) \\\\  & =8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cae6982-03b7-597e-93e4-369fa68c8f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296102} :answer-field/id #uuid "e26d719b-6dbc-535a-8df8-4e273868c85f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "395be2fc-ff05-5c2f-8d61-c5ab0d5aca86" :question/math-academy-id "q-308854" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=3{x}^{e}+ln⁡x,$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{e}+ln⁡x) \\\\  & =3e{x}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(3e{x}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =3e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =3e+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a21d7c1-657b-5c7c-bc8d-a5a78f0a5e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}3}-4+5e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295600} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fc67ac-4879-57d5-8bd2-d439a30262bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8688ee43-a87d-5757-aeae-1697946d83af" :question/math-academy-id "q-308866" :question/problem "Given the function  $y=2{log}_{3}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{e}^{x},$ find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{log}_{3}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{x}+5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{4}{1}+5{e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-4+5e\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eda8d71-b593-5d95-86c9-5bba5f41ecdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12x+6sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295355} {:answer/id #uuid "9cdb325f-ae0b-5a49-b609-cab3e9c25d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x+6sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296105} {:answer/id #uuid "a190184d-f11f-593d-b408-9b37e070ca95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12x-6sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296127} {:answer/id #uuid "d3db1a2e-d54f-5696-906b-c48583e1acb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12x-sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296382} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4b1b52-780c-57c9-9707-2dacfce41710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12x+sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295355} :answer-field/id #uuid "44751772-2752-5c4b-a6b7-c43cb14bd23b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8745f56e-112e-51d0-a4cd-8f6c067107b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-30991" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=6({x}^{2}-cos⁡x)+5.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6({x}^{2}-cos⁡x)+5) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2x+6sin⁡x+0 \\\\  & =12x+6sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd3f823c-979f-5d9f-9a75-a1d72b9ea787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296615} :answer-field/id #uuid "2796bd13-a599-55af-9fb2-de539c4e21ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f22eb9bd-4b64-597a-920f-3c1311c73e8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-310144" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(5{x}^{2}+4)$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=5{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & ={\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(1)}{5({1}^{2})+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e1e123-6d5e-55d1-b3f7-259ea04d1490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295570} {:answer/id #uuid "ab645299-d152-479a-9de7-e6107001c2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\ln4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673877}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295570} :answer-field/id #uuid "5daac6b9-af4b-5226-9dc7-fcd1d59fa479" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9148bcf-019e-5ab0-a8b9-eb415bd0a6d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-310145" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={log}_{4}⁡(6x-2)+7$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}⁡(u)+7,\\,u=6x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}{|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3(1)-1)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab4c8814-91db-509e-be0f-19786b9a5b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296165} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a3e276-476f-5c15-b0cf-fc02a349ee68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c7331908-3e29-5baf-bb7e-ba78549a5c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-310149" :question/problem "Given that $y=log⁡({x}^{5}-2),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=log⁡(u),\\,u={x}^{5}-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)ln⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c11aa0cd-50a9-55f9-b43c-ec6085afea6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}(3{x}^{4}-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296287} {:answer/id #uuid "b2066b38-d1dc-4961-8caa-0b613f496873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12x^3\\sec^2\\left(3x^4-5\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186676045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296287} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d54636-77f2-54cb-a399-2372401bb451" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a87f80fe-d2d2-50e5-bf92-8b685bd67b58" :question/math-academy-id "q-310179" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3{x}^{4}-5)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as a composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡u,\\,u=3{x}^{4}-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={sec}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot 12{x}^{3} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{sec}^{2}⁡(3{x}^{4}-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaa9039f-813c-5a39-8485-506d974ba6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{csc}^{2}(2{x}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296163} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b02564-c009-551f-ac97-3090c5c01752" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a2ac9db-9f05-59f3-8c0a-71e8b95b390c" :question/math-academy-id "q-310222" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2{x}^{3}),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot 6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{csc}^{2}⁡(2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f743bf76-aa68-58e9-907f-2483aa71ceaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296581} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b334c0-adcd-5c15-b3b3-c3ba23b7d937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "96fff31f-332c-5572-b655-bad437de2f44" :question/math-academy-id "q-312462" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{{x}^{3}+1},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,{5}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={5}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{u} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38bd8b8c-b4c6-5ce9-a1d8-1966ee4c81bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{ln⁡x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295587} {:answer/id #uuid "588dfff7-5cbe-5038-b14d-3d8e751e698d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295779} {:answer/id #uuid "986402eb-7839-5d62-b792-c55aac95a275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296083} {:answer/id #uuid "9902409f-61ad-5f6d-bef9-23a2badd42e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{2x}}{ln⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296087} {:answer/id #uuid "ba798157-3c82-5684-a8b2-fe1f9b64430c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296083} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c70d75-1a42-5da1-9557-cc4e7e234b2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a909a720-f9c7-52e5-a8a4-abd3492e6b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31249" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constant $3.$ Then, we integrate the exponential function and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddda862-700e-5259-8a9b-324825fe885d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295436} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaae1a6-c031-5bc5-a104-23401003f961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295691} {:answer/id #uuid "531024e5-c103-583c-92eb-e4200ac7a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-\\frac{{3}^{2x}}{ln⁡x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295744} {:answer/id #uuid "56637843-7c20-5243-80d5-7c1405ee9de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} :answer-field/id #uuid "32505d16-6c49-5e59-9ed0-fdcb1b385a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42f6b48b-f5fd-5245-b823-ea76a025e943" :question/math-academy-id "q-31252" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d14480-6ea8-53a7-8d9d-e4372b3c13d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡|x|}-{e}^{2x}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295687} {:answer/id #uuid "4b209e11-101c-567a-969b-9f779fc2d1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{x}+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295694} {:answer/id #uuid "a3afa67c-3743-529b-80a1-f481ffdb27cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{3x}}{ln⁡x}+2{e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296132} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d0920-ff23-58e4-bde5-060a3ca311cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{ln⁡5}+2{e}^{x}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296224} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd1d280-28cc-5226-a0a1-cc64b4710f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-2{e}^{x}+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296612} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2edd6af-13fe-51fe-9021-b17c8fe0c1c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73bc3d8b-49e8-5e01-8096-212f791e5c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-31254" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-2{e}^{x}+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "585614dd-9ea9-5784-93b4-6914d0f259e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295776} {:answer/id #uuid "81771957-0036-5c33-bdc4-df04751e4fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295963} {:answer/id #uuid "8598c747-3949-5f9e-8cb4-3d148fb0cdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}+\\frac{x}{ln⁡4}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295982} {:answer/id #uuid "aed107ef-e155-5c3a-86cd-e0c4ddc3690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡|x|}+4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296189} {:answer/id #uuid "b9377cb5-4f12-5d35-bfa2-ab4190b617d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{4x}}{ln⁡2}+2ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295776} :answer-field/id #uuid "33473edb-2246-524d-982e-ed6605f64e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8203576d-9480-5aa7-b8ae-908a8c1708f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31256" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eaae83-aa97-5d62-99ec-86500aa38887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{x}{ln⁡5}+{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295284} {:answer/id #uuid "19e3e8af-7108-5d2e-854f-5d3554910a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}+{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295420} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef143b8-e3a9-5d16-af8b-ddc53392af90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡|x|}-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296028} {:answer/id #uuid "b91478a3-d7d5-51a3-b0f5-b450152d8c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{5x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296238} {:answer/id #uuid "be5397f5-2ec4-53e9-a9c8-ea1bdd02f904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296271} :answer-field/id #uuid "561f5968-33ed-5046-bbef-83726bbb8b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9412645b-c765-5ef4-bf32-1eae5303c300" :question/math-academy-id "q-31260" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a9ec9-6bfa-51c3-b293-a2e6c7634c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295302} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8e6cf9-9f11-5e02-a08b-b0ce3fd9f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} {:answer/id #uuid "64d5cac3-e49f-55a4-a517-b40d5927cff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295841} {:answer/id #uuid "748fff53-6b97-5ddb-a2b7-7573ce8fffb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295904} {:answer/id #uuid "c09b0310-1b57-58cd-bbcf-bbf81a785509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295608} :answer-field/id #uuid "67494433-5316-509e-bbfc-ae0d8a99e4af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d5db746-ab9b-5b5a-bc25-31e21fc4632d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31338" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\pi -3x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is given by ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u=2\\pi -3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3{csc}^{2}⁡(2\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a923d0d-683f-5a22-8b67-4a4f6919866f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295424} {:answer/id #uuid "272999f6-d0d3-5ba0-9d45-afe5af8fefcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295481} {:answer/id #uuid "a23f01d4-b0cf-5d81-b3a4-2e37d3003baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x{csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296131} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4d398b-f413-539d-9058-6015c9708ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2(x+6)\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244} {:answer/id #uuid "e1aa6a79-375b-56fc-a7eb-84281d9d15fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295424} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cd72a85-2293-547b-8a73-3db56c2ab7b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c9a1630-1e07-5782-a0c4-5e97e3d68c4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31352" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ when $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡u,\\,u={v}^{2},\\,v=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-{csc}^{2}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=2v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot (2v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2(x+6){csc}^{2}⁡[(x+6)^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143d951a-1e7a-598c-ab01-6ac4eebb972d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} {:answer/id #uuid "80ac022d-2fa2-5312-b6bb-20edccc427c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{cot}^{3}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295959} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd53f52-57a0-58c2-84e5-51e7836dcaa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{2}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296358} {:answer/id #uuid "df819a99-e827-5e8d-8038-4bdc1f81e2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\operatorname{cot}⁡1{sec}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296451} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c37201-fff5-5a86-9be9-3c9d741d2eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18783dd-cacf-52c8-94df-17f7aaad8258" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "60550674-e8b4-5f20-9b57-5b71c82e6894" :question/math-academy-id "q-31361" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ when $f(x)={cot}^{4}⁡(5x-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{4},\\,u=\\operatorname{cot}⁡v,\\,v=5x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=4{u}^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-{csc}^{2}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4{u}^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡v)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x-1))^{3}\\cdot (-{csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5x-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(5(0)-1){csc}^{2}⁡(5(0)-1) \\\\  & =-20{cot}^{3}⁡(-1){csc}^{2}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-20(-{cot}^{3}⁡1){csc}^{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =20{cot}^{3}⁡1{csc}^{2}⁡1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f61aea39-f97c-5016-8587-a2f49bb65ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296575} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fc1501a-a5bd-5b32-9331-155d085aefef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1bab49d0-a1b6-58fc-8a02-9d9fd89dd0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-313723" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ if $g(x)=csc⁡x-2{x}^{3}+cos⁡x.$\n\n${g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-csc⁡x-2{x}^{3}+cos⁡x) \\\\  & =(-csc⁡xcot⁡x)-2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+(-sin⁡x) \\\\  & =-csc⁡xcot⁡x-6{x}^{2}-sin⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-csc\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)cot\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-6{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}-sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1-6\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{8}{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a00bd77-f937-55b8-9148-a5c1a2df5750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295597} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0e44e3-51df-57b7-9de3-73290c5703d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295690} {:answer/id #uuid "8b673669-26b9-55f4-aeb7-78ad83c0d37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296009} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b0c7ae-0328-590a-8a7c-83c5e5868a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296290} {:answer/id #uuid "e1684df1-979f-5851-a0c3-4dbc8f47887a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296290} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f353b4-b1a0-534f-8162-8c7bfcdf5cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0501bb6-8d0d-5290-9b3d-4f26ccd12ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-31394" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{5}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{5}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\cdot \\frac{{log}_{5}^{2}⁡x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f094e6ec-5f6f-5194-9357-eb5f114fc97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296547} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a06ad97-49ee-5366-b8f2-4bd5dbd655a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296744} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c475049-0b34-568a-8379-75689981b1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295611} :answer-field/id #uuid "58394d53-5ee7-54cb-8c01-d51839d9b8d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e8e1210d-36f7-5324-a441-3fe5324d9d56" :question/math-academy-id "q-313957" :question/problem "Let $B=\\begin{bmatrix}x-4 & 1 \\\\ 7 & x+2\\end{bmatrix}$\nbe a singular matrix. **In ascending order**, the values of $x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $B$ is singular if and only if $det(B)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =(x-4)\\cdot (x+2)-7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x-8-7 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-15 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $x$ are\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032db269-5170-5f9b-a293-a64c6b8de5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295278} {:answer/id #uuid "8c57cbd1-d8ae-5cb5-96bf-565f2293425f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296015} {:answer/id #uuid "a011a8fa-a5ba-5019-8866-0b7377968785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{3}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296116} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f2aee1-0c4b-5cfd-93d1-18aaa3aae927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{3}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296399} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc49c0-7e50-5c30-97a5-a53696b8ec4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296116} :answer-field/id #uuid "58373ceb-68ef-5d2e-ad2f-6c3319d69691" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46a1f16d-d0cd-5aab-85fe-3d2691d325ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-31397" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{3}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{3}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dae15a2-1ee3-5e1c-9740-88dbcb27308a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295346} {:answer/id #uuid "21c52b8f-99f3-501e-b48b-11fa940e47fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295455} {:answer/id #uuid "8339edfa-ac01-5a35-ba61-f72d13beb9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295971} {:answer/id #uuid "afde279f-b6c5-5153-a7ee-1486db54540c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}{ln}^{3}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296193} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9ca14-d6e0-59ec-bd95-95f8ca1bf1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295455} :answer-field/id #uuid "15bc6d3f-2f78-5fec-acf5-1f972668822a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7cf9e06a-68e3-56b4-9085-33ef355832a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-31398" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{ln}^{3}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdf01e2-d959-5d74-8b6f-e69d4a474616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{log}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295521} {:answer/id #uuid "455fefdc-f2d0-5c81-8eb5-1798b1ae907b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{3}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295661} {:answer/id #uuid "49e87fa3-26bd-5446-af1f-f1898bd93c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{log}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295688} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2f8f3e-a15c-51d9-8314-704645beda07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{2}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295880} {:answer/id #uuid "d510a940-8c9e-5a4f-9e72-4e2553f2cdf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296388} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ec13b3-2234-5cd3-af27-ef024afdf609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a480eb68-e1fa-542d-9387-098493118819" :question/math-academy-id "q-31399" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247f5c61-8dea-5c72-9001-ec5d7b70591c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295467} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9ec191-f4bb-5c86-8e6c-08aee654144a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{log}_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295697} {:answer/id #uuid "8c832d15-3e5e-5c99-9339-9f4b3d100902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x{log}_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296017} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4de440-cd8e-5ea6-9b03-b62c0fe90378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296031} {:answer/id #uuid "d47dd115-efcf-5c72-a9de-08221fb2d4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{log}_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295697} :answer-field/id #uuid "34099187-cafa-5bea-a9c2-38e91ea94b1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "baa80704-3dfa-5ff9-bf20-e404177dc892" :question/math-academy-id "q-31400" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{log}_{2}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26cf2f12-d9f3-59fd-be22-70990f0c064c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{3})|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295477} {:answer/id #uuid "32474011-c0eb-5c98-b028-863b8f17da19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295550} {:answer/id #uuid "8b40389e-34eb-5524-b04f-3adc8016457b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296008} {:answer/id #uuid "a5006fea-9435-5ebd-a15e-7ec4fb282356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296137} {:answer/id #uuid "e0893163-fbac-5102-8d54-6ed75f43ac2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296461} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59c5853-4a44-56f0-94bb-09e77fa03053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f906b7ab-49ff-57e2-8ffb-323870b5ae05" :question/math-academy-id "q-31402" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡(3x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{5}⁡(3x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f0c893-b41c-555f-9cd1-f30221f4928e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295378}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e664cc4f-0d1a-5655-9546-b6231e1608de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad42f537-44a3-537f-a759-f5a89512da15" :question/math-academy-id "q-314152" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(4+4x){e}^{2x}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+0){e}^{0}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+6}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de7f1af-415f-51eb-a89a-f5a9af6b6f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295349} {:answer/id #uuid "62bd78e1-18cd-5849-8517-09646421802e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295831} {:answer/id #uuid "a03305d5-7f4f-5550-a188-a6da402fb92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296117} {:answer/id #uuid "b9342552-3083-5d85-b29c-990c5f86b409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296239} {:answer/id #uuid "d429aef2-6c87-5948-8f26-e1c68c1e0cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296384}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296239} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5c283ac-dde2-5edf-8dbe-d0693455c2c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2561bb5-3392-587d-bd91-b80052a1b14b" :question/math-academy-id "q-31599" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0-{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2(0)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b020db61-bc7d-550d-af95-405cb768e4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296195} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ef4377-5327-5e85-be0e-3645dd1642f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "96fe6acf-39fc-5928-b3e7-6b1c83c18a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-316022" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5432ed129d85a3718f8e5ebe0cca141731c366d1b32e8e72d0916c60c34de2c.png)\n\nA region in the $x$-$y$ plane is bounded by the curve $y={sec}^{2}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}$, and the $x$-axis between $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, as shown above. \nFind the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}({sec}^{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)){|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{\\pi /3}{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53539640-bf8b-506e-bdc0-a98614bbaf75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295746} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2f62b30-0d7e-5226-bbfa-f9da2b0d309c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e8278e6-1f98-55c4-88ff-9d0d17e17bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-316024" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d282e4afd0a66dd79fecf474da9f618a5970197943044d24208f86c4c43781e6.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x{|}_{-\\sqrt{2}/2}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =-(arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-arcsin\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e03faf3-3e92-514d-a666-b56caff650f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295437} {:answer/id #uuid "ceae20b4-51bc-53f3-a56b-409b71e224ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516550} :answer-field/id #uuid "46fed9e6-b6f9-583b-951d-71398609b0ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a9a63b25-eb4e-543f-9169-7fae6ed27cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-316302" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)={cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $[0,\\pi ].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this integral using a substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{8}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\left(\\frac{{u}^{9}}{9}\\right)|}_{1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }[(-1)^{9}-{1}^{9}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2562a9c9-aca4-5aab-ae8c-dfc0d8930a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295470} {:answer/id #uuid "31d9ef31-5977-5a8f-86a4-f2af5b82793e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295548} {:answer/id #uuid "4915700f-dd8e-57ab-ac0f-4d614790089c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295685} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9da615-dcc8-547b-b139-e0fddd363070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295945} {:answer/id #uuid "9df1f497-a52a-514a-a098-e2493f3a2f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295548} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6f305a-1541-5b71-afe2-360d801bd991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f30c06d-366c-59ac-9244-d2bac8160fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-31648" :question/problem "What is the value of  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{3{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\sqrt{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{3{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})}{\\sqrt{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{log}_{2}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot (16-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e046d20a-588d-5b92-a657-b3ed4097c01b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296459} :answer-field/id #uuid "235cb913-9e0f-518e-a93e-a8ac9c3649b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b409383e-6fc2-599c-a9ff-f2d3f2066a9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316567" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|6\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 4| \\\\  & =|18-20| \\\\  & =|-2| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee63275b-2854-5ded-a179-79b5d7d5f1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296532} :answer-field/id #uuid "642ebee4-29eb-58ed-91c1-e48ef7bc9422" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c2b63b5-8e9a-53f8-91df-f96b487d39a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-316569" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|-10\\cdot 8-9\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|-80-9| \\\\  & =|-89| \\\\  & =89.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1951a6be-1532-5bf0-b953-a8a259d62706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295416} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd1c613-aa0f-579d-8531-78890bdba5ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74f25c82-a67d-56cf-b708-a7f7d4c8d54e" :question/math-academy-id "q-316570" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -10 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|6\\cdot 3-(-10)\\cdot (-5)| \\\\  & =|18-50| \\\\  & =|-32| \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79a2ffbc-e1ce-5bc2-85a5-d261eaf7d37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295932} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b6cff15-b6c8-52c0-b19b-c28ec078d77d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2111fc26-d996-5d1d-a930-d12f9563ae8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-316571" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|3\\cdot (-1)-10\\cdot 8| \\\\  & =|-3-80| \\\\  & =|-83| \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "861028d2-4e5b-5441-aba8-939308df93de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295986} :answer-field/id #uuid "273d93cc-fc11-532f-9fec-1702fee2c232" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6ed5603-df26-5609-9767-31ff4e7c4552" :question/math-academy-id "q-316573" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|-6\\cdot (-5)-1\\cdot (-4)| \\\\  & =|30-(-4)| \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c240b9cd-6090-54d9-afd2-6f7c1b4e5ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296293} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6f9b78-f98c-5255-8629-186eb26e64ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "96128bf4-884b-533c-a22d-9c6cd62a740d" :question/math-academy-id "q-316575" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|9\\cdot 1-(-4)\\cdot 2| \\\\  & =|9-(-8)| \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd6904fa-e9e3-51ba-8d1e-781883c4a882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296443} :answer-field/id #uuid "5253c083-4499-5117-80d0-d1160db69c0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6f58c85-198e-5060-8c53-9bc302a186d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-316578" :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\nThe area scale factor is\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "The area scale factor of a linear transformation $T$ with standard matrix $T$ is equal to $|det(T)|.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(T)| & =|8\\cdot 2-7\\cdot (-3)| \\\\  & =|16-(-21)| \\\\  & =37.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0b7ecc1-e9b8-5b8b-964c-e4cf9a4249ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296121} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36acde6-4a09-5ef4-9906-e204f2625e8b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c3acf041-eb7f-5e8e-b449-3675782ccc97" :question/math-academy-id "q-316580" :question/problem "![](images/49/4977d6636a96eceea64c266f688da7ec7576770ea1bcee9aa3d2878cc0a130d4.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\,2 \\\\ 3 & \\,1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=4\\cdot 3=12.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-1)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 3=-1-6=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-7\\,|\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2d38a2-bc8b-5a4a-9f10-ed0afb45fd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296014} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e155268-1285-533c-b160-41d564ed0853" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "228bc163-d98d-54b9-9894-e9af5abc4d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-316583" :question/problem "![](images/23/2318282d0f822fdec18bee5808a3d6be2d477cb106f124c32d4f0d589dac0978.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=3.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,5\\,|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b373104-387f-5698-b1c5-ff0306bfa57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295793} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbcd2ef4-faa2-5438-a1a2-ee9d6bdc8d72" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a42863c3-7761-5c90-b6bd-06f39444b587" :question/math-academy-id "q-316584" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f38424364e3d4efd8ebc416974163e3eb87b3350c0c6b8ec7f5ae4ea69339824.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,4\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a72305af-6015-5a67-9a53-61b172148c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296146} :answer-field/id #uuid "64af5f53-2d9a-5da9-820b-14a837fc2b01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5093505-1cef-57a3-9393-884c4f2f64a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-316585" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7edace8cab645caab8309768ede92f534281567640aba7082f647ca1c8a49074.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=3\\cdot 4=12.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,3\\,|\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8a61de-b029-5aa3-937c-791d1b86aea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295329} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca0aa565-7f4b-5b51-aaf6-6af3844a7165" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7ab3622f-c952-5ccd-8f01-9bc45bfd7795" :question/math-academy-id "q-316588" :question/problem "![](images/ce/cec4f46a70e55735e07cc77fb2013c8d5262ca15feda1ee9eac005a79d38147d.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,(4+2)\\cdot 2=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-1)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot 1=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,4\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bc53aad-7695-5e62-8036-c834e97608ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295797} :answer-field/id #uuid "27d28c37-d3a0-5b1c-a959-856c21fa3d69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57e5f03e-9b88-5788-8d99-3bc4b928e9fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-316591" :question/problem "![](images/45/455e0a08e2a0929a60d7fa1b76973e1b7a460efe8cc1cf01719ce211feacd8c0.png)\n\nConsider the shaded region $D$ shown above. What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=6.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-5\\,|\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 