tion, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "405bfef8-5ed1-579c-ba62-3f1f4f76e566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18-5v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297808} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e0aed29-b850-516d-8106-d11c0bc04f39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5c4eca7-04b2-5aae-8b1d-a3490669714a" :question/math-academy-id "q-319125" :question/problem "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle F=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & v & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{13}$ of the element ${f}_{13}.$\n\n${M}_{13}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${f}_{13},$ we remove the first row and third column: \n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & -2 \\\\ 3 & v & ∗ \\\\ 5 & 6 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${f}_{13}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{13} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}3 & v \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & v \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-v\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-5v\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8dd30aa-64cd-54e3-97ef-52657d5910da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14p-6q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298355}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298355} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a84fb1-1ce3-5386-a052-3aa3a78b8db9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dff6538b-44cc-5d72-9d22-4ad197bd0a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-319162" :question/problem "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}2p & q & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4p & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & 2q\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{23}$ of the element ${b}_{23}.$\n\n${M}_{23}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${b}_{23},$ we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2p & q & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${b}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{23} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2p & q \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2p & q \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2p)\\cdot 7-q\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =14p-6q.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc08b7e-07d8-5c20-b369-366b41c15409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297755} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c57bb7b-d738-590b-918f-bb2ca08dbb74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "77631f1f-549e-50af-8aff-2f23f2d4d3b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319164" :question/problem "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x & 3 \\\\ y & 5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2y & x\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{23}$ of the element ${p}_{23}.$\n\n${M}_{23}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${p}_{23},$ we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2y & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{23} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & x \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (2y)-x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df5685ad-5b2c-5c88-8e40-dc7264c6327b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e-3df" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298510} :answer-field/id #uuid "705a8ea4-eb27-51d7-983f-d4f5acc273af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5ce922b-4b06-539f-9202-8293bbf3e968" :question/math-academy-id "q-319175" :question/problem "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & e & f \\\\ 5 & 3d & 2 \\\\ 4 & 7 & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{31}$ of the element ${t}_{31}.$\n\n${M}_{31}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${t}_{31},$ we remove the third row and first column:\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & e & f \\\\ ∗ & 3d & 2 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${t}_{31}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{31} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}e & f \\\\ 3d & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ 3d & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =e\\cdot 2-f\\cdot (3d) \\\\  & =2e-3df.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "986dc63a-e742-5041-bb2d-4d5f51536a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k-3ij" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298213} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a0fcd13-ab7c-596a-9b62-151dcc74aee2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73507977-fd56-5a33-bedf-10842b1fff67" :question/math-academy-id "q-319176" :question/problem "For the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle V=\\begin{bmatrix}j & 1 & 3i \\\\ 2 & k & 5 \\\\ 4 & j & k\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind the minor ${M}_{21}$ of the element ${v}_{21}.$\n\n${M}_{21}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the minor of ${v}_{21},$ we remove the second row and first column:\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 & 3i \\\\ 2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & j & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${v}_{21}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{21} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3i \\\\ j & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3i \\\\ j & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(3i)\\cdot j \\\\  & =k-3ij.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bac31f3f-82dd-5744-9c52-62141c569e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+\\operatorname{sin}(x)){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298365} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d13588f-071e-5a52-a869-877b2c497c93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9a55990-04ba-5f4c-bd0b-a335f0aeb15a" :question/math-academy-id "q-319350" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)).$\n\n$({e}^{x}(2+sin⁡(x)))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=2+sin⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}sec⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2+sin⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2+sin⁡(x))+({e}^{x})(cos⁡(x)) \\\\  & =(2+sin⁡(x)){e}^{x}+{e}^{x}cos⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70915bcc-86df-54d2-a4fe-50e0b9f25633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298034}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298034} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c11172-6006-5675-abeb-a1bc58910b0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dca45644-472a-517e-a9dd-987f8f5907bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-319357" :question/problem "Differentiate $2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x).$\n\n$(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′}=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=2{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=cos⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}cos⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot cos⁡(x)+2{x}^{2}\\cdot (cos⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =(4x)(cos⁡(x))+(2{x}^{2})(-sin⁡(x)) \\\\  & =4xcos⁡(x)-2{x}^{2}sin⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4f2334d-4d19-5657-ae3e-4861480ed63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298594}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298594} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b9513e-697d-5d7b-bee1-be7a3d121ec8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbfc66b-fff8-5004-b6dd-86427e259c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297674} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d77a70-66b2-5ffc-82b6-2ce4bec2f8bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e59730-fbc2-5bbb-83ce-1f84517628d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298183} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9818ff9-bf1d-504a-a640-f403039467ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0876fd99-09ab-5402-abb3-c07e19b050b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-319360" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=-5.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-17$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$12$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-3$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & -12 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 12 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b1e8767-de2d-52c7-8e2b-c1d59dee9c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298230}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298230} :answer-field/id #uuid "083d9e98-ca8d-5676-958b-ffeedf627de1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1899ba9d-d3c7-55ce-9eb0-e8c2dc7a4673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297659} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3ce860-5116-5e9b-8117-529c03e68d73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298901} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5975fdb6-cb9e-539a-b015-6e5266540e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297926} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b9f6c6-8575-50de-8fb6-95b3ce4f2904" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "228d219f-a988-51e3-add6-8253de28fd6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-319370" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=-4.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$4$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$7$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46a2082-4701-5caa-8a18-53b0c4269a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298274} :answer-field/id #uuid "598ac1fb-ed3b-5180-8cb1-1204c40529bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298943} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ae1504-4970-5613-87dc-94b7c840f849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297990} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e4160be-bab1-5130-a5b3-baad952ec2d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd4225cd-a6f9-5811-a080-99edbbbc07c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298445} :answer-field/id #uuid "b02672dc-f7e5-58dc-a2a8-072fe03a302b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74394102-8729-5a30-8d7e-62d8f0117047" :question/math-academy-id "q-319372" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\ngiven that $det(A)=7.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{7}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-11$</td><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-4$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** We are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 11 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -11 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -10 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -11 & -10 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284d504d-e400-57ba-866e-f8dff66216fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297724} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb4d22f-ab7d-5aa3-8db2-cf3a5509f8ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9df2a3ce-ad57-5588-83d2-42b69ca3314d" :question/math-academy-id "q-319385" :question/problem "Differentiate $\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x).$\n\n$(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′}=\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x}tan⁡(x))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡(x)+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot (tan⁡(x))^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}(tan⁡(x))+(\\sqrt{x})({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{tan⁡(x)}{2\\sqrt{x}}+\\sqrt{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aca02af-1267-5495-8514-3c0b3c60eb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298229} :answer-field/id #uuid "334b73ec-67fc-5e0a-a799-7aa8a5b37cd6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298812} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43320049-f27b-50cd-97bc-171b22e5c4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297824} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f99d59-45ab-5371-902e-8352a898486d" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e895a78-e9aa-58ba-aab5-2fa17cb12e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297950} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9d8fad-aa30-5871-84b3-b6f919b19455" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49aed189-4a5b-58cc-bc59-cee854ce956e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297854} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ece9237-745d-5b97-a4df-453863812eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca1a8117-1d26-5acd-beac-beb6e4c1cfa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319395" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">${A}^{-1}=\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em} {{field-2}}$</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-3$</td><td>{{field-1}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$3$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$0$</td><td>{{field-4}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)(2-3)-0(\\,-2-(-3)\\,)+(-3+3) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6598caed-bd43-5485-82fc-6172d6afee81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2/11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297986} :answer-field/id #uuid "87b160ba-8853-58cf-97da-7d8ad0eb9b07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7e72b45-0695-5b36-9476-7542514f8aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-319409" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-3-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4ed675a-e374-5734-95e6-b38d1d46a8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298339} :answer-field/id #uuid "9144a26f-d207-5329-a4f2-7e4b73ff0242" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9cc9cf4-3e2c-5fa2-9cc3-9a41fb5db658" :question/math-academy-id "q-319413" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{12}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-2-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08bb5b15-8035-5a87-b994-7a63a290fd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5/13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297598} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8cea55-04b6-5a91-988e-863b5a98418a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8dd1f6d6-3609-5dae-86b5-57186f677ef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319416" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2-(-3))+2(4-0)+0(6-0) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}^{t}={c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1+4) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca3eaad9-1dc3-55cb-ab62-7139f24f233d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298432} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef19aba-acb5-5246-8048-25a4732b0153" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6120ca6-9e08-5dd7-8756-664c0b14c937" :question/math-academy-id "q-319420" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{31}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(2+1)-0\\,(0-1)+2\\,(-0-2) \\\\  & =3-0-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}^{t}={c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd6401f2-6da7-5c65-8aa1-add3b78f3859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298448} :answer-field/id #uuid "26876702-3fb0-5366-a888-d8bd68fa6992" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ccc1718-8889-5c4c-8f82-a3c2895dd7ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-319425" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}tan⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{(tan⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}ln⁡(4))tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}({sec}^{2}⁡(x)) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}ln⁡(4)tan⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a25d310-05ab-55e4-a1c5-609bfd85c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297780} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a79b5b0-3b96-5509-bde8-4a7499f7d418" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b74699a3-68fd-5996-962f-7d078766e370" :question/math-academy-id "q-319434" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $det(A)=1-2k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=1-2k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot k-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-2k)}\\cdot (-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3k & =2k-1 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6821836-cff5-597c-bfe8-284e273211b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298473} :answer-field/id #uuid "5334a997-fbd8-5492-a178-58a2c8133561" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4e42e14-a47f-5067-a3e2-ccca26a8935c" :question/math-academy-id "q-319437" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-1\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1+2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{k-1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k-1 & =-2 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19779c74-033c-527e-8527-75fcd9cd52be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9/2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297661} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a13f4b6-0d0b-5ddd-bc05-cb3e7f407353" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7cdf079-0dbd-550b-985e-14ed1ec133fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-319442" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=-2k-3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-2k-3\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1+2) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2k-3)}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 12 & =2k+3 \\\\ 2k & =9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9efe1417-3245-50a4-a08e-1226edd401f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298255} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2fd473d-2b44-5cd3-afbc-189ab115bc74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24df41a5-9c85-5f3d-b3b0-277ec8e095a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-319443" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-2\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(k-2)}\\cdot (k+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(k+2) & =-(k-2) \\\\ 2k+4 & =-k+2 \\\\ 3k & =-2 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b15d823-5299-5f11-8964-489306e38373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298156} :answer-field/id #uuid "df880d85-f2de-5046-a4ec-3c13075a93d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a5f26b04-c224-5e5b-b7ec-b6e734fe3cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319492" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-5+3\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/21/217fc7ba4c6a6c18d0be184a9d5cc7b0a682ed2bd0da6e284955040643bc2e03.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$\n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =0.540\\,419…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -0.540\\,419…≈2.60,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae38652c-2ec4-5294-bfa0-e1935f6c9b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298311} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb6c9cf-47c9-59b5-b61c-a18d9aef50e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19bf53c8-97d2-5dba-bc67-23661ef3ef49" :question/math-academy-id "q-319499" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-11+8\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/d3/d3b5558c0268843618c52c588ffcf1c8012189041b17d52fadd6fe74c3526700.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{8}{11} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{8}{11}\\right) \\\\  & =0.628\\text{ }796\\text{ }…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=\\pi -0.628\\text{ }796\\text{ }…≈2.51,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2c7cc1e-19a8-50e3-b731-ab68e5a297c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298529} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9d2765-a148-5704-bebb-2a5fc3a968ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11e7ecac-aef1-58c7-ae41-03384a6b83c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319507" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=-5+5\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/f4/f466a110eda3b07b50d988de6e46a541ad85d7ff64f43a6f056ea216752fa2d9.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{5} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc701588-10c4-5d4e-9543-08d3d2ae94c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298496} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d3b7e38-0b54-59dc-9f13-4a0765a858b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1e12c374-884a-5b4a-b66b-2ae8579d07d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-319512" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-6-5\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/93/93018ffa3e41f787cf32e4fa9735be8f5c6f9da360361434076cbfaecce51259.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)=0.694\\,738…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi +0.694\\,738…≈3.84,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d16c0be-59cf-52e9-8bdb-920c6cac59ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297615} :answer-field/id #uuid "df60367e-d76b-5d80-9d6f-56f689210ad0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a7750e6-1212-56c5-8da7-d5e6f9776240" :question/math-academy-id "q-319517" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-8-5\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/c5/c598c3b872cd2c4b3af08d8b2dbaf6a79512fe382473320feb526f31e5ece246.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <arg⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)=0.558\\,599…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=\\pi +0.558\\,599…≈3.70,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d8d7c10-dd12-5903-af0e-c687a3bccf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298024} :answer-field/id #uuid "c156e4b9-7c3e-5f5b-a864-47e9e4fe256f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "36d80563-8d54-5785-b9e6-542151d14e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-319519" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-11-6\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/ab/ab2f3a9137c05ca19507e44d2bb2ef4b369c762189019265d8fe3bad34822fe7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\pi <arg⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{6}{11}\\right)=0.499\\,346…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=\\pi +0.499\\,346…≈3.64,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67507fa1-9c8b-533b-b35f-b2b75970e742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297996} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f29c10-686e-51f4-8830-5217f4abdf63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b5cf762-3178-5e63-a1b0-8da0e65dccf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-319546" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $17.$\n\n${a}_{13}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30fc4049-b346-5ef3-b721-8b7b6804b08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297759} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e9901e-f892-512e-a362-56ac30375949" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "66b7eb82-0c6f-5154-a5bd-434c9571ba55" :question/math-academy-id "q-319549" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $3.$\n\n${a}_{21}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\,(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c573351-ee99-56f5-889f-894b79759197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298090} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e78f910-eafd-5dde-95e7-840e8bbf0359" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "959ebdb0-727b-5193-9194-4f73c8f717da" :question/math-academy-id "q-319550" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nif its determinant is $-22.$\n\n${a}_{33}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-22}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299779 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b99653d1-9906-5c8c-b3cf-ef75662558a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298360}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298360} :answer-field/id #uuid "1162fc23-32c9-529d-a242-487de940e14e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dae3d16d-fc3e-5ce1-9aad-53c052f42d7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-319562" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=8-11\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$arg⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/16/16408e6add22c687e3d2cf865908011602bfa87878fcbc3387c4ad3d22180e47.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡\\left(\\frac{11}{8}\\right)=0.942\\,000…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.942\\,000≈5.34,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ed74be-5bd1-5cfb-83ae-8da1c46abddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298461} {:answer/id #uuid "bf73cf84-e656-5869-bd56-89d900a17b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544495} :answer-field/id #uuid "664bc144-8adc-5276-97ef-e29bdce72ee9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5b42c0b-e569-59e6-b7d2-a632bdfada48" :question/math-academy-id "q-319581" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the **exact value** of the argument of $z=8\\sqrt{3}-8\\text{i}.$\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ \n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/78/78399e67e0ba69c1d7848a41aa5d4a5f8040fc578aab30a99f7e19ac86ece179.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{8}{8\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94fe4615-3627-5184-b596-faacf4579fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5b5855-e173-5cc0-be78-e8c7ed94613e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6dd1a867-f879-56d0-b4c0-d0297b5035d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319806" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y=(x+2)cos⁡x,$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+2$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-sin⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x+2)\\cdot (-sin⁡x)+cos⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-(x+2)sin⁡x+cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)sin⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+cos⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)(1)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a9ff769-7920-54d9-9d56-bc88c33849af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298084} :answer-field/id #uuid "4571a1bf-33ea-5e4c-ab47-61e0c018609f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6b71a24-90f7-5ca7-b521-491ff5b33ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319829" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by $y=(x-1)ln⁡(4x-3),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=ln⁡(4x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4x-3}+ln⁡(4x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4(1)-3}+ln⁡(4(1)-3) \\\\  & =0+ln⁡(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f879e289-265f-50e1-93a8-bc1f3228e612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298611} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a8d0a5-264a-526a-bf26-7c2ead18114a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dc09a39f-9bc3-5b8d-97b8-ec01ec4b162d" :question/math-academy-id "q-319859" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\,(0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot k)+(-1)\\,(0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =(2+1)-2\\,(-k)+(-1)\\,(-k) \\\\  & =3+2k+k \\\\  & =3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-(4-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3k+3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3k+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3}{3k+3} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3k-3 & =-6 \\\\ -3k & =-6+3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbeaf4d4-0e00-520e-99d0-3bee5b93deca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298438} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcfa3e0a-bb95-5124-94ff-faa0c2a5654f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7aba959b-00b2-5aea-a09a-80fa0c6d7e58" :question/math-academy-id "q-319937" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot k)+(0\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)+(0\\cdot k-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2(k-2)+3-3 \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2k-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =-(k-2) \\\\ k-2 & =-1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f85809-939f-5696-b20f-6a24d1b3a8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297553}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297553} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c533b51-00bb-5918-948d-af70d14f79e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "44f3f3e2-3340-514f-8bf3-b71afd4ac18e" :question/math-academy-id "q-319944" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nequals $-2.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)((-1)(-1)-(-2)(-2))+2((-2)(-1)-(-2)k)+(-2)((-2)(-2)-(-1)k) \\\\  & =(-2)(1-4)+2(2+2k)+(-2)(4+k) \\\\  & =6+4+4k-8-2k \\\\  & =2k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-((-2)(-2)-(-2)k) \\\\  & =-(4+2k) \\\\  & =-2k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k-4}{2k+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k+2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =2 \\\\ k+2 & =2k+2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73700a2b-b121-5954-9be4-09e3cf8c2a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298052} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31ff876-fad2-5cde-a7f5-e10303d7363a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "362d0901-58c5-537a-b4a8-7e15773992b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-320392" :question/problem "![](images/68/6881c7b52de83103dcfc483ec25455781a4d399653e2369d28f6924327f8ee56.png)\n\nDetermine the area $A$ of the parallelogram in the figure.\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨-3,2⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨1,3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9-2 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-11|=11\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b56b387-754b-5cf9-8829-0fe285e89c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297862} :answer-field/id #uuid "5159ce6f-4aaf-5edd-ac57-35a7fc9bcffc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2106b5f7-d155-5686-8911-c293b62f546c" :question/math-academy-id "q-320394" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f63cf622acc15681e3815118bbd2a187ea6039a548d4be1773634c0dc450c343.png)\n\nDetermine the area $A$ of the parallelogram in the figure.\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we get the vectors\n\n$$\n\\text{u}=⟨4,-1⟩,\\,\\text{v}=⟨-2,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFirst, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|-14|=14\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b23c197f-4fe3-5160-8515-6886564259fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298328} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e551ff-e04e-58bb-bb43-1994a42ad05f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ae47b66-7d53-5c4c-a6bb-e60ff971339c" :question/math-academy-id "q-320401" :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8-15 \\\\  & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-23\\,|=23\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc979caa-0729-52db-b9ad-f037fabe9650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298440} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0e666f-0f33-50fa-9b3a-68050ea5366f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b877ff9e-20e8-50e1-8344-e018774f6173" :question/math-academy-id "q-320402" :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =15-2 \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,13\\,|=13\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e43811bc-26a3-5920-9a63-18bb7725c3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298533} :answer-field/id #uuid "a611f036-0785-5438-b6af-2ed6eec3d8ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "586460f4-83f8-52e0-990d-cdd1c6e95132" :question/math-academy-id "q-320404" :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-35 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-33\\,|=33\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f964d77-5097-5d81-aafa-05414b56fb08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297803} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4f3b80-c19e-56ca-9011-75fd1a7f231d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e9f3e3d-c238-5325-aee8-71b571be0f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-320412" :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨2,7⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨-3,1⟩.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =2-(-21) \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,23\\,|=23\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9058e27-9604-5ce6-b952-b3e5d2273a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298483} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba8883a1-1995-5b39-99f7-87361a36a0f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7553ad76-d7b1-5eca-9463-9df9742eb383" :question/math-academy-id "q-320413" :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨-4,-1⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨6,2⟩.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8-(-6) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-2\\,|=2\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd440cb0-4f36-544e-b800-2b632f84913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298446} {:answer/id #uuid "d22bbe4f-92dc-4e04-8639-521df175c87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298446} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4386ef2-d375-5d83-9422-3a383082baa8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "216d9367-5418-5e10-afd0-533eb5320c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-320414" :question/problem "Determine the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $\\text{u}=⟨5,-3⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨-2,-4⟩.$\n\n$\\,\\,A=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-20-6 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA=|\\,-26\\,|=26\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "609f7b5f-824f-5147-9406-7af9613b62e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297963} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a79ff4-55ea-5211-91a9-33767972b15d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53380aeb-e3ec-5770-a193-a3eec01995c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-320550" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-5⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,6⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & a \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6-a\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =9+5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =-9 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75dc16f3-b24c-59e8-aac9-c1ea4729e37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298066} :answer-field/id #uuid "70277a6d-54df-5d86-932e-e42ed6a7a735" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6597267d-a0da-5d5e-b28e-9dd4c1c2688b" :question/math-academy-id "q-320553" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨-\\frac{4}{3},7⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,-9⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & a \\\\ 7 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\cdot (-9)-a\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-7a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-7a & =0 \\\\ 7a & =12 \\\\ a & =\\frac{12}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e8c81fe-246e-57ca-a489-3e90a1b01ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298170} :answer-field/id #uuid "8766dc08-52b5-57f0-a29d-8b40f0a1ad6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e91baa4-e2fe-58ac-ba3a-3b023f074435" :question/math-academy-id "q-320556" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨3\\sqrt{2},\\,-\\sqrt{5}⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨a,\\,10⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 10-a\\cdot (-\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\sqrt{2}+a\\sqrt{5} & =0 \\\\ a\\sqrt{5} & =-30\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{30\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ a & =-6\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ba0de8-d856-579e-918d-924c542513ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298047} :answer-field/id #uuid "285ddb88-a48d-55de-a633-cf3747e9f898" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53138b4d-25df-5dbe-9072-3708ca850fe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-320557" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & a \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 6-a\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\sqrt{2}-3a & =0 \\\\ -3a & =-12\\sqrt{2} \\\\ a & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "511bc076-6388-527d-9ef2-97562ff289bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297892} {:answer/id #uuid "499a268b-5793-4d27-95c0-d7745bd19f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297892} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1d6b1e-107a-52c6-beb0-de130a1ad00b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb3235d6-3da0-50ae-8be8-4a92d3030a44" :question/math-academy-id "q-320558" :question/problem "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\text{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & a \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-\\sqrt{7})(-10)-a\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{7}-5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\sqrt{7}-5a & =0 \\\\ 5a & =10\\sqrt{7} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e896be-b4a5-5d09-9cd8-a51857e21d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298190} :answer-field/id #uuid "53818104-8243-535a-a22d-6b989af29871" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "970e3fe6-140a-52ff-acfd-c8d9428562eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-320642" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $arccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\ncos\\,({120}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)={120}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e23098a-292c-5e41-933d-2c943df2b8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298025} :answer-field/id #uuid "a788ab8f-102d-593c-bb34-5731d47144fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299203} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d61aa4c-2794-518e-91b2-d0c09236624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297678} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5f2fd0-1b42-593e-8d73-e13d7f5a09c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de2328b7-9e15-5845-9b2b-25744372387c" :question/math-academy-id "q-320696" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $2\\sqrt{3}\\,y=x$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "810220e4-07c3-5e10-b5f5-e433e8878f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298108} :answer-field/id #uuid "78358a79-a060-52c1-9b42-5591d919fe6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aeb26d3-55ae-5c32-90f9-630a21076245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297858} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1840a4b-1116-5f0e-8231-ee35f8206068" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4cb0fec8-288b-5688-a0ea-decdd504c292" :question/math-academy-id "q-320705" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=0$ are\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,\\theta =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =arctan\\,(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60373466-004c-5543-b101-269d5fd3cb62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297957} :answer-field/id #uuid "b032bbbd-b845-5fd2-8a10-89f8e2008935" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01eea586-0fb0-58da-b8d1-c0f9849fa6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-320789" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\ntan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2303e32-8b26-55ec-9d0f-18e1bc3899cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}(3){log}_{3}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298462} :answer-field/id #uuid "792dd6e3-1db8-586c-a5f7-0da2170dac92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d825ca35-2305-5442-afb6-a99517062893" :question/math-academy-id "q-320814" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={3}^{x}{log}_{3}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={3}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{3}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({3}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{3}^{x}{({log}_{3}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))({log}_{3}⁡(x))+({3}^{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\right) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3){log}_{3}⁡(x)+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca46b1c5-ece7-5119-abe8-777dcc7ec3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298433} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa44b57-7202-5d5a-9c5f-cd88604e7d86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6161762d-7ca8-5487-95de-c569a1e07882" :question/math-academy-id "q-320815" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={4}^{x}{log}_{4}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={4}^{x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{4}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({4}^{x})}^{′}{log}_{4}⁡(x)+{4}^{x}{({log}_{4}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4))({log}_{4}⁡(x))+({4}^{x})\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\right) \\\\  & ={4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4){log}_{4}⁡(x)+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e34c232-975f-51cf-8ed5-ecf0f78eff8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{5}^{-x}\\operatorname{ln}(5){log}_{7}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297797} {:answer/id #uuid "8a591582-6e6f-4147-9d4d-ddb81a77153c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5^{-x}\\ln5\\left(\\log_7x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297797} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b389b0c-f96f-5746-b4d1-da5e3f5fb10c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42b535b3-90b5-5bc5-bbb3-36d4082e369c" :question/math-academy-id "q-320816" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{-x}{log}_{7}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={5}^{-x}$ and $v(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & ={({5}^{-x})}^{′}{log}_{7}⁡(x)+{5}^{-x}{({log}_{7}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5))({log}_{7}⁡(x))+({5}^{-x})\\left(\\frac{1}{xln⁡(7)}\\right) \\\\  & =-{5}^{-x}ln⁡(5){log}_{7}⁡(x)+\\frac{{5}^{-x}}{xln⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1552cb6c-5622-5577-bd40-d5ad4231f48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f50c87a8-d765-563d-bc7f-05ccab0605f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e55b33f-c2bb-5990-8ae0-14349507bd3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321100" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $arcsin\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\nsin\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8503fdd5-b8df-5855-aa50-f032f6d9c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298127} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d7ea95-40e1-5a4a-9535-40af137627ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d69d5404-b3a2-5817-ae30-04fc0f7ba2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-321120" :question/problem "Expressed in radians, $9\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9arcsin\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) & =9\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67871740-2511-5309-983f-d94c00dcb871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297997} :answer-field/id #uuid "580383a8-d490-5b5e-8329-f3a941754ed8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2641ea1e-dafb-594a-8a7a-dc35ec436fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-321269" :question/problem "Expressed in degrees, $arccos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)={45}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "4b36e600-edc9-5157-a498-f287a4b7cd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ {csc}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300349} {:answer/id #uuid "501e1d95-2bd1-5435-8f78-7596f1498f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300380} {:answer/id #uuid "5f88b745-9bdc-54be-b721-128fb29a0ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+5t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300450} {:answer/id #uuid "700d02f6-add0-58d1-aa57-1c870d9b599d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300525} {:answer/id #uuid "750059bf-1fbd-5d5f-8f3d-a88ccf5cd385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300542} {:answer/id #uuid "79e49c15-d4b5-5b2e-8001-7ad890632ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300563} {:answer/id #uuid "7f26f5e8-5953-5faf-b4b7-b250dfddfcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{sec}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300592} {:answer/id #uuid "9b21a7b8-d6aa-55b9-8ee9-dffc31ea9a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300743} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a79539-3151-5766-9535-8788b2c21919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300882} {:answer/id #uuid "c43980f9-e21c-54e7-b1c7-684a8cbfbaa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2}+10t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {6}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300943} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fb4f9a-38a9-5452-af60-00505647a38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{csc}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300944} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a32bb-ffbe-5322-a5e3-769c1f859d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301064} {:answer/id #uuid "e0edb6ed-e4a8-5781-a8c4-16e5568af265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301081} {:answer/id #uuid "fb442d37-181a-56e4-803e-7593e5f9e71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ec2eaa-2c8d-539c-8bdb-821acebf3120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300318} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec06feb-cc30-57c9-a079-e3a4432209d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301272} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5277ef3a-57c7-5beb-ad36-6af3b0fc2470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300391} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2eb0237-4ba0-5821-98a5-482d3f00c67a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55d3ab74-717a-5e4d-aa0b-4d52044b6f36" :question/math-academy-id "q-321345" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{x}_{1} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=60\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=240\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fdd1475-384a-5422-be05-881929c1958c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300276} :answer-field/id #uuid "0782e01c-2e6f-5a1c-9dad-dcf537586fc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57198438-ae58-5ced-853e-28d175022bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300416} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaa8678e-05da-542d-adae-2234c494f820" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2d054a95-51bc-5cb9-9dbe-815d03306268" :question/math-academy-id "q-321347" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={180}^{∘}+(-{30}^{∘})={150}^{∘}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}+(-{30}^{∘})={330}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=150\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=330\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e7c4336-a655-5ca4-b53b-787475eaf802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300684} :answer-field/id #uuid "14edbe52-e8d2-57ab-a851-83206759e38a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2263a534-e5d1-58ac-89a8-1772bdf38fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300126} :answer-field/id #uuid "a382cf1f-148d-56f6-bdf0-d3aef72ce48d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50d412ae-6892-55a2-8759-91c3ccf6ee84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300383} :answer-field/id #uuid "eafb6899-5687-5f22-ba3b-6313936f42d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a5dceea2-37ec-5d79-9349-768d5f27de4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-321385" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{9}{5+2\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (cos$\n$(${{field-2}}$)$\n$+\\,isin$\n$(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $5-2\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{9}{5+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(5-2\\text{i})}{(5+2\\text{i})(5-2\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{{5}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{{5}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{45-18\\text{i}}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{29}-\\frac{18}{29}\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\frac{45}{29}-\\frac{18}{29}\\,i,$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{45}{29}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{18}{29}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2025+324}{{29}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2349}}{29} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\frac{45}{29}$ and $y=-\\frac{18}{29},$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{|y|}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{18}{45}\\right) \\\\  & ≈5.90\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{9\\sqrt{29}}{29}\\cdot \\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.90)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.90)).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be1a65c2-eca2-5b45-8663-ee7f50a9be04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300911} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bdfcd38-1e9d-57ca-9699-842624f624bf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301670} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92ccb25b-4703-517c-bdc3-21a06e7f11e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300698} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd53698-094d-5ef2-b9ca-9b0bcdb7a218" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "328224d2-452f-5f0c-ba2d-99488ec4490a" :question/math-academy-id "q-321613" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)=0.982\\,793…≈1.0\n$$\n\nSince $1.0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +0.982\\,793…≈4.1.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.0$ and $x=4.1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3305af8-bdd6-5e9a-896e-c45e93186eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "189" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300786} :answer-field/id #uuid "28514f5b-8ffb-523d-b746-691f965f37a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301354} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1acaff9-8720-5626-8966-d0cfd0f42b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301007} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97bc96b-a6d2-5453-9c58-63570bef11c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a823b8d4-ba47-5b33-ab00-0143319290ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-321622" :question/problem "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Round your answers to **the nearest integer**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)=9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{9}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${9}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={9}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+9.4623{…}^{∘}≈{189}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are $x={9}^{∘}$ and $x={189}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fcb247-f440-55a2-9bf7-cc1d06887442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300039}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300039} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e77d3c5-f86d-5bff-8127-98400c1e9a4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "521c20c6-8032-5c25-9cc6-c7967c4bc8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-321877" :question/problem "![](images/36/369954f7abfabce27d112287ed232ce9a3a7e75ce993bba6300203777442f8fc.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(2,2),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The angles that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\theta =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the ray lies in the first quadrant, its polar equation is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db87a9e9-814b-5244-b7e1-633df8f1840c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301049} :answer-field/id #uuid "3416650a-306d-58b1-a5f0-d1d046fd2a8a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cede9d-f74e-5efd-a821-e817e6ba88d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300474} :answer-field/id #uuid "382ee829-97fd-5ba7-958a-3e2945045d4f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6f26b9c-d6be-5728-81f7-ec89b388ce38" :question/math-academy-id "q-321879" :question/problem "Find the **exact solutions in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b09d730-d6f8-5cf7-a8bf-f99e04015682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300086}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300086} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dbb153e-6b4d-545b-afde-8ca7a7eb19fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301266} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e6aa180-7dbf-516f-b362-4b746a2f18e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300266} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e86db1-e1dd-57f3-bff4-6c02780d1fdf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1036ad5f-680e-537b-8426-836a619b5091" :question/math-academy-id "q-321970" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e29d99fc6ae3a848bfe59ab9e77595ab9ca2a605d0d03139e8599adf15e6fd9.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=9$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9sin\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801e3126-6f97-55f1-ad47-e2afc002d4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300597}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300597} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1fbc73a-3d60-559a-9cc5-5a1225fc2fc9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23db9de0-9618-545f-95a4-aa33750b9019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300132} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f0bfc6-692d-528d-8a4b-122802a5cc69" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53021125-fcea-5da1-87cd-be2698ee5417" :question/math-academy-id "q-321972" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6aeb571c312e4dcc0f9ffc4fe2ad9b1a1db5cf02a2f757b66191e31337a8c498.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=6\\sqrt{2}$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,cos\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-6 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\,sin\\,\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6,6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48cb0e9c-99a8-5c76-94e7-1da9d41fc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300332}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300332} :answer-field/id #uuid "f657407c-1496-5c4b-848f-c06eeb41b835" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1cf4030-03f9-5725-9abc-7ed81f76c1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-322566" :question/problem "![](images/02/024a7e545d37295878d7f32efd393310dcf1c2c9f88f124ffc5fe46b05155e2c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9cc18f-21e8-50d6-8280-c21de7bc3150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300024} :answer-field/id #uuid "6406b312-e5c8-5e85-89eb-c0e6bc42b89b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381f0c99-1e40-5da7-9c4e-21f5ad6dd753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300233} :answer-field/id #uuid "a233f29f-b939-5649-84d3-849ec5c73dfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9a47e3d-9197-505f-a7e6-6061378afb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301194} :answer-field/id #uuid "d88b2241-c8fc-57e0-b788-b7e7a905dddc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2781cad1-5251-5d9e-95c8-df9b823a321e" :question/math-academy-id "q-322666" :question/problem "Assuming that $n$ is an integer, the general solution to the equation $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+bn,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+bn,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2}.$ Find the values of ${\\theta }_{1}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}$ in $[0,2\\pi ),$ and the value of $b.$\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4581840b-3d29-57b4-a333-d40d9add8ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300309} :answer-field/id #uuid "114978bb-e758-5eac-93fe-4c98a046b605" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301283} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fe5dcd-4ace-531c-a89e-536e66053313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{146}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300144} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f133b3-10c8-5782-81b0-f846d0f7d4f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8563b667-c254-556e-810f-72d65eb0b597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300629}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300629} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de03a0e-a212-5bfe-ad02-23b7393e3deb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ad8e92c-0ebd-5c39-8d19-d9dce92fcbc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-322670" :question/problem "Express $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** value of $r$ and round $\\theta $ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi )$ to **two decimal places**.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=5-11\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-11)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+121} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=5$ is positive and $y=-11$ is negative, $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-11}{5}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{11}{5}\\right) \\\\  & ≈5.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=5-11\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{146}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5.14)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5.14))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e6ba4e7-ec53-5571-b404-d7ae8fed7aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300370} :answer-field/id #uuid "021e74f2-cbf1-5b98-b2ba-3fa1e24972e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859e57c5-85b4-5628-998b-9a784a91ac0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300633}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300633} :answer-field/id #uuid "1daae901-9a53-5d34-b502-7c5af4fe8a96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab4ccfa-051a-593c-a805-70f1d216c94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300739} :answer-field/id #uuid "50afb390-5bc8-5b51-9edb-ccec40f173bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf8141f3-3d6b-54e7-81c4-c29188080b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-322671" :question/problem "Express $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$ Give the **exact** values of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ in **radians** from $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(${{field-2}}$)+\\,i\\operatorname{sin}⁡(${{field-3}}$))$" :question/worked-solution "In polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i},$ the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $ represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=\\sqrt{3}>0$ and $y=-1<0,$ $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z=\\sqrt{3}-\\text{i}$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "634badc8-2901-5d3c-8ed8-499bf0a2f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300465} :answer-field/id #uuid "a68d2433-e88a-574d-8b1e-5758f18355af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f9978e0-d89e-56b3-a4e1-ec22c1dfd203" :question/math-academy-id "q-322701" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e03611d72e1c88d1e49806ede117b6808186de27346e426104dca69391f29bbf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$\n\nThe **exact** value of the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ is \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ To get a value that's inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x+2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10034851-c3dc-522b-8e9f-79f761ae3ac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300025} {:answer/id #uuid "1c89099a-8f65-59d6-8b86-51ea17597482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300093} {:answer/id #uuid "242c2c26-7f42-5937-8408-83c0348f5952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300134} {:answer/id #uuid "ee280677-ec46-52c6-b8f5-07d9602a81a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301141} {:answer/id #uuid "f5863708-92db-5e15-8111-3db5d040aab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301171}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301141} :answer-field/id #uuid "0029851a-804f-51dd-856f-df69f962be7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "591da14b-a9b8-58b9-a31f-95d22b99b938" :question/math-academy-id "q-32280" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}^{″}(\\pi )$ by substituting $t=\\pi $ into the above:\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\pi )=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\ -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-(-1)-0 \\\\ -6\\cdot 1 \\\\ -4\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5c988df-c966-574b-8c5f-7b809d6dce28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301026} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b95b7dd-352e-5004-a281-30df20181226" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa163341-c16f-54fb-aae0-a325367cbfbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301196} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8b22ffa-8de5-5e10-8d6a-6d0e70b06c64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58b5a06d-6765-5f58-9d98-7e98880563e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-323833" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({15}^{∘})=2-\\sqrt{3}.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(2-\\sqrt{3})={15}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29d97e88-0dc9-5afe-801a-a2de2729b18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300160} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaf46a23-28c3-523c-99e4-a513349c1624" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab9cd3b-762f-57f0-ac96-ec5adcffc938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300569}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300569} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa8d22bc-70e4-56e5-9537-53845a8e82a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3e989feb-9d82-58b4-8c8e-96d5e37a6d7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-323836" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc8edab2-cd7d-5e38-8100-7c7ccd7e47d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301213} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a4600e7-f8b3-5877-a4ac-b3057884dd5c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb5ab85c-3ec5-56a3-8472-b2f147fb1a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300901}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300901} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a52b5bf-2e8b-504b-b32c-7f5fe280c1bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f1c2e80-bcc9-5d9e-9477-56e077c4e55e" :question/math-academy-id "q-323843" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+2}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({75}^{∘})=\\sqrt{3}+2.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}+2)={75}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={75}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "babc6a7b-b4f3-52a4-a4fd-0d786abd1b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "157.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300895}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300895} :answer-field/id #uuid "727522dd-c183-533b-867b-1493191e32cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301581} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b499cda-d4a8-5321-931e-f283670461ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300249} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d0b61c-2740-5e56-9b51-ea3e301d0a0b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac7bfba0-750c-59a2-819b-59513772943a" :question/math-academy-id "q-323844" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}$ in the form $x=\\theta +n\\cdot b$ where $\\theta \\in [0,{180}^{∘}),$ $n$ is an integer and $b$ is the period in degrees.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $tan\\,({157.5}^{∘})=1-\\sqrt{2}.$*\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}+\\,n\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1-\\sqrt{2})={157.5}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of ${180}^{∘}.$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={157.5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93011b54-b30d-5d46-8ff2-f6022adb9e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300700} :answer-field/id #uuid "683942f2-71ae-5f63-8332-5d60fac43fc5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301554} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "239eb865-fc3b-5cd7-9cf7-6f5da57603c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300131} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a1324c5-558d-5f6e-beeb-c47d31fdf323" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "852b299f-33ea-5d64-9868-fea936c065df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300627} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fdc424-7103-5e35-8737-019e0713b20b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58a0a3a9-3419-5548-9c90-737710ae4aa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-324056" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f3382db4b66df920c22c7050ea5e22c56892094c51d8889872b7bfcb541f684.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{2}.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$ \n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff58f07acb93044b6bca56ca30fe5f3b8447603ca9cb6d120559d287c435b987.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a74d48fa-fcb9-5f97-9dc4-abaa3ba03155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300804} :answer-field/id #uuid "2056d42e-c6d0-5386-8f5e-a91ca0f276a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301327} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97da6e3f-e645-544c-9cfb-00fc7af9453e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300725} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3c4870-f369-5d3e-9075-fa1b8c7239d4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301374} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93f3deb0-683d-5eba-acaa-7c512203acdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300706}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300706} :answer-field/id #uuid "87e0189c-b478-5a47-a80a-20755c7fd69f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ff70978-9a65-58ca-9d6e-6cfce9ac09ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-324057" :question/problem "![](images/43/435dd125850e3f97b5c3753a027f2db8718131ab499ef6711eaac637b1a022bd.png)\n\nThe graph shows a portion of the curve $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the line $y=1.$ Find expressions of the form\n\n$$\nx={\\theta }_{1}+n\\cdot b,\\,x={\\theta }_{2}+n\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ ${\\theta }_{1},{\\theta }_{2}\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ $b$ is the period in radians, and $n$ is an integer, that generate the $x$-values of all of their points of intersection.\n\n${\\theta }_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,{\\theta }_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,b=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "At the intersection points, the $y$-values are the same for both graphs. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $2$nd quadrant and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0738a8a505b89ff687cb4de350a00f2e10d6209fbeee7dcdfdf0ee359a9cc3c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${\\theta }_{1}<{\\theta }_{2},$ we have $$\n\\displaystyle {\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\text{ }{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\text{ }\\text{and}\\text{ }b=2\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c78ff14-c03c-56f1-be7f-58608c94e650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300361} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a229b7b-9184-53e5-8fa6-8630a0038588" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065a303f-5e11-53fc-a2f3-3bad07ac7eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299963} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a18d5f4-2049-5a72-9123-2c9febfd8716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ac6cfc5-8412-5ead-ae4f-e70cb354576c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324392" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{4}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)≈0.723\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈0.723.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b95f4a3d92d2214472f8c72f9322f6d1aa89a6e2c4f8cc2ae9f13015217a84d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.723 \\\\  & ≈5.560\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=5.6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "731758a4-7b51-54b2-9208-ce4122e6b908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "78.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300534}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300534} :answer-field/id #uuid "2349bb5b-a4de-53b5-8227-9a084342fdd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2de191-e974-5606-80cf-1ec2b7b1ba89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "281.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300759} :answer-field/id #uuid "391f6007-94a5-5a49-b561-e1a56585ccc9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c36479a-53a2-504d-8cbf-7d29626fa8b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-324396" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)≈{78.463}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/e0/e05c5b50dc8308d278cb6dac3408349da1865d37601003e0ee3025c937404239.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{360}^{∘}-{78.463}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{281.537}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=78.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}$\nand $x=281.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "667f8593-c404-5891-ad6f-768da4a13a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "115.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4418f07e-f4a6-5926-a0bd-b1fedf38313c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbf3194f-f80f-544e-bbb7-87d30eddad8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "244.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301051} :answer-field/id #uuid "e63dcfb3-c5b6-5d40-8637-84587fd9e56a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "442bcaa4-6366-559d-86b7-c0022ac3924b" :question/math-academy-id "q-324397" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2.3$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2.3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2.3}\\right)≈{115.771}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{180}^{∘}-{115.771}^{∘}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/97/974fad6534267df035b759df9064ed7dfaff80cb299c41744e8888f248ed1eee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}+{64.229}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{244.229}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=115.8\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=244.2\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493dc1d7-927f-58a0-b73a-a76e636a0f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300336} :answer-field/id #uuid "311c1232-bea5-56ca-8573-78b8c656d44e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301383} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079a6ad9-2432-5c6c-b93f-214d73ad8ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299968} :answer-field/id #uuid "94fd2765-f411-5827-8751-81c105ddfe0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac93d942-8a4e-5c8d-b866-cfb74d41e336" :question/math-academy-id "q-324401" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)≈2.062\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈\\pi -2.062≈1.080.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.080.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8020c19d17f3e79b825cb4e0cad4471e68ec0e2618a12578115a0fc8c014599.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi +1.080 \\\\  & ≈4.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.1$ and $x=4.2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b08128b-1f0b-5a20-a676-5e2f6c57e710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300248} :answer-field/id #uuid "257f288c-307b-5385-a7d9-8419607f71c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed5f4c45-3631-5885-bfe2-a19dc9739185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301134} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b8ccddc-3790-52b9-bbb6-e4975313ec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a7e25bbd-62e2-55a9-9433-daf79eb00d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-325107" :question/problem "Find the **exact** solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/17/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99ce7345-0881-54d7-963b-790e4b1b8812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300735} :answer-field/id #uuid "395ae32e-ffd1-57d7-a4ad-7c42940cf5a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e8d3f5-6a59-55cd-a429-968af392b6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300651} :answer-field/id #uuid "944f0584-ebf6-5588-9bbd-e29d582d93f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d3bce2a-acb5-5009-b608-6cf97a06fc87" :question/math-academy-id "q-325113" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $x={120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/29/29560b074afbb7d4e0cde3c1964e14696ef9879618e42c0b0c41f478e7ffcb72.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the third quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-120\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=120\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2ad50e2-704b-5f15-8f59-8903e66cedf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300780} :answer-field/id #uuid "0595c7f4-2b1b-55b0-8eaf-405f478bd32c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301240} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c11d39fd-7d64-5d17-8d0b-23bc18cb4c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300931} :answer-field/id #uuid "5243862f-5764-5836-b392-6aee0b0a590e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eab8e489-594d-52e1-8e07-a54c47bfe885" :question/math-academy-id "q-325125" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-45\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=45\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96c4ff95-8613-51d7-a9ef-620e6df2477a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300721} :answer-field/id #uuid "396970b5-d059-558a-a8ad-06f08f053d94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "467165d3-c0af-5b88-a79d-84681c3e417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300315} :answer-field/id #uuid "e202a75c-4286-5ff8-b2b1-fdcb40fd80d4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1bf30272-c0b4-5eee-b418-28d5533437be" :question/math-academy-id "q-325846" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,-\\sqrt{14}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a29cdfe-93c9-5408-97f1-ddcbaf3cf83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300497} :answer-field/id #uuid "3377c8bd-b97b-57ab-a29b-7a5c6e961b59" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114f964a-494a-5484-ae82-f1b20326c0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300031} :answer-field/id #uuid "e13b9e37-4b86-530f-96dd-7ca308418ec5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbcc472f-5ffe-5c12-b68c-6360fd3ce661" :question/math-academy-id "q-325854" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,+\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ef5a95-a797-5de3-9ab6-c201d321d2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300635} :answer-field/id #uuid "480b183c-d6ab-5353-ad3a-04c02fe2dc05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4df9edb0-31a2-5a49-8afc-8246406c7c24" :question/math-academy-id "q-325905" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant–tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{5}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{45}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{45} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab99ec7-771d-50f2-a834-3fa3d70595fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300662} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7732646-406c-5f9c-9ea1-c94b0f21d6c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "023ac44e-fe44-507e-8d12-2924e0e540c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-325931" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41440699-b75d-5944-ab74-520b4d06071a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300283} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3775ea-8e55-5352-b907-0d3e129f8913" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aec6c9ae-aedb-5300-bab0-b5fc016dbf5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-325932" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56160139-9600-54fe-9993-a2c0467b932f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300410}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300410} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cfb89a4-b356-5f40-87eb-069ff78d420b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b994176-5e05-5ad2-a948-d3ceb4ff536a" :question/math-academy-id "q-325936" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{8}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{8})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af5d0b1-479c-542f-902a-db1f028a7dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300573}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300573} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a07881-1c6d-58a3-acd2-9ca3cdedadb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14757807-d472-5c26-8560-91938e110338" :question/math-academy-id "q-325938" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f640f9ca-d4c1-5f61-9795-f5bc1c9b5551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301175} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb5f0076-7ab4-5eb2-a2f7-6c58f49742a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e652b1c-1300-5ad8-bf3a-d556d6af72e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-325940" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8af840-146c-516b-9b0d-db759208defc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301228} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c7fcbad-b1b6-5b61-a78d-5e045f7e2b30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5eeeae17-7ba0-5384-9314-34b43700e9fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-325941" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{10} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{100} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{100}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68579839-b946-5535-9f18-159ca2a0d5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300486} :answer-field/id #uuid "610e23b8-d11d-5ef6-8730-322a81cb18c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301539} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c61ec04-038a-51f0-9b16-a0ec69da59c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300177} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dbabfdb-ca09-5062-aa79-3848b6b197e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "74967a64-b648-5512-8822-bc2f06be078f" :question/math-academy-id "q-325991" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=$ | $=$ | $-\\sqrt{5}\\,-\\,$ {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{5}-4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895ad219-17a6-5bff-8059-11feef8a1657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300655}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300655} :answer-field/id #uuid "094faf34-e8e8-5bb3-b4fb-b094c9773440" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4705ab53-4430-532d-b491-423a1ff4313a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300321} :answer-field/id #uuid "407d99c3-8188-56b5-a90e-dddc6ae6ea95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4a1bfb70-9351-599e-8d42-359c4bb40742" :question/math-academy-id "q-325997" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{159}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $325$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{159}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{159}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe’re given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{7}{25}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{24}{25}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}}{325}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{10}}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}-24\\sqrt{10}}{325}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "503204c2-5202-5eb6-9c99-1d89a4256bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300382} :answer-field/id #uuid "775ffea0-6f09-507b-ba4a-f5929a9c5ece" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d2ae08-199c-5474-adf2-cfd379ea5346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300115} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fdb03f8-a8af-5dff-897a-1a48b028739f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301740} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8236cfbf-50c1-52a1-9051-6e2573a692b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300611} :answer-field/id #uuid "be04d349-7486-598b-9dcd-fc83d123cadf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cefa32c2-7378-5ecb-a3a0-9b87e829889e" :question/math-academy-id "q-326255" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right),\\,y=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right),\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (2)}{4}\\right)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{6}\\right)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $y=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(1)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (4)}{6}\\right)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cda53e7-8da8-5d86-a98c-5728719bc41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300000}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300000} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f273e-d8b0-5e4d-9631-40e611c24ed3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f796a4-f9bc-52c2-95c4-861c28462e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300143} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c15bfd-ddd4-56df-9248-59fef4cfb8ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011fae4e-c40e-5543-bf07-c0c1656584a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299934}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299934} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7a0f49-5d74-598d-9cc7-a44f9d26dfa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9040c3c-4511-5ef7-a719-36ef3ae85af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-326256" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\\,y=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right),\\,-1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(0),$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(0)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (0)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into $y=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(1)=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (1)}{3}\\right)=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (2)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642793a6-a34a-56ef-9ac0-66acfab64dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300469} :answer-field/id #uuid "758cf272-ca60-5740-8291-4894048c7c1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b1dc201-81d1-5231-9601-4ced8e8e4925" :question/math-academy-id "q-326363" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{8}{17}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{15}{17}\\right)\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{85}-\\frac{45}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07fdf48d-ac6b-5ec1-8d05-3fb9d5592051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299969} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd0e826-e817-5cd1-a6c9-b0bf566ad656" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "950be932-80bf-5689-a4f2-453b0e731ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300710} :answer-field/id #uuid "a284af3a-e3f6-539a-9766-5fafad5aeb16" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6128daa9-a33c-5d00-8017-59d8aba58347" :question/math-academy-id "q-326378" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{3}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $50$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{7}{25}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{24}{25}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{50}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7-24\\sqrt{3}}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "700d02f6-add0-58d1-aa57-1c870d9b599d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300525} {:answer/id #uuid "7f26f5e8-5953-5faf-b4b7-b250dfddfcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{sec}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300592} {:answer/id #uuid "9b21a7b8-d6aa-55b9-8ee9-dffc31ea9a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300743} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fb4f9a-38a9-5452-af60-00505647a38e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{csc}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300944} {:answer/id #uuid "fb442d37-181a-56e4-803e-7593e5f9e71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300743} :answer-field/id #uuid "14021c93-da11-5615-87f6-23a0deb901ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e2dac7d9-d918-58d0-a1d8-f6e51efc32ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-32639" :question/problem "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x-1)-1,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6y-1)-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6{csc}^{2}⁡(6y-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6y-1)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(6{g}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "501e1d95-2bd1-5435-8f78-7596f1498f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300380} {:answer/id #uuid "79e49c15-d4b5-5b2e-8001-7ad890632ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300563} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a79539-3151-5766-9535-8788b2c21919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300882} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a32bb-ffbe-5322-a5e3-769c1f859d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301064} {:answer/id #uuid "e0edb6ed-e4a8-5781-a8c4-16e5568af265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301081} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ff66be0-4535-5d25-ae7e-68cee249f71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "812a9224-9b4e-516e-80da-078aa909f4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-32643" :question/problem "If $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡({x}^{2}),$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={sin}^{3}⁡({x}^{2}),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}⁡({y}^{2})\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({sin}^{3}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =3{sin}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot 2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =6y{sin}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{6y{sin}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({y}^{2})}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡({[{f}^{-1}(x)]}^{2})}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9bdc1b2-8ea1-5127-89c0-6b997d1343aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300968} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c7c1d5-e064-5947-ad92-edb4b63aee4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fc8ff6e-fc33-5efd-ab02-f17a877d633b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327247" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)]+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))}^{2}=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bec7eef-98ab-50a1-a827-b84e71a0b5c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300251} {:answer/id #uuid "3f84bdbb-e043-5047-a36e-5da69a5c79e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300274} {:answer/id #uuid "48bd8464-99de-5369-8e7b-9fe677ea1700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300331} {:answer/id #uuid "a04b15ad-a1bc-5b8c-b98c-a273e45d97d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300771} {:answer/id #uuid "a93e42c1-d25f-5c74-aa8c-ac931bf2b4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300251} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf01f982-1095-5239-a807-cfb6858ba6e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f43456bf-f295-5e3b-a9b3-15b153a5e800" :question/math-academy-id "q-32727" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & ={s}_{11}{C}_{11}+{s}_{12}{C}_{12}+{s}_{13}{C}_{13}+{s}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+2\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2{C}_{12} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}2} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\left(1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-2((-5\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 2)-0+0) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91a691d2-3272-5830-972b-59bf8bd6c830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300692} :answer-field/id #uuid "159550db-f56d-528e-a19e-1726051bf4cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2008ce5d-336f-581b-8cfd-ae901047e3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-327669" :question/problem "Given that the expression $7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=7 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={7}^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =49+72 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =121 \\\\ R & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ab2b106-cfdc-5e07-a354-2fb8d1af9992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300568} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa8e92eb-4372-53e3-84ed-be78c8d378ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c8d389f-2866-55ff-a326-43d633b38963" :question/math-academy-id "q-327678" :question/problem "Given that the expression $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=9 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={9}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =81+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =90 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{90} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632d2e86-5773-5d7e-b4d1-e79da19a90c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300463} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e784eca-c415-5a6b-a42a-567c26c5934c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ae3053e1-ef66-54e3-a77c-5b0dc0738ff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-327702" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e45bcae-def6-5c8b-ab94-a55e6888a8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300368} :answer-field/id #uuid "40d133dc-db0f-5148-8ff2-6fbc2490785e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f53dbaee-2ae9-5ecf-8882-7a17ab88ebf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-327703" :question/problem "Given that the expression $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9cb646-12e0-5cc9-a5a0-d97db7f6720b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300979} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb945c6-18c8-5c3e-8121-d1b3da73260f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e82816cb-fa9a-5239-a243-6d4c247f2e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327714" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\ ϕ & =30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2506dc2c-9a48-5ddf-a190-045af9bb3db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300138} :answer-field/id #uuid "e51629cd-762c-5f50-ba6f-6feeb02fa23d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bcd20c00-207d-5776-ad99-e9ea0fea55dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-327798" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{y+5}{2\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bc28628-0500-5a4e-b7c4-aab08e1def7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300578} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b576c6-b6ea-547c-b701-c6162e1d6269" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cf7a561b-81b3-5465-9ed7-22d55b60ba48" :question/math-academy-id "q-327803" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter  $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{10} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12 \\\\ \\frac{y-12}{3\\sqrt{11}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ec0879-40ef-5eca-bcab-024dcd2d2ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300557}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300557} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7318c6-8514-5dcc-b694-eb9615ec63ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42002e7f-0b06-57fa-9bb9-6d321bc37cf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-327808" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab159e0e-467d-5f21-8fde-88f54619f27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300821} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d87d77-faf2-51e3-8007-0a1b765c8eff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c43bad9-0cfd-52b5-8797-68675d788468" :question/math-academy-id "q-327809" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{48} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ab42d7-89d3-5227-b9a9-2d4a46ac75dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300280} {:answer/id #uuid "611e2833-ce93-5c10-9e5b-40b95d3ca49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{72}+\\frac{y^2}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540693} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cdeb47f-480e-5ca4-a46f-7f342cc63ce7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9e4592b8-c042-55bc-b24f-4355e15f5f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327811" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abde6ff-968f-5c10-a57e-e9337ccb9853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299986} {:answer/id #uuid "0c0b0e8c-b5e8-5d1a-8a64-83154e74252f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299995} {:answer/id #uuid "1824105f-b810-5b30-a0aa-0beb57bd268f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300062} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3832ab-4326-5da4-9681-13c8424ca43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300518} {:answer/id #uuid "b97de0dc-d8bc-5f26-9307-169bdbb2d021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-432" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300890} :answer-field/id #uuid "54890664-57f3-5254-b119-6383b3902222" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8643e81c-8c77-5255-a120-2681f124c049" :question/math-academy-id "q-32791" :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & ={t}_{11}{C}_{11}+{t}_{12}{C}_{12}+{t}_{13}{C}_{13}+{t}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+4\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-{C}_{11}+4{C}_{13} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}-1} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & {\\color{lightgray}4} & {\\color{lightgray}∗} \\\\ 9 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}∗} & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\left(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+4\\left(9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =-0+4\\cdot 9(6\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-432\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d76fab-2f56-5b67-bbb4-3fac0ef14a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300864}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300864} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1830fc0-d9af-5d64-8c03-c71ba621745f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1369c3-c5a8-5903-b97a-51feef5a8e47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.675" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300102} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f23c1f-daa6-52df-92ad-335271c423fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e16b2152-6577-5fb2-90ae-6762bd67fd4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-327980" :question/problem "Express $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.675\\,\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ) & ={4}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ)) & =16+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =41 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{41}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.675)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a400b3-1805-5a4a-a1c5-a10cd56e3a96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300125} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a8c55d-0187-5967-b5c4-488aec645fd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2126e4b6-9b8c-5eca-8bd7-19f152c15bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.381" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300119} :answer-field/id #uuid "3056aedc-16f3-5242-9a76-69e73fc879d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "89941d18-05e5-5495-94f7-02c35c889216" :question/math-academy-id "q-327982" :question/problem "Express $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude–phase form\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=5 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.381\\,\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFor the amplitude,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ & ={2}^{2}+{5}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+25 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =29 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{29}sin\\,(x-0.381).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "387abbd5-7a35-5c1e-b84f-4cf4f6316359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.061" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300234} :answer-field/id #uuid "112ad757-0c3a-5732-ba3f-165b48a2c516" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d1823d-388d-5e43-a6da-a2e4fa7cce44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300962} :answer-field/id #uuid "d90e3f64-e1e7-583c-adde-24054cbfb4a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46d62efa-444f-5b8c-8b2d-8175168be70f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327988" :question/problem "Express $\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Use the **exact value** for $R,$ and round $ϕ$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,≈\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)≈1.061\\text{ }\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =21 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)≈\\sqrt{21}\\,sin\\,(6x-1.061).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1af3740-7b17-5d59-8ad8-3c35d3466137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301008} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9fe0c2-75f5-57d6-9e59-d062c5ae0131" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301259} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524b7a24-aeed-5c50-8643-3b6e27202b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300390} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31d8eec-c65d-53c4-94b2-ac40a6e7ce03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6c9f2368-0f34-5629-b2b5-f9b43de0b560" :question/math-academy-id "q-327990" :question/problem "Express $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=$\n {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+(3\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+27 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =36 \\\\ R & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+{60}^{∘})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdf60874-30f9-5a10-b9bc-b50d758a9ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301218} :answer-field/id #uuid "551610fd-7e76-5a08-89b7-b7fb24956c9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301504} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e5754f-4de0-595f-9371-cee20a75bfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300306} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b5d1ce-0eba-52d7-9041-1cc5d2b1d480" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dddb6c53-8f38-5969-8eb0-770120aca4ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-328001" :question/problem "Express $-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ in the form $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$\n {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+{45}^{∘})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07f539d-9f04-5db0-9d61-6daf5f5f4c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300927} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3c8930-a1a6-54f3-bf21-f87489323946" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3e045d2-6cd9-5743-8c88-8e195ab07316" :question/math-academy-id "q-328007" :question/problem "Given that the expression $-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ &=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ &=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\ ϕ & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d946acc-1690-5312-86d7-7cf32eab80f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300005} :answer-field/id #uuid "36376771-433e-5cb4-ad85-7a583159d896" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6911461d-1eec-524f-81d5-980cedf8faec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300490} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b0e770-aab7-549b-9a99-36b7bc6bc735" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17905033-ab88-5a2b-a0a3-a96238af287a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300059} :answer-field/id #uuid "78d05137-0c1a-53eb-8dc8-440d4dd570bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89353c9a-0abe-5319-848d-3b0db5e80514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300653} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6144399-9a57-51ec-8e3b-e87679b69d3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8798d95-6336-5f26-817e-c5e7e84cb46f" :question/math-academy-id "q-328350" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the **exact solutions** to the equation  $3tan\\,(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3}=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n+1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d539a035-bf73-5d20-b104-6281f17107b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301021} :answer-field/id #uuid "096d7802-fe90-5a34-a909-5b791279f217" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f28b3e7-692f-5d5b-91eb-54dc3a7fffef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300108} :answer-field/id #uuid "98f732b9-5f6e-53b9-98df-ddd352236abf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4e34b2f-1c92-5f18-a0cc-db49cea0c12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-328393" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arcsin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d50a4a9f62be8b8af4cc657a5b0ab1547288b844eaa05af1a57ed39b8afa0d6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c670d43b-ee41-53da-ae7e-1f8dfdbc2e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303674} :answer-field/id #uuid "1563fb9b-3781-5e30-ba46-153882c3c7fd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304125} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8198f5c3-3025-572f-90f9-58b3e8affc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303266} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde30a5e-263f-5fb2-ad95-78bcddb5245e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3e413c3-35de-5999-a08f-53da2546a391" :question/math-academy-id "q-328530" :question/problem "Solve the inequality\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3x+4}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}>\\frac{x+10}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that the denominator $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >\\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ 3x+4 & >x+10 \\\\ 2x & >6 \\\\ x & >\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aaabb3f-4e99-55f0-9dbd-01ef725aca6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302632} {:answer/id #uuid "419eff75-fbc8-5431-b2d9-4d103e0d3ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302867} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3c4f4e-eed7-56f3-ab74-a7d7be25f9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302943} {:answer/id #uuid "7c453689-e339-528d-ac08-d39e80bfe576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303227} {:answer/id #uuid "98749ab8-506f-5fd8-97b8-c25f97827e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303403} :answer-field/id #uuid "bec43d4e-da49-53d9-975f-06215337845e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ecd43ed3-12b1-5649-a96a-79cb243b0602" :question/math-academy-id "q-3295" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x)={cos}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $[0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this integral using a substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\left(\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}\\right)|}_{1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5\\pi }[(-1)^{5}-{1}^{5}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22533e15-f1ec-5569-97dc-eb0352a5c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi }{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302671} {:answer/id #uuid "397f19c9-e48c-5bb2-a801-d596f8d84d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302813} {:answer/id #uuid "5672cca0-f4d0-513e-a4be-1ee54068d16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{6\\pi }{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302986} {:answer/id #uuid "9e626c98-485e-59b9-98cd-a829c9ba6ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303443} {:answer/id #uuid "a950def5-f890-5120-9447-bda52c78aa48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302813} :answer-field/id #uuid "58654c2c-4d33-502a-9a53-6b8e29dd8288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5e66b71-3583-567d-9802-213ba5e35885" :question/math-academy-id "q-331032" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+3) & =cos\\,(6\\pi (t+3)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t+18\\pi ) \\\\  & =cos\\,(6\\pi t+9\\cdot 2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+3)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+(6\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+36{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01fdf650-4ae0-5cdf-b342-e4d4351d1e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302466} {:answer/id #uuid "93b08f05-48be-510a-b2cf-24c19e13eb43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303372} {:answer/id #uuid "d608221a-c5e9-5f12-9269-d7fe62811933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303783} {:answer/id #uuid "ea38e5fc-8cfb-5531-84f2-cca1fbf7392c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303919} {:answer/id #uuid "fb45f821-b615-509b-b093-07556b48da15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303919} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3f2742-e298-59b7-b9d6-791b6b3433ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f6510b2-7716-5473-979d-e49ac2d9ac7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-331039" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-1)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-1)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+1) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+5\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot (-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}\n$$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+1)} & =L{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-1)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-s}\\,L{g(t+1)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73615501-75a8-5a0d-ae42-5a5baa81c031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303153} {:answer/id #uuid "9933a85c-b338-5c59-bc0f-c6ccaa03146f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d4e5af-f5b3-5d8c-9eff-4d717e22b60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303497} {:answer/id #uuid "cf25f9c3-58af-52c3-ac83-c9fdad7c1e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+4{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303734} {:answer/id #uuid "e3165b4c-3ad9-5a08-9d70-2d92c07b7f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303406} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d09784f-1642-59bd-831a-f9fb74d842e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "477fdf0a-8165-5144-ad0b-72ada4a489e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331040" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-2)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-2s}\\,L\\{g(t+2)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+2) & =sin\\,(\\pi (t+2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t+2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}\n$$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+2)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\,L\\{g(t+2)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182cce37-e813-530f-98fb-0166e11c6e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302615} {:answer/id #uuid "5bec99c0-cf71-588b-8408-c82ee7075312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303017} {:answer/id #uuid "694800b8-f95c-5340-9666-c59d00da26e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303097} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5783a5-8163-5806-a9ad-7baa90d54d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303321} {:answer/id #uuid "9010f692-aa6d-55ba-9b54-ff7a95dd1655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303354} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd4e453-e8db-56ba-b356-f272dfe52e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4afa90f5-03be-5d03-a214-8d5eff7bdf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331042" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L\\{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)\\},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\,L\\{g(t+a)\\},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\}={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+4) & =sin\\,(5\\pi (t+4)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t+20\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡20\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡20\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡5\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡5\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}\n$$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t+4)\\} & =L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-4)\\,g(t)\\} & ={e}^{-4s}\\,L\\{g(t+4)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{{s}^{2}+25{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c23ab43-e4b5-586a-ab5e-a6d493a63001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302735} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb39b89-17ee-5540-9dec-33ebf0c304c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303531} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf6d5b8-b576-5e82-a6d9-63438e70d23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303614} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0d8e31-4f6e-51de-8612-40f03a165205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303695} {:answer/id #uuid "f32fe8d8-74b0-5eeb-8046-8742adaf9d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303982}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303614} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f18b585-1434-5a7c-ab8e-a073a6ced244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa678d9d-354b-5744-8911-8a7690b1902b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331043" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-3)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0$ and $L{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}$ exists for $s>{s}_{0},$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-as}\\,L{g(t+a)},\\,\\text{for}\\,s>{s}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $u(t)$ is the unit step function.\n\nIn our case, we wish to find\n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ and we have \n\n$$\nL{u(t-3)\\,g(t)}={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)},\n$$\n\nwhere $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(t+3) & =cos\\,(\\pi (t+3)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t+3)} & =L{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-3)\\,g(t)} & ={e}^{-3s}\\,L{g(t+3)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a81a47c7-179d-5483-a227-55f039ceab1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303492} :answer-field/id #uuid "b703b978-722c-5e0b-95fc-55b9de7046c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7557cacf-886f-55fb-9076-1837e9a0913c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332138" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\right) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =cos\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1704f202-ad10-5a8f-b37a-2571e0cb54a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302606} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f219ae-f2f8-5acf-af63-4e88382630fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "24bac5e7-d993-51f0-aa0a-781406c4bd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-332139" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =cos\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943ada75-e8c9-5d64-8cce-ce3d2d08c85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303377} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2517b9-ed76-5cd1-9530-d352f6239167" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64b6ca5b-7886-5c02-8389-aebf02e6026c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332147" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$1-2{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f8a4639-cf5a-5adf-a1ac-d48e128b9c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303253} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b51173-4ff4-5274-ab59-653c294a8939" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7667b041-07ed-5199-9efb-833c8da623ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-332148" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-2{cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}){sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})+{sin}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-2{cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}){sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})+{sin}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘}) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={67.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {67.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d11a4f8-ffee-5767-9368-4c4c6f3d7aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302940}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302940} :answer-field/id #uuid "51165ca4-38a6-5ad0-bb61-4d9760434384" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3454171-a1b3-502e-bdda-985c2af86469" :question/math-academy-id "q-332150" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right) & ={({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =cos\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\right))}^{2} & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d347f734-6ca7-5952-bea8-22d7f06f35a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303767} :answer-field/id #uuid "f93642d3-ae42-5979-8df4-3afa14978559" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0d3b4ee6-98d1-5207-a0a1-2482e40fe54d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332151" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{sin}^{4}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{sin}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}^{2} & ={0}^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db407194-1963-5243-8d79-cf5a3f72be48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303820} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbccc8ec-a5eb-5258-9187-bb43d0322dc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e371d2-f34b-5f6a-8fcd-44fa665e2761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303879} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf6a6ab-6c25-5904-834e-b935f1492c43" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "87336710-8c90-5846-a70d-1640898f0eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-332188" :question/problem "Express $sin\\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$sin\\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin\\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{7\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2eca6a-19f2-5539-9a12-4f4fdf797fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306610} {:answer/id #uuid "d249826b-78f5-5920-8dc9-794a95892408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfd761d9-e69c-58d9-a446-66495dde14ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306939} :answer-field/id #uuid "f36b9e2a-5d17-5bdf-9222-000ef69b718f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0db7040f-f565-57af-8a26-d71dd11bd9ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-333696" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\cdot 5{e}^{-2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =10{e}^{3t-2t} \\\\  & =10{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ef6be6-9e02-54ff-9278-cf2232ce8a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306243} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd02b8c2-dc5d-51e1-bb51-53cf85f9b1a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85f7fe3c-a4f1-5f77-bb76-b7c289145c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333704" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 & t \\\\ t & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-t\\cdot t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c834be47-bcfb-5968-9536-e48f9ede9136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307010} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e8cc76-4c7a-529e-aaa3-e30e37cfd220" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70bc6694-e09d-56df-9518-478836c1739c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333728" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t+1 & t-1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(t+1)\\cdot 2-(t-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2t+2-2t+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3419477a-37c8-59fa-bdb0-6d2be