nswer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306544} :answer-field/id #uuid "90e3e927-d7ad-50b7-8db9-e0dbd9d0eb06" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1422b740-90d0-5152-ba64-2a4dc29fee88" :question/math-academy-id "q-333741" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce8a8dc-0340-532b-a7b0-361ac0bcfaff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305263} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aaa09c3-bdff-5af3-8016-55bcd90c904d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ddbbb86-3f9f-52fc-9618-f8aa27cacf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305749} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dfde61c-0bfa-5840-901c-1d3691876fa3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307761} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640f10e2-1182-581d-a984-a5f0c41c62dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306119} :answer-field/id #uuid "b410cff7-978b-54e3-8a02-ff66461a4fe6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "839b2059-18bf-5b84-9bf7-bf583f1251e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333792" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{y}^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)y+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4{z}^{2}-4z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′}+4z & =4{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=4,\\,Q(x)=4,\\,n=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27674f0f-6f88-50cb-843e-201c3b2965fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305519} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae7a4cb-b19d-5e44-9ecd-d0fa9689b5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "zero for all $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305873} {:answer/id #uuid "e16d54f6-bd39-5cd2-86d6-96ab82ee38b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "zero for all $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305873} :answer-field/id #uuid "198d9076-9f4c-56a5-a30b-0db7b86cd0fd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d52fcde-c615-541e-a242-e015491425b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305269} {:answer/id #uuid "3840a98b-53aa-5e79-b3eb-ba2cfb333fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305690} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e1b086-4a0b-5a62-8d17-4b9870e36c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307020} {:answer/id #uuid "d620d9c6-7dbb-53ff-8d35-4e7d6d2b4862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307135} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db13195-849a-5eb6-843a-1cd119024ebb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307968} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a03ec8-027d-5316-a7e5-f61e2ae3dd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305636} {:answer/id #uuid "99ec886f-d8da-5a17-9621-25e903f7e2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "dependent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306587} {:answer/id #uuid "dde8b6ac-4adc-5db6-9970-f297fe2647db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306587} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab3e8f5-be75-5075-b098-001aa6e1834e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5afef72e-9d34-5cfb-8e31-ea9c3eb23bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-333843" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 6{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({e}^{t})\\cdot (6{e}^{t})-(-2{e}^{t})\\cdot (-3{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}-6{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c7c365-5985-566e-a09a-f489c5652939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305788} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec7cdcd-5ae2-53f0-8