b/ref 17592186306332} {:answer/id #uuid "9cece98b-0a0c-542f-a559-8f87cbae741a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $t\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305852} :answer-field/id #uuid "e740a05f-fc24-5814-928c-f03e37eda97b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27b787b2-4e51-56eb-90cf-8824cc40ba92" :question/math-academy-id "q-333869" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc51249-4527-5fa5-9176-ed3740861093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306625} :answer-field/id #uuid "b981d064-e25e-566d-a6dc-8cd30733920f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d1246374-7d17-597e-bd92-6ebe36c38c74" :question/math-academy-id "q-333882" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nThe integrand $(1+{t}^{2})^{2}$ is continuous for all $t.$ Therefore, applying the FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {(1+{u}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${G}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(0) & =-(1+{0}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(0)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(0))}^{2} \\\\  & =-1+1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7a71e59-394d-504a-ba76-18346fec1ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307150} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6b1d8b-0369-5651-aba8-ce6a90829230" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2626c21-ff9f-5aaf-a1fe-4eadd84b4f98" :question/math-academy-id "q-334352" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}sin\\,({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{1}{(-x)^{2}}sin\\,((-x)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a2192b-ff2b-5c73-85b6-5b24d278614b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305315} {:answer/id #uuid "52b2fa2c-ea70-5ecb-83cd-720af9baecef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a(t),b(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305952} {:answer/id #uuid "603a932c-9053-501f-af88-8060d493fcad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305952} :answer-field/id #uuid "16288257-f509-5332-bb0e-4df8511b5cec" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "618b7d53-710a-57fc-a8cc-442dd4871045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306093} {:answer/id #uuid "c2cd5e7f-57ca-5