id "4bf4728b-49d9-5839-ad97-96e47501a705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305878} {:answer/id #uuid "f68cc781-4167-536b-9f03-1eed8cc76e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307439} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf556b16-2545-521a-8e05-8465605098da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7e86ed-cc57-5d9e-b5f8-bfc650323d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305279} {:answer/id #uuid "5faf653f-8053-53e8-9b65-68e1e4727f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305279} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1e6ab21-66fc-5d8a-af5e-f2541ea04193" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "940032dd-db72-5ffe-8f84-7331f242458a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334404" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(4{e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+3\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(2{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}({e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot {e}^{2t}+1\\cdot {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{2t}+3\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4{e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4{e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}-0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,b}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fe2af08-c267-5c03-811f-924d9a5e6b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306075} {:answer/id #uuid "6dfb4b41-53c3-5906-be84-ffc00eb0c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306200} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fd92b0-733e-51b4-9ec3-2b2d9c1f052f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306869} {:answer/id #uuid "e3776d4e-ba3a-54e0-aeaf-45b834b90542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306869} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e070ae-8967-53e0-8ea9-0329b0fedf67" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307605} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7247a42d-168c-5cb4-87db-46005abd8db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "b,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306241} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbb783a-0453-5645-9f37-c7a5983cbe17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a,b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306938} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a04c3a-0c99-5652-8fa5-f47bef9616d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a,c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306938} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a7b9d1-9876-56dc-9479-4473faafb9ff" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47a259ee-b193-5098-b70f-9c9b31916760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305846} {:answer/id #uuid "c8393e72-d9bf-566d-bb19-994d1dd98055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307011} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d54dd41-b542-5263-809c-b4103c5aed20" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307815} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1568fa63-8ab0-50d0-82f7-ae8c66e3e52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305338} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0e82c2-8c84-50e2-8c91-a4b13504a4a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305879} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca1a5d8-7458-5a8c-a5ef-6c8bfddcf9c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270598d0-7879-5bda-9389-4f01b159130e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305515} {:answer/id #uuid "bf887993-160a-5db9-bf56-1e2bde7f2fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a(t),b(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306936} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f4eda8-70a8-5f95-882e-fa79e7002722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b(t),c(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306936} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d801fd-1b8f-53a3-abc4-7ea372766614" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd7632e-efa0-5c8a-877d-e6444bcfb01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305462} {:answer/id #uuid "407f2ca4-bb6b-5d7c-9957-fc7c7a8769bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305462} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1112e7-7fcb-5a8e-87eb-54cb9923bfc8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57f185dd-4868-5d08-a6b7-2a3380ec12f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-334527" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-1}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-2}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}$  \n{{field-3}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}}  \ndoes not give a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian  \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for all $t\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent. In this case, they do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(1-3t)^{′} \\\\ (3t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 3t \\\\ 3(1-3t)+3\\cdot 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(2(1-3t))^{′} \\\\ (6t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 6t \\\\ 3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+3\\cdot 6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}(t)^{′} \\\\ (t)^{′}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3t+(-3)t \\\\ 3t+3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6t \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1-3t & 2(1-3t) \\\\ 3t & 6t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1-3t)\\cdot 6t-2(1-3t)\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =6t(1-3t)-6t(1-3t) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronskian is identically zero, they are linearly dependent and do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions of the system."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e1419ab-1afa-580c-9877-c4408bc4f5d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306203} {:answer/id #uuid "9733bb8e-293e-5278-800b-2795308d1835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306574} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b0f9cf-c9db-56ef-b99e-c124e43af36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306820} {:answer/id #uuid "d87774eb-717c-5f3c-9026-269a0721e92b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307158} {:answer/id #uuid "f3241a1b-f338-52cd-8859-c5fc94a33eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306203} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f792c18-672e-5914-bb91-6bd334d29670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49aaf671-dedd-5860-803a-66d152eac8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-334542" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.310.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.078.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.310$ to $0.078.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.310}{0.078}=3.974….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈3.974… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(3.974…) \\\\ p & ≈1.990….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37a75f7e-a838-5973-bb64-b551cb9cfe04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305685} {:answer/id #uuid "5855365d-d250-5efe-8364-a9472842f491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306001} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf1b126-0264-5a4a-ae26-1b59f6110382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306186} {:answer/id #uuid "a9678aaa-1979-52a6-a282-12ab0bfb03d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306748} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5af58a-2eb2-5615-ac0f-0e95f605436d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307499} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f35104-4448-5352-8cd8-47c672044b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d97636b8-044d-5032-9858-9a92e3347d6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334544" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.740.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.090.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.740$ to $0.090.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.740}{0.090}=8.222….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈8.222… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(8.222…) \\\\ p & ≈1.917….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308641 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2799aa5a-ed9b-5b21-bfd3-2dcb7524fe57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305523} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecc6880-8400-5596-b032-131ea2624d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306369} {:answer/id #uuid "828aa0c9-e28f-5245-9745-37d3d10f96f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306399} {:answer/id #uuid "a81c60b8-ab9f-5ed7-adec-427dc50c769d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306736} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b47cb7-81c5-5c0d-a157-33459420c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306399} :answer-field/id #uuid "94522b9c-8ce8-51fb-835e-f8705f7e8843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f94b6c1-fa0e-5c48-970a-a0d605d1856d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334546" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308642 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2521fe3a-8b31-5a7b-8e5f-6a7b412c656c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305502} {:answer/id #uuid "6f59211f-8ff0-5fd6-b7b3-dc2b2c76acb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306212} {:answer/id #uuid "88fd4774-ba0c-58e9-870f-0f64e77bfb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306462} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4e4287-1e7b-56ef-9795-56a20e356fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860