ath-academy-id "q-72716" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(X)=-13.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(-13) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0122f81d-14c1-5cc7-89e6-42dfd3041d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339139} {:answer/id #uuid "8db70a89-c4a7-52aa-a82b-08bb9bb45f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340403} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed4dfcf-162b-587d-9c61-149bf91b4981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340562} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6a903d-35ab-5f9c-963f-2806a082d30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "637" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340930} {:answer/id #uuid "d56fca42-d779-56b4-81a2-77e0f483ea76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341018}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340562} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d41cc19-b64d-5bb9-b7ed-a300d5fc2b8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7b0644d5-53aa-5532-b39e-bdadeb30e9f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-72723" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $det(B)=91.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{6}↔{R}_{2}$ (switching the second and the sixth rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(91) \\\\  & =-91.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fbca7f-f430-54fe-98d9-b7f7f315925a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339357} {:answer/id #uuid "44b41ca8-7df3-5293-a744-43b0e1afa203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339736} {:answer/id #uuid "6eaa8f7b-0624-52d4-867d-8074d8a609c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340110} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c2ea0e-f522-5333-8ebc-34fec8c1185c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341191} {:answer/id #uuid "f8396929-697e-5359-9a61-dfbd7717bfa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341323} :answer-field/id #uuid "8870664d-2306-5269-bbb6-26917d2370fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2858ce5b-8e2a-5132-aac3-acedb511e7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72731" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=-64.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{5}{8}{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{5}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{5}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot (-64) \\\\  & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04461982-533a-5e14-a19b-02276aebc7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339156} {:answer/id #uuid "2e33a925-4daf-58c5-a21e-3d4f4217360d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339537} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecd2fc3-d4a8-52b9-9d61-7bc2eced0fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "176" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339692} {:answer/id #uuid "71d46223-059f-51b1-a88d-cb76b966f2cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340141} {:answer/id #uuid "e92a6c3e-a51a-5f4a-85da-5de36c8fce2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "275" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339156} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5f87ca-efec-5b28-8dc7-45a1cc64fa5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e092bdb9-fca3-5ab8-88ef-c95e1a9b5b5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72739" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(B)=11.$ What is the value of $det(2B)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot B)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2B) & ={2}^{5}\\cdot det(B) \\\\  & =32\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =352.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17922ecf-87f8-5af9-872a-3d5dc6da26af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339339} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3f2587-b4ad-5bf0-81b7-1df62ffb7a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339686} {:answer/id #uuid "a21fdf0b-c950-5ec9-b67b-31a39827c266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340600} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1d7c41-28d1-55a3-9882-2c2b96500495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340714} {:answer/id #uuid "f3da6cbc-6c90-5ed2-9aad-88c3625d3d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341277} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded707a6-03c1-52cc-8868-8933afddd3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bce6072f-0fd1-50b6-a305-a94b72b85f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72740" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(Y)=-3.$ What is the value of $det(4Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(4Y) & ={4}^{3}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =64\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-192.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324c36d9-d587-5ad4-b856-b4a184318900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339571} {:answer/id #uuid "38399355-cd75-56b5-9d19-7d3f47ff3b69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339634} {:answer/id #uuid "8be69226-28da-50ff-98ec-1d3d59e714b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340381} {:answer/id #uuid "961b4655-5108-5d90-a218-c3ac9780ce82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340483} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9c387a-7e8e-5edc-8f1b-4a0bf8e4e535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340381} :answer-field/id #uuid "d175a015-4083-5d2d-b145-bf4b4fc1ec76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "31c24ae4-5a26-5d23-82f7-e8e80b937d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72752" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-10.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{1}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba347bc-4f21-5259-92d2-aea2794dad81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339808} {:answer/id #uuid "4f4fbf8f-7e1b-5233-b67d-e55d70bd2317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339850} {:answer/id #uuid "65380dbb-92da-545c-926b-6fcfbf725b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340022} {:answer/id #uuid "6762b44d-f9e8-5558-ab36-c50ebd10b113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{p}^{-1}(x)+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340044} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6ce369-de08-59cc-8242-ceb4337d2b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340417}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340417} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfdcc32-3604-5312-b622-c1c23295c369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e62dc2c9-3155-5968-a118-3c566a7e8d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-73074" :question/problem "If $p(x)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find ${({p}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${p}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2y+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={p}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal. Notice that we will use of the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{cos}^{2}⁡(2y+1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y+1)\\cdot 2+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4y+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4y+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${p}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={p}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{p}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)} \\\\ {({p}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1910d3-ddb5-5b22-93b1-e1181225a80a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[4(2+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339211} {:answer/id #uuid "5840befc-777f-5aef-a2d2-41bd161a13ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339932} {:answer/id #uuid "ad11d805-2df8-587a-be27-6823052d0d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340705} {:answer/id #uuid "e27d1af6-daae-52e7-8728-f40348a1ac64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(3+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341133} {:answer/id #uuid "fa50765d-1b79-5369-9fa5-0b7058931d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341340} :answer-field/id #uuid "945fa420-349f-5491-85df-d089b1df2cad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d15a7348-cc49-5a9d-8853-862dbb5ad2b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73185" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{m}=a+mΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=ln⁡(4x-3),$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-1}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+m\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(4(1+\\frac{5m}{n})-3)\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090a45ae-4a14-53bd-bff9-96bc2c7a0d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339206} {:answer/id #uuid "4c52d9ff-6ce1-5908-b527-e5d1742ab70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339819} {:answer/id #uuid "5588d6f2-8c6f-5a01-b89a-7b7a17265739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339912} {:answer/id #uuid "d3238de0-22ee-5721-a21d-7e7f9b4dbeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340998} {:answer/id #uuid "e96c033c-24e5-59d6-9c28-291c33500939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341187} :answer-field/id #uuid "1da3650a-fb35-5fa8-b4ff-0230c249cc80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "98dd252e-cda8-57e3-b4c8-968d1e2d356e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73289" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+3}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25da2f97-1233-53a9-916d-a71898b3df50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339455} {:answer/id #uuid "72f61544-e2c8-53c1-8a4e-3af5ff8bdb60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340148} {:answer/id #uuid "adab89e2-8e27-5382-8960-921d0ee9c4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340709} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d86727-c7e5-55c4-bf76-09b8a18150e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340879} {:answer/id #uuid "ece5f479-65eb-52bf-9ade-027e7bebf59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339455} :answer-field/id #uuid "979df9b1-6818-520d-9541-8aa235a34437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9cc7964b-3169-5f22-8d75-6a7a05d33c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73324" :question/problem "![](images/33/333a99cd7b1f4aa0c3017c2b5aa42cb04acad842b19345a1176d57a71c5fd120.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-{sec}^{2}⁡x$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, as shown above. Find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(-{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =-(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e8e070-de91-5d4a-9b99-628f6756d676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339193} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccf4ffd-92b7-5a00-a7f6-90d277aa4692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339518} {:answer/id #uuid "3c18563b-3c67-5345-9b64-9fed57602fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339664} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2c8228-381e-5f58-a3c1-ada285b73163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340947} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ba006-51af-5e25-a7bf-390eee765235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339664} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec2dfa8-1f0e-5019-a5b0-9b7117cace3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8bd0eb34-b6c0-50be-b394-72a3c77e61f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-7338" :question/problem "Express $8{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-4$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n4(2{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-1)\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={15}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {15}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2{cos}^{2}⁡{15}^{∘}-1) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8c0277-f286-5f5c-95f1-742c5c84a4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468058} {:answer/id #uuid "be8dd074-7787-59f7-a72c-bcaee5b2f576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468058} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5514612-b769-5808-8bd5-f23974490b11" :answer-field/key "$[-1,2]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61a803aa-7689-5e3d-ac70-2fd7924783b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468060} {:answer/id #uuid "54a9b0c7-fc2a-5a6c-8b61-d9b8cb0d6b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468060} :answer-field/id #uuid "796ce61b-6691-5177-9e8d-07206e5a1e77" :answer-field/key "$(1,3]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc0fda9f-032f-5239-88db-2a5cd71a0cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468062} {:answer/id #uuid "5592e116-d543-53b7-bc8a-6200d16d248a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468063} :answer-field/id #uuid "0afadab0-2a76-5ed8-839b-7a13c2067a7a" :answer-field/key "$[2,\\pi ]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dcdd6cca-dc15-5e0c-a55e-4d73a59866ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-73504" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[2,\\pi ]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abf4c72-fdb0-5af9-b5fd-ae0fef31f40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339218} {:answer/id #uuid "3006d583-f16e-5d0e-b844-fb9f46126f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339547} {:answer/id #uuid "58ac14b0-18e4-5654-8906-1f8fbd918eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339935} {:answer/id #uuid "790e0574-3d28-597c-9244-19f4d13a9da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340206} {:answer/id #uuid "e92f8177-dba9-5a06-8301-1ec125054798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341183}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341183} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f35a480-d276-5dfc-a868-82f4e34bed3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "00a7e7d3-20f3-5e6c-9ee5-254f2e2cf50c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73513" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3({cot}^{2}⁡x+1)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3({cot}^{2}⁡x+1)}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+3\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1532740a-2f2d-58c7-a6d3-a482a5f5a6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339317} {:answer/id #uuid "15b2c4f7-f782-5f81-a1ee-e7ffd1fc0490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339319} {:answer/id #uuid "48e560ba-6af2-558f-95fb-42e2b35a6c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339778} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a79814-20f7-51a7-bd13-1b428992b75c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340745} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc7305e-fd58-53b9-84f7-f26c0414f57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339778} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbbfcf5-6b36-50e0-919f-6258f7b46391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2d677d9e-b0eb-5a9e-a8cf-bc476f79f4ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-73518" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have to use the identity  $1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ed3ec8-3905-5f6f-9a2f-2ab7735300df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339631} {:answer/id #uuid "39a3f0e3-caa9-547e-af78-d5299cb72d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339648} {:answer/id #uuid "74fac45e-7687-567e-92e4-c173b3b7bbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{192}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340175} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b65dd6-83c1-553c-b33b-ed750172fcaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340673} {:answer/id #uuid "ab841e54-a3a9-54bf-8504-9fbb5b804b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340175} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe1bbcec-fcbd-576d-8667-2c78a327af54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "229fa8ac-a324-584e-838d-1dd2b70fd89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73525" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x-{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡x+1={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x-{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{2}({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{192}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a9859b-5170-57d8-8411-14136c647e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339436} {:answer/id #uuid "49910893-af16-557c-8e27-a4aaced14c9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339790} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2a6bfa-4dd1-5593-a0d5-92d207814e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339966} {:answer/id #uuid "7d765f7e-cdef-5ed5-9844-08ba36a4876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340258} {:answer/id #uuid "97190335-f139-5a10-86e1-c4a055ec520d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339436} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb0dee8-fb4e-54ca-bd70-262fb54c178d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac782c3b-bb2e-59e3-b104-909eae65be8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73526" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444cd60d-98f8-57c7-9875-93fe9bccf0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339729} {:answer/id #uuid "534e79d6-1a48-52d9-8361-6b5e12a9cdf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339897} {:answer/id #uuid "53b60466-c23f-5e4c-9676-8a33e27c13b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339902} {:answer/id #uuid "87d00cf2-3923-56f4-a41d-5b87aa3fb375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340345} {:answer/id #uuid "da2aff30-bbeb-5d42-9525-3fe5517ebd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341064} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ff8668-6658-5885-8426-f8efdc154b15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a069e33-ab79-5e52-8347-ee8dc3251c92" :question/math-academy-id "q-73535" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & k \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=6+3k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=6+3k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2-(-1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6+3k}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2+k} \\\\ 2+k & =4 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "665c812a-56dd-5bc5-b0c8-ffde25e8fa1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340028} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5fface-39c3-5bf8-a1ce-22c8363e23e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340717} {:answer/id #uuid "b15c3399-c900-542c-998e-a75077eccf14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340742} {:answer/id #uuid "c27c70d1-a640-5ece-ae8a-27b55a8741af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340859} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fa4eea-3361-5d16-9459-3c3594fb683d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340028} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aa240fd-e96d-535e-8b4a-f32a7de1ce5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "33a7f588-04f8-5fc0-9705-3e73eb1453a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73542" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $1.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ k & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2-0)+(-2k-0)+0 \\\\  & =-2k-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1-(-3)) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k+1} & =1 \\\\ 2 & =k+1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05beee91-a392-5f65-88d2-f7fc95e409b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339177} {:answer/id #uuid "817291a6-cca7-53a6-bdbd-a95cc30beb48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340293} {:answer/id #uuid "98547d62-efa4-543b-b561-835c71d9986d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340498} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1fa043-012b-58dc-8eb3-9d118befac20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340804} {:answer/id #uuid "e51c2424-ac45-594a-b4be-5f0fea419df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341155} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c974da0-7d8a-5c4f-9610-badc09e2e785" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "93b0be09-4ca6-5085-9855-e90445a7accc" :question/math-academy-id "q-73545" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $\\frac{4}{5}.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-k-6)-(0-3) \\\\  & =-2k-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{11}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{11}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{11} & =(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2k-9}\\cdot (-k-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+6}{2k+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{11}^{′}=\\frac{4}{5}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+6}{2k+9} & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 5(k+6) & =4(2k+9) \\\\ 5k+30 & =8k+36 \\\\ 3k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38ccfea7-fed5-5b7e-bc09-bcd69091da1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339639} {:answer/id #uuid "60022a80-d927-5a41-9b20-e5576e6438cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339983} {:answer/id #uuid "89a04548-acfb-5947-b2ac-4e7dff064d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340356} {:answer/id #uuid "afb751a0-88c2-502a-8157-5752b308f317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340732} {:answer/id #uuid "b36db622-271e-564e-927d-d6a6763d261f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340759} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b2bee7-ee63-5f93-be18-3bd951bd1c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b53097b6-0795-598d-9c41-894871fbb1f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73546" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08301326-b847-5bf8-a7ac-ae63cd79e249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339196} {:answer/id #uuid "56930227-1428-50c5-a8cc-034a49832e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339922} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbc5f38-16b9-57ee-a58b-64b2d5ef36e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340083} {:answer/id #uuid "eec38fc4-d48a-5e6e-8931-f06a7a864ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341234} {:answer/id #uuid "eed401bf-7466-5857-a56b-834def403b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341234} :answer-field/id #uuid "53154123-fac5-56a4-af6a-e5b7c7ec264f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "67b1cc06-52db-594d-af64-fc96a02b017c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73653" :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{4{log}_{4}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{4}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{4{log}_{4}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{1}^{4}{log}_{4}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164d03a7-1370-51a6-8397-ae3a37ee3435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339325} {:answer/id #uuid "59a89007-afad-59e1-97c9-f01d389bf291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339943} {:answer/id #uuid "9266fee0-0299-5561-8066-c587641ef39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340441} {:answer/id #uuid "a3158c6c-e82d-594c-bf87-7aa6ded323cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340606} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b063ef-7d2e-5c4b-99bd-f6ee337f7b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340606} :answer-field/id #uuid "083ceb34-fb13-5d1c-8ff1-ed4f8fdff6b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46fc463a-0e37-5adb-8efe-657661cd9784" :question/math-academy-id "q-73655" :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{16}\\frac{2}{x{log}_{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{4}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\frac{2}{x{log}_{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{{log}_{4}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =4{ln}^{2}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eafeb14-c8c5-545b-a26c-84a8ffdb3bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339540} {:answer/id #uuid "31f0df20-fa82-5df5-ac98-2f11e0f712be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339567} {:answer/id #uuid "453ac55b-b5ef-5bd9-ab47-2d658db0c581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339742} {:answer/id #uuid "66afc99b-c4df-5906-b1f4-1abfb6ca13c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340031} {:answer/id #uuid "997973b5-0aa4-5755-be92-64594db4e075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340031} :answer-field/id #uuid "246f92ed-b4b3-5d28-a1bf-1d986ab28138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ddbff7e-d3cb-563f-8731-6916af8ccc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-73656" :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{8}^{64}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{8}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{8}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $8$ | $64$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{8}^{64}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{{log}_{8}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{8}^{64}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{log}_{8}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡8{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡8(2\\sqrt{u}){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡8(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{1}) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{2}-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba9b513-fd9e-5532-ac97-f39096b6d80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339229} {:answer/id #uuid "5146b66f-c081-5a5d-8a49-9465f6a29017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339874} {:answer/id #uuid "6f33def5-e388-5e0c-8e27-ef1debc47437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340114} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b7ff34-6e45-5d0f-881a-f864fd3aefad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341276} {:answer/id #uuid "fc039a6e-3ced-5554-821f-11c2bfcbf96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341355}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340114} :answer-field/id #uuid "71a8e5ea-7823-5b4d-bd7c-7ee99e3470da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f9fa12f0-8b86-59fd-acb9-b45aae5f88d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-73658" :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{21}\\frac{1}{x}{log}_{7}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={log}_{7}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $21$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{21}\\frac{1}{x}{log}_{7}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{21}{log}_{7}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7{\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡7\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a76728-b839-5d06-89f1-9934eae3f027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339291} {:answer/id #uuid "665fe7b3-58d5-51b3-a6e6-d098ccd5df2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340029} {:answer/id #uuid "689ac8e4-68fd-56b3-a68a-d04dadd83f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340060} {:answer/id #uuid "926a2dfc-a131-5563-ba53-5afa24278c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(36x)}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340442} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ae6232-f8ce-5ece-a30d-6e27d814267f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(36x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340060} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e02fde-8f73-598e-a892-0518e56b81c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8cd85d7e-9002-5474-aae0-35e5eb480c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73715" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}=3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $6x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be9359f-ea8b-563c-9bb9-6b1a6826176f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339961} {:answer/id #uuid "64bf2439-df5c-5f13-8e13-540b1d44c9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340020} {:answer/id #uuid "81d073cc-887d-5b6a-aeab-6cecc28a4382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{arcsec}(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340297} {:answer/id #uuid "95b7f420-0b28-5282-a657-40d893b628dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arctan}(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340478} {:answer/id #uuid "d5244b0b-6f4f-5504-8207-05f6294fbb10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{arctan}(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339961} :answer-field/id #uuid "d91ba1e9-1dd8-5d35-a35f-ac59fd9e8143" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78df4070-47af-5dba-93f4-a34eb10c085e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73716" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|4x|\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $4x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|4x|\\sqrt{(4x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(4x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14690a52-f522-5cec-90d3-9f420e7fc080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arctan}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339311} {:answer/id #uuid "5da47576-8911-5b68-868f-555462fcef8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339968} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec5d84f-e713-5832-b748-a0e4c715f798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arctan}(5x)}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340111} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9a50aa-ecbb-5989-afe3-1d8c3cdb9d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340239} {:answer/id #uuid "99ab830d-95ff-5734-ab2f-bab2f3570815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339968} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d389694-7fb3-5b9c-b985-67d93a5095c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b1a4da5-f80f-5816-895f-0dc3214bccc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-73718" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $5x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{5|x|\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(5x)}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3521af7c-6a0c-53b4-96bb-52426bff62c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339600} {:answer/id #uuid "397b6292-8d42-55ca-9a4c-7ed981885dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339645} {:answer/id #uuid "8210efe7-7013-5074-be6c-98c1836672bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x){tan}^{3}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340300} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1b9a1a-acba-5583-8e80-cec5d5e31cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340945} {:answer/id #uuid "f3639cb2-5ab5-5e80-87fb-52bccad8a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340945} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a0512af-d969-5007-b06c-2047dfae60f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42a9bb11-63b7-5a50-a482-a6e10afd8275" :question/math-academy-id "q-74157" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x-1) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x{tan}^{2}⁡x & =(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){tan}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x=(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x){tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca46b9d-67e6-5d4c-8bdb-2e592d381d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339241} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebd3934-82f6-54fa-bd5e-2e4c77677afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339396} {:answer/id #uuid "59dc50d6-8423-515b-a6d1-0dc64f293d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339945} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7d5460-4b43-5d8d-8ee9-afbb2043de61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339970} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb64fbd-43e5-5d6d-908a-efaa483f9570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340561}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340561} :answer-field/id #uuid "550abd65-52df-5079-8f18-862d0f7c0fa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "297ad6da-3f24-5ac9-9662-ff7d0cf1cfec" :question/math-academy-id "q-74162" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad5728c-c2b7-5836-8480-72986507ad51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339364} {:answer/id #uuid "979c7400-1d68-5d7a-89ce-d758efaeacc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340492} {:answer/id #uuid "9f692567-4d83-58f0-8d88-77e18873b9b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340569} {:answer/id #uuid "b5395ee1-8fd0-5434-a527-804dfa38a99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340773} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9584d2-5ff5-5bc1-809f-4a5332b7e7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340773} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac0677a-f8b8-5736-a298-04c5ed3d86eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7dbb6350-292b-5e35-a0c3-f36b7bfcba3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74166" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the denominator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}}{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4316bd87-b24a-5dbe-b89f-b035a9321a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{tan}^{4}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339715} {:answer/id #uuid "43d75293-5a69-5fa5-96b8-c3ca4a332fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339724} {:answer/id #uuid "567ba303-d94c-5f15-ad30-0cd3ba34e46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{4}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339920} {:answer/id #uuid "67d8a7d5-dec8-5b8b-a262-4c9b3518162e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{tan}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340052} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbb1537-925e-59d1-8fde-5fe12b3946bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{4}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339920} :answer-field/id #uuid "169d9f3d-7fac-5a76-8e2f-8d85801edd99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2bdf8f87-7ddd-53de-9456-6249fb8c6a74" :question/math-academy-id "q-74167" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)-1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡x({tan}^{2}⁡x)-1 \\\\  & ={tan}^{4}⁡x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{4}⁡x-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58575d51-ffdd-543a-bade-6634febbdd76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cot}^{2}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339934} {:answer/id #uuid "7a186c20-1506-5364-98fb-f5f5b90eafc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340219} {:answer/id #uuid "a09f4f00-858f-53bb-8ec8-28c387d75743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340585} {:answer/id #uuid "b49ad4dc-2081-5be8-ae64-74edb3e4c85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{2}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340766} {:answer/id #uuid "d84932da-0d02-599c-86d1-0c8975757cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340585} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2214d64-85df-5343-8f56-2f58cd99796e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6ad08b6-febc-5a5b-8528-78fb972c420b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74279" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{{sin}^{2}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{{sin}^{2}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡4x\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡4x\\operatorname{cot}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c30e3f-d29e-5013-9514-d17c3eee3460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339466} {:answer/id #uuid "376779f8-ab66-595c-9bd0-478fd63c0239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339625} {:answer/id #uuid "447b1d64-6d6b-508d-818e-c81c4fc983e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339733} {:answer/id #uuid "744da144-5af4-5254-8514-317a1d8d743d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340164} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6a59f6-df8c-5517-879f-5849fb7de659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339466} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede24e4d-4785-5cd1-8533-d9afadca7785" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "504ab9d3-58bf-5555-aee6-cd8fa47fea51" :question/math-academy-id "q-74281" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\left(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n-\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28e379d5-e3cc-5d9e-bc79-0c97e64b6708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339487} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcd2216-9604-5884-8b30-db487c1a8759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339855} {:answer/id #uuid "504b2a4d-3440-5d43-be35-369fee051002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339859} {:answer/id #uuid "590fabf1-c336-583b-9675-879635adb659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339939} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe19926-e662-55ca-954a-7f792e392851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339859} :answer-field/id #uuid "db770bc7-7f71-5cc8-b15e-9124fe857596" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53ef3692-4956-5314-a757-85cf1b947b82" :question/math-academy-id "q-74402" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08a360c5-8a7c-5a65-987e-00553a930888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339201} {:answer/id #uuid "176ae1a7-bd31-50d3-978f-0edb1a0d13b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339337} {:answer/id #uuid "51b40c43-b379-56f9-8bdc-7498e596148e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339878} {:answer/id #uuid "e1afd76e-bdca-515c-820c-04dee9086808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341128} {:answer/id #uuid "e91a5dfa-1e3c-5f8f-bda8-34cecace1388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+1+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339201} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc1bb94-4791-5d30-99cc-0d0ce156bb0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88de3e26-8c93-5f4c-a03c-1cf27cc2d8b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74403" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{cos}^{2}⁡x+1-3{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{cos}^{2}⁡x+1-3{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int [3({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)+1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c2115a-5ed2-5371-8918-f7e354a1d62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339202} {:answer/id #uuid "16de5a56-e617-57d5-8817-731dfc81e9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339329} {:answer/id #uuid "62d37b48-7912-5305-a61e-7fd235a5731c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340007} {:answer/id #uuid "79cab980-b193-5523-8d0e-588ccd890d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340215} {:answer/id #uuid "fb628851-44ac-5bf6-b332-d8f9333fd40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339202} :answer-field/id #uuid "61ace95e-0c2a-565d-969a-7bd880bb9e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a267544-1d98-5eb4-b631-7909c49f5d74" :question/math-academy-id "q-74404" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x={cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡4x-{sin}^{2}⁡4x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289aabe0-1e1e-5910-9d2e-b9b987a9b8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339482} {:answer/id #uuid "45db9866-c803-58a2-91e3-02246d1bedbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339749} {:answer/id #uuid "b966213c-f24a-514a-9e84-7ddd6fcfa373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340790} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ea7df-841b-5d89-8d8a-d892386f6acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341069} {:answer/id #uuid "df281c9b-3a5e-538e-ba07-d5569da3cb7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340790} :answer-field/id #uuid "affb554e-79af-50cd-b975-14f5c6d10fb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da6ed9c0-a564-5f50-bd69-a6ceb6be9c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-74407" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}⁡5x-10{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =5x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x={cos}^{2}⁡5x-{sin}^{2}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}⁡5x-10{cos}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10({cos}^{2}⁡5x-{sin}^{2}⁡5x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-10(\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36a0fbaf-03d1-5b54-a24e-b720779da6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339617} {:answer/id #uuid "50da1d59-4e84-53f5-b681-bf1e62142ea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339866} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e97ac2-1f10-5707-8232-c838bcbc37b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340603} {:answer/id #uuid "df193e3d-616a-561a-9d75-714a744de258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341111} {:answer/id #uuid "fa62133d-835c-59e1-afaa-6d24e24866a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341111} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd2bfaff-3fe0-535b-9654-0184987cd35c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c04929c-0219-5394-9df3-d0e9e41196b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74408" :question/problem "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5-8{cos}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5-8{cos}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & =\\int [5-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int [5-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-4(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fc79bf6-8f6c-57b2-9d1f-5fc658310f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339274} {:answer/id #uuid "63f4613a-08df-552a-871b-8cd72a27d6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340015} {:answer/id #uuid "77e84e23-8db6-51a7-b2e3-fc6cfce254cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340198} {:answer/id #uuid "af064d56-d9c1-59da-ba5d-0ba13a8f31bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340723} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdfe711-4ec6-51c1-b4a5-5c04e4a2d0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341091} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7fe7645-f001-599b-9bb7-3b491bf22c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45475e02-4d34-5bca-af8a-ebf1bb0d0f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-74410" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cf30032-05e3-5ffe-9842-91c7ccc289fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339378} {:answer/id #uuid "6f69f879-9fe1-59df-8f60-65fe6d0aabb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340115} {:answer/id #uuid "79893e92-e263-5a73-b079-5e88e837146e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340213} {:answer/id #uuid "91defe3a-19b7-5654-bb4f-0109f319a85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340437} :answer-field/id #uuid "50fb2e43-c9e3-5f1d-8654-b7d3bb52d8d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f1e79dd3-b04d-5c24-a841-80ff4b69c7df" :question/math-academy-id "q-74411" :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6{sin}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n2{sin}^{2}⁡3x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{sin}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\cdot 2{sin}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc56d0d-1fc1-55b0-a858-0f72c1814fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339244} {:answer/id #uuid "222afa10-e65e-535c-b778-59ff5f57e53e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339420} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab24c23-c3cd-5916-8d10-f8f9cd41df90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339654} {:answer/id #uuid "5240e2fd-ff1b-54d0-9ace-5be02e2c6a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339885} {:answer/id #uuid "839765cf-3921-5bbd-be4f-c72628e60a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340311} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e95e6b-57c0-5f5a-83f7-9b72881b2c92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ded7813f-7862-5c34-8d67-a2dd0cf18a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-74412" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right))\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ef63a0-c750-5bc2-8bd8-46d803ce1c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7+\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339194} {:answer/id #uuid "2149715e-055c-5838-a5fd-c4ec5e2a6fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339415} {:answer/id #uuid "672d1893-2807-5343-bed6-48541cf459e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340040} {:answer/id #uuid "b6017d08-881d-53ad-b2d3-3b6991a45f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340778} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f3a2af-4efe-52a0-be5d-8a4f036b53dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340990} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a982e9-7945-5d9b-ade9-b65001a1ecd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8517f4c3-6d9b-58e0-ad68-ee37cb097540" :question/math-academy-id "q-74458" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{12}{15},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{9}{15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{12}{15}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{9}{15}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{30}-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
